{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CgCyQK5yyK2y1QCyDJPPh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good — PostgreSQL confirmed, which supports `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` natively (needed for NFR-4). Now I have enough to write the ADR."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":376,"cache_read_input_tokens":37811,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":376},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d16cd034-ac55-45e5-b91d-ca59b3040df6","uuid":"f7edc915-b39d-42c4-af94-23f5fb9ba8cc","timestamp":"2026-10-20T05:56:05.176Z","request_id":"req_011CgCyQJrbFW4WpjCZtZDLF"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CgCyQK5yyK2y1QCyDJPPh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FNWV4Vy55umDRogqL8GRpZ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-04 Notifications & Email\n\nTicket: abcd-04 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Status: complete\n\nBase platform (from `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%):\nNext.js 14 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5 / PostgreSQL,\nMUI v9 + emotion. This PRD is brownfield on top of that stack — the\ndecisions below are only the deltas this PRD's requirements force, not a\nre-litigation of the base stack.\n\n## ADR-1: Email rendering — `@react-email/components` (new dependency)\n\n**Context.** FR-7/FR-8 require branded HTML+plain-text email templates\n(invite, password-reset, generic) sharing a base layout. The codebase has\nno email-rendering layer today (`sendInvite()` in\n`src/server/services/users.ts` is a logging-only stub per FR-9). NFR-1\nscopes MUI to *visible app UI*; it explicitly exempts email templates\nbecause MUI's `sx`/emotion runtime CSS does not survive email-client HTML\nsanitizers (no `<style>` tag support, inconsistent CSS-in-JS rendering in\nOutlook/Gmail clients).\n\n**Decision.** Add `@react-email/components` (+ `@react-email/render`) as\nthe email templating layer, rendered server-side to both an HTML string and\na plain-text string. This is a new dependency, but it is the one intake\nalready named in `detectionNotes` (\"adds Nodemailer + @react-email/components\nfor the email layer\"), and it's the standard choice for React-authored,\nemail-client-safe markup (table-based layout, inlined styles) — hand-rolling\nthat is a bigger lift than reusing the library intake already flagged.\nTemplates live under `src/server/email/templates/`, outside the app's React\ntree, so they never import MUI or app-side providers.\n\n**Consequences.** One new runtime dependency. Templates are a separate\ncomponent universe from the app (plain React components, no MUI, no\n`'use client'`) — contributors must know not to reach for app UI components\nhere. Base layout (logo/name header, footer with school address + manage-\npreferences link + disclosure sentence) is a single shared component per\nFR-7, so the three templates (FR-8) stay thin.\n\n_Cites: FR-7, FR-8, FR-9, NFR-1, NFR-6_\n\n## ADR-2: Email transport — Nodemailer with a console-transport dev fallback\n\n**Context.** FR-10/FR-13 require sending queued emails via SMTP, with env\nvars `SMTP_HOST/PORT/USER/PASS/SECURE`, `EMAIL_FROM`, and an\n`EMAIL_TRANSPORT` switch (`smtp|console`, default `console` in dev) so local\ndev never needs real SMTP creds.\n\n**Decision.** Add `nodemailer` as the SMTP client — it's the de facto\nstandard Node SMTP library, zero-config for any provider the school's ops\nteam points `SMTP_HOST` at, and needs no additional service/queue\ninfrastructure. Wrap it behind a one-function `sendMail()` in\n`src/server/email/transport.ts` that switches on `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`: `smtp`\nconstructs a Nodemailer transporter from the env vars, `console` just logs\nsubject/recipient/text (FR-13) and returns success. No new dependency\nbeyond Nodemailer itself — no mail-queue service, no third-party ESP SDK,\nbecause the queue is already modeled in Postgres (`EmailOutbox`, ADR-3).\n\n**Consequences.** Local/dev/CI runs need zero external services. Production\nrequires real SMTP credentials in env; if a future PRD needs provider-specific\nfeatures (templates-as-a-service, bounce webhooks), that's a new ADR, not a\nretrofit of this one.\n\n_Cites: FR-10, FR-13_\n\n## ADR-3: Email queue & delivery guarantees — Postgres row-locking, no message broker\n\n**Context.** FR-10/FR-11/FR-12 need an outbox with retries/backoff and a\ndispatcher invocable both as an HTTP endpoint (external cron) and a local\nloop. NFR-4 requires no email is ever sent twice under concurrent\ndispatchers, and that rows stuck `SENDING` for >10 min self-heal back to\n`PENDING`.\n\n**Decision.** Model the queue as the `EmailOutbox` Prisma table (FR-1) and\ndrive the dispatcher with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` (as a\n`prisma.$queryRaw` transaction) to claim a batch — Postgres supports this\nnatively, so no Redis/SQS/BullMQ is needed. This keeps delivery state in\nthe same transactional store as the rest of the app (one DB to operate,\nbackup, and reason about) and satisfies the \"never sent twice\" guarantee\nvia row-level locks instead of a distributed lock service. The 10-minute\nstuck-`SENDING` recovery (NFR-4) is a plain `UPDATE ... WHERE status =\n'SENDING' AND updatedAt < now() - interval '10 minutes'` run at the top of\neach dispatch call — no separate sweeper process.\n\n**Consequences.** Dispatch throughput is bounded by what a single Postgres\ntable can do under `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`; fine at this app's scale\n(schools, not millions of emails/day — NFR-2's 1,000-recipient/2s bar is the\nstated ceiling). If volume ever outgrows a single-table queue, promoting to\na real broker is a new ADR, not a patch to this one.\n`ponytail: in-memory last-run tracking for purgeOldNotifications() (FR-17)\nis a single-process assumption — acceptable per the PRD's own note that\nre-running after a restart is fine; move to a DB-tracked cursor only if a\nmulti-instance deploy makes double-purges a real cost.`\n\n_Cites: FR-1, FR-10, FR-11, FR-12, NFR-2, NFR-4_\n\n## ADR-4: Dispatcher trigger — HTTP endpoint + optional local loop, no cron infra\n\n**Context.** FR-12 asks for both `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`\n(bearer-protected, for an external scheduler) and `npm run worker:email`\n(a local loop calling `dispatchEmails` every 15s) \"for local/simple\ndeployments.\"\n\n**Decision.** Ship both as thin callers of the same `dispatchEmails()`\nfunction — the route handler for the HTTP path, and a small `tsx` script\n(`scripts/worker-email.ts`, matching the existing `tsx`-based scripts\npattern already used for `db:seed`) for the loop. No new process manager,\nno node-cron dependency: `setInterval`-in-a-script is sufficient for a\n15-second local loop, and the HTTP path is what production actually relies\non (external scheduler hits the bearer-protected route). Auth is the\nexisting bearer-token-against-`CRON_SECRET` pattern (NFR-8), no new auth\nprimitive.\n\n**Consequences.** Production deployments must wire an external scheduler\n(cron, platform's scheduled-task feature, etc.) to hit the dispatch route —\nthis PRD does not provide one. That's explicitly in scope per FR-12's\nwording (\"for an external scheduler\") and out of scope per NFR-10 (no new\ninfra beyond what's listed).\n\n_Cites: FR-12, NFR-8_\n\n## ADR-5: Notification type safety — literal union derived from the registry, no new validation library\n\n**Context.** FR-2/FR-3 require a typed registry (`NOTIFICATION_TYPES`) and\n`notify()` rejecting unknown type keys at compile time.\n\n**Decision.** Define `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` as a `const` object and derive the\nkey union via `keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES` (standard TS pattern,\nalready idiomatic in a `strict: true` codebase — no new dependency). Runtime\nvalidation of request-boundary inputs (e.g. the `type` field arriving in an\nAPI body) uses `zod`, already a dependency, matching the rest of the\ncodebase's API-input-validation convention.\n\n**Consequences.** None beyond normal TS discipline — adding a notification\ntype later means editing the one registry object, and every caller of\n`notify()` gets the new key for free (or a compile error if they typo it).\n\n_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, NFR-9_\n\n## ADR-6: No new dependency for polling/popover UI\n\n**Context.** FR-14/FR-15 need a 30s-interval unread-count poll (paused on\n`visibilitychange`) and a popover with a notification list.\n\n**Decision.** Use plain `useEffect` + `setInterval` + the\n`document.visibilitychange` event (native browser APIs) for polling, and\nMUI's own `Popover`/`List`/`Badge` components (NFR-1) for the UI — no\npolling library (no `swr`/`react-query` in the dependency list today, and\nadding one for a single 30s interval is disproportionate to the need).\n\n**Consequences.** If a later PRD needs broader client-side data-fetching\n/caching (multiple polled resources, dedup, retry), that's the point to\nintroduce `swr` or `@tanstack/react-query` as a real ADR — not pre-empted\nhere for one endpoint.\n\n_Cites: FR-14, FR-15, NFR-1, NFR-10_\n\n## Summary of new dependencies\n\n| Package | Why | Alternative considered & rejected |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nodemailer` | SMTP client for the dispatcher | Provider-specific ESP SDK — rejected, over-fits to one vendor; PRD only asks for generic SMTP env vars |\n| `@react-email/components`, `@react-email/render` | Email-client-safe HTML+text templates | Hand-written HTML strings — rejected, harder to keep DRY across 3+ templates and the shared base layout; MUI — rejected per NFR-1's explicit email exemption |\n\nNo other new dependencies. Everything else (queueing, retries, locking,\nscheduling, type-safety, polling) is covered by Postgres, Prisma, the\nTypeScript compiler, and native browser/Node APIs already in this stack.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":376,"cache_read_input_tokens":37811,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":376},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d16cd034-ac55-45e5-b91d-ca59b3040df6","uuid":"77d4b975-0abd-4853-8029-1b4faf6f0b4d","timestamp":"2026-10-20T05:56:46.734Z","request_id":"req_011CgCyQJrbFW4WpjCZtZDLF"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FNWV4Vy55umDRogqL8GRpZ","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d16cd034-ac55-45e5-b91d-ca59b3040df6","uuid":"0ff7f8e3-c980-4e0b-8ab1-3290a04b9759","timestamp":"2026-10-20T05:56:46.796Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-04 Notifications & Email\n\nTicket: abcd-04 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Status: complete\n\nBase platform (from `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%):\nNext.js 14 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5 / PostgreSQL,\nMUI v9 + emotion. This PRD is brownfield on top of that stack — the\ndecisions below are only the deltas this PRD's requirements force, not a\nre-litigation of the base stack.\n\n## ADR-1: Email rendering — `@react-email/components` (new dependency)\n\n**Context.** FR-7/FR-8 require branded HTML+plain-text email templates\n(invite, password-reset, generic) sharing a base layout. The codebase has\nno email-rendering layer today (`sendInvite()` in\n`src/server/services/users.ts` is a logging-only stub per FR-9). NFR-1\nscopes MUI to *visible app UI*; it explicitly exempts email templates\nbecause MUI's `sx`/emotion runtime CSS does not survive email-client HTML\nsanitizers (no `<style>` tag support, inconsistent CSS-in-JS rendering in\nOutlook/Gmail clients).\n\n**Decision.** Add `@react-email/components` (+ `@react-email/render`) as\nthe email templating layer, rendered server-side to both an HTML string and\na plain-text string. This is a new dependency, but it is the one intake\nalready named in `detectionNotes` (\"adds Nodemailer + @react-email/components\nfor the email layer\"), and it's the standard choice for React-authored,\nemail-client-safe markup (table-based layout, inlined styles) — hand-rolling\nthat is a bigger lift than reusing the library intake already flagged.\nTemplates live under `src/server/email/templates/`, outside the app's React\ntree, so they never import MUI or app-side providers.\n\n**Consequences.** One new runtime dependency. Templates are a separate\ncomponent universe from the app (plain React components, no MUI, no\n`'use client'`) — contributors must know not to reach for app UI components\nhere. Base layout (logo/name header, footer with school address + manage-\npreferences link + disclosure sentence) is a single shared component per\nFR-7, so the three templates (FR-8) stay thin.\n\n_Cites: FR-7, FR-8, FR-9, NFR-1, NFR-6_\n\n## ADR-2: Email transport — Nodemailer with a console-transport dev fallback\n\n**Context.** FR-10/FR-13 require sending queued emails via SMTP, with env\nvars `SMTP_HOST/PORT/USER/PASS/SECURE`, `EMAIL_FROM`, and an\n`EMAIL_TRANSPORT` switch (`smtp|console`, default `console` in dev) so local\ndev never needs real SMTP creds.\n\n**Decision.** Add `nodemailer` as the SMTP client — it's the de facto\nstandard Node SMTP library, zero-config for any provider the school's ops\nteam points `SMTP_HOST` at, and needs no additional service/queue\ninfrastructure. Wrap it behind a one-function `sendMail()` in\n`src/server/email/transport.ts` that switches on `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`: `smtp`\nconstructs a Nodemailer transporter from the env vars, `console` just logs\nsubject/recipient/text (FR-13) and returns success. No new dependency\nbeyond Nodemailer itself — no mail-queue service, no third-party ESP SDK,\nbecause the queue is already modeled in Postgres (`EmailOutbox`, ADR-3).\n\n**Consequences.** Local/dev/CI runs need zero external services. Production\nrequires real SMTP credentials in env; if a future PRD needs provider-specific\nfeatures (templates-as-a-service, bounce webhooks), that's a new ADR, not a\nretrofit of this one.\n\n_Cites: FR-10, FR-13_\n\n## ADR-3: Email queue & delivery guarantees — Postgres row-locking, no message broker\n\n**Context.** FR-10/FR-11/FR-12 need an outbox with retries/backoff and a\ndispatcher invocable both as an HTTP endpoint (external cron) and a local\nloop. NFR-4 requires no email is ever sent twice under concurrent\ndispatchers, and that rows stuck `SENDING` for >10 min self-heal back to\n`PENDING`.\n\n**Decision.** Model the queue as the `EmailOutbox` Prisma table (FR-1) and\ndrive the dispatcher with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` (as a\n`prisma.$queryRaw` transaction) to claim a batch — Postgres supports this\nnatively, so no Redis/SQS/BullMQ is needed. This keeps delivery state in\nthe same transactional store as the rest of the app (one DB to operate,\nbackup, and reason about) and satisfies the \"never sent twice\" guarantee\nvia row-level locks instead of a distributed lock service. The 10-minute\nstuck-`SENDING` recovery (NFR-4) is a plain `UPDATE ... WHERE status =\n'SENDING' AND updatedAt < now() - interval '10 minutes'` run at the top of\neach dispatch call — no separate sweeper process.\n\n**Consequences.** Dispatch throughput is bounded by what a single Postgres\ntable can do under `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`; fine at this app's scale\n(schools, not millions of emails/day — NFR-2's 1,000-recipient/2s bar is the\nstated ceiling). If volume ever outgrows a single-table queue, promoting to\na real broker is a new ADR, not a patch to this one.\n`ponytail: in-memory last-run tracking for purgeOldNotifications() (FR-17)\nis a single-process assumption — acceptable per the PRD's own note that\nre-running after a restart is fine; move to a DB-tracked cursor only if a\nmulti-instance deploy makes double-purges a real cost.`\n\n_Cites: FR-1, FR-10, FR-11, FR-12, NFR-2, NFR-4_\n\n## ADR-4: Dispatcher trigger — HTTP endpoint + optional local loop, no cron infra\n\n**Context.** FR-12 asks for both `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`\n(bearer-protected, for an external scheduler) and `npm run worker:email`\n(a local loop calling `dispatchEmails` every 15s) \"for local/simple\ndeployments.\"\n\n**Decision.** Ship both as thin callers of the same `dispatchEmails()`\nfunction — the route handler for the HTTP path, and a small `tsx` script\n(`scripts/worker-email.ts`, matching the existing `tsx`-based scripts\npattern already used for `db:seed`) for the loop. No new process manager,\nno node-cron dependency: `setInterval`-in-a-script is sufficient for a\n15-second local loop, and the HTTP path is what production actually relies\non (external scheduler hits the bearer-protected route). Auth is the\nexisting bearer-token-against-`CRON_SECRET` pattern (NFR-8), no new auth\nprimitive.\n\n**Consequences.** Production deployments must wire an external scheduler\n(cron, platform's scheduled-task feature, etc.) to hit the dispatch route —\nthis PRD does not provide one. That's explicitly in scope per FR-12's\nwording (\"for an external scheduler\") and out of scope per NFR-10 (no new\ninfra beyond what's listed).\n\n_Cites: FR-12, NFR-8_\n\n## ADR-5: Notification type safety — literal union derived from the registry, no new validation library\n\n**Context.** FR-2/FR-3 require a typed registry (`NOTIFICATION_TYPES`) and\n`notify()` rejecting unknown type keys at compile time.\n\n**Decision.** Define `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` as a `const` object and derive the\nkey union via `keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES` (standard TS pattern,\nalready idiomatic in a `strict: true` codebase — no new dependency). Runtime\nvalidation of request-boundary inputs (e.g. the `type` field arriving in an\nAPI body) uses `zod`, already a dependency, matching the rest of the\ncodebase's API-input-validation convention.\n\n**Consequences.** None beyond normal TS discipline — adding a notification\ntype later means editing the one registry object, and every caller of\n`notify()` gets the new key for free (or a compile error if they typo it).\n\n_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, NFR-9_\n\n## ADR-6: No new dependency for polling/popover UI\n\n**Context.** FR-14/FR-15 need a 30s-interval unread-count poll (paused on\n`visibilitychange`) and a popover with a notification list.\n\n**Decision.** Use plain `useEffect` + `setInterval` + the\n`document.visibilitychange` event (native browser APIs) for polling, and\nMUI's own `Popover`/`List`/`Badge` components (NFR-1) for the UI — no\npolling library (no `swr`/`react-query` in the dependency list today, and\nadding one for a single 30s interval is disproportionate to the need).\n\n**Consequences.** If a later PRD needs broader client-side data-fetching\n/caching (multiple polled resources, dedup, retry), that's the point to\nintroduce `swr` or `@tanstack/react-query` as a real ADR — not pre-empted\nhere for one endpoint.\n\n_Cites: FR-14, FR-15, NFR-1, NFR-10_\n\n## Summary of new dependencies\n\n| Package | Why | Alternative considered & rejected |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nodemailer` | SMTP client for the dispatcher | Provider-specific ESP SDK — rejected, over-fits to one vendor; PRD only asks for generic SMTP env vars |\n| `@react-email/components`, `@react-email/render` | Email-client-safe HTML+text templates | Hand-written HTML strings — rejected, harder to keep DRY across 3+ templates and the shared base layout; MUI — rejected per NFR-1's explicit email exemption |\n\nNo other new dependencies. Everything else (queueing, retries, locking,\nscheduling, type-safety, polling) is covered by Postgres, Prisma, the\nTypeScript compiler, and native browser/Node APIs already in this stack.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":141,"newStart":1,"newLines":166,"lines":["-# Tech Stack Decisions — ADR Log","+# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-04 Notifications & Email"," ","-Ticket: abcd-04 | Phase: architect (`arch-research-stack`) | Status: complete","+Ticket: abcd-04 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Status: complete"," ","-Brownfield PRD-04 (Notifications & Email) on top of PRD-00..03. Per","-`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` (confidence 100%) the base stack is fixed:","-Next.js 14 (App Router), React 18, TypeScript strict, MUI v9 (+emotion) and","-MUI X, Auth.js v5, Prisma/PostgreSQL, Zod, React Hook Form, Vitest/Testing","-Library/Playwright. `handoff-to-architect.json` reports 97% confidence and no","-blockers. This PRD's only real stack question is the email layer (FR-7..13);","-everything else slots into the existing app. NFR-10 explicitly caps scope","-(no SMS/push/websockets/digests), which rules out several heavier options","-below.","+Base platform (from `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%):","+Next.js 14 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5 / PostgreSQL,","+MUI v9 + emotion. This PRD is brownfield on top of that stack — the","+decisions below are only the deltas this PRD's requirements force, not a","+re-litigation of the base stack."," ","-## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack, no new framework","+## ADR-1: Email rendering — `@react-email/components` (new dependency)"," ","-- **Context:** FR-1..FR-24 are additive feature work inside an existing","-  brownfield app; NFR-10 forbids scope expansion (no SMS/push, no realtime","-  websockets, no digest emails).","-- **Decision:** Build entirely on the detected stack (Next.js API routes,","-  Prisma, MUI, Zod, RHF, Auth.js). No new runtime, ORM, or UI framework.","-- **Consequences:** Zero migration cost; new code must match existing `src/`","-  conventions (response envelopes, `requireUser`/`requireRole` from PRD-00/01).","+**Context.** FR-7/FR-8 require branded HTML+plain-text email templates","+(invite, password-reset, generic) sharing a base layout. The codebase has","+no email-rendering layer today (`sendInvite()` in","+`src/server/services/users.ts` is a logging-only stub per FR-9). NFR-1","+scopes MUI to *visible app UI*; it explicitly exempts email templates","+because MUI's `sx`/emotion runtime CSS does not survive email-client HTML","+sanitizers (no `<style>` tag support, inconsistent CSS-in-JS rendering in","+Outlook/Gmail clients)."," ","-## ADR-2: Nodemailer as the one SMTP client, promoted to a direct dependency","+**Decision.** Add `@react-email/components` (+ `@react-email/render`) as","+the email templating layer, rendered server-side to both an HTML string and","+a plain-text string. This is a new dependency, but it is the one intake","+already named in `detectionNotes` (\"adds Nodemailer + @react-email/components","+for the email layer\"), and it's the standard choice for React-authored,","+email-client-safe markup (table-based layout, inlined styles) — hand-rolling","+that is a bigger lift than reusing the library intake already flagged.","+Templates live under `src/server/email/templates/`, outside the app's React","+tree, so they never import MUI or app-side providers."," ","-- **Context:** FR-10/FR-13 need an SMTP sender with env-driven config","-  (`SMTP_HOST`/`PORT`/`USER`/`PASS`/`SECURE`, `EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp|console`).","-  `nodemailer` is already present in `package-lock.json` only as a transitive","-  dependency of `next-auth`'s email provider plumbing — it is not a direct","-  dependency and must not be assumed available at a pinned version.","-- **Decision:** Add `nodemailer` (+ `@types/nodemailer`) as a direct","-  dependency. One transport module (`src/server/email/transport.ts`) builds","-  either a real SMTP transport or a `console` transport (logs subject,","-  recipient, text body) based on `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`, defaulting to `console`","-  in development per FR-13.","-- **Consequences:** No ambiguity about which nodemailer version ships; no","-  second mail library introduced for the console/dev path.","+**Consequences.** One new runtime dependency. Templates are a separate","+component universe from the app (plain React components, no MUI, no","+`'use client'`) — contributors must know not to reach for app UI components","+here. Base layout (logo/name header, footer with school address + manage-","+preferences link + disclosure sentence) is a single shared component per","+FR-7, so the three templates (FR-8) stay thin."," ","-## ADR-3: @react-email/components + @react-email/render for templates","+_Cites: FR-7, FR-8, FR-9, NFR-1, NFR-6_"," ","-- **Context:** FR-7/FR-8 require branded templates (shared base layout,","-  invite/password-reset/generic) rendered to **both** HTML and plain text,","-  built with `@react-email/components` per NFR-1 (email templates are the","-  one UI surface explicitly exempted from the MUI-only rule, since MUI isn't","-  email-client-safe markup).","-- **Decision:** Add `@react-email/components` and `@react-email/render` as","-  direct dependencies. Each template is a React component under","-  `src/server/email/templates/`, sharing one `BaseLayout` component (logo,","-  content slot, footer with address + disclosure + preferences link). Render","-  call sites use `render(<Template .../>)` for HTML and","-  `render(<Template .../>, { plainText: true })` for the text part — no","-  separate hand-written text templates, so the two parts can't drift.","-- **Consequences:** One rendering path backs both EmailOutbox columns","-  (`html`, `text`); template changes automatically stay HTML/text consistent.","+## ADR-2: Email transport — Nodemailer with a console-transport dev fallback"," ","-## ADR-4: EmailOutbox dispatch via raw `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, no queue library","+**Context.** FR-10/FR-13 require sending queued emails via SMTP, with env","+vars `SMTP_HOST/PORT/USER/PASS/SECURE`, `EMAIL_FROM`, and an","+`EMAIL_TRANSPORT` switch (`smtp|console`, default `console` in dev) so local","+dev never needs real SMTP creds."," ","-- **Context:** FR-10/NFR-4 require concurrency-safe batch claiming (no email","-  sent twice by two dispatchers) without a message broker — Prisma's query","-  builder has no `SKIP LOCKED` support, and NFR-10 rules out adding","-  infrastructure (Redis/BullMQ/SQS) for a feature capped at polling-scale","-  volume.","-- **Decision:** `dispatchEmails()` uses one `prisma.$transaction` containing","-  a `$queryRaw` `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` to claim a batch of","-  PENDING rows (flip to SENDING), then sends each via Nodemailer outside the","-  lock, then updates status (SENT, or retry backoff per FR-11, or FAILED","-  after attempt 5). A second periodic query recovers rows stuck in SENDING","-  for >10 minutes back to PENDING (NFR-4).","-- **Consequences:** No new infra; the only raw SQL in the codebase is this","-  one claim query, isolated behind `dispatchEmails()` — every other read/","-  write stays on the Prisma client API.","+**Decision.** Add `nodemailer` as the SMTP client — it's the de facto","+standard Node SMTP library, zero-config for any provider the school's ops","+team points `SMTP_HOST` at, and needs no additional service/queue","+infrastructure. Wrap it behind a one-function `sendMail()` in","+`src/server/email/transport.ts` that switches on `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`: `smtp`","+constructs a Nodemailer transporter from the env vars, `console` just logs","+subject/recipient/text (FR-13) and returns success. No new dependency","+beyond Nodemailer itself — no mail-queue service, no third-party ESP SDK,","+because the queue is already modeled in Postgres (`EmailOutbox`, ADR-3)."," ","-## ADR-5: Two thin drivers over one dispatch function — HTTP route + loop script, no scheduler","+**Consequences.** Local/dev/CI runs need zero external services. Production","+requires real SMTP credentials in env; if a future PRD needs provider-specific","+features (templates-as-a-service, bounce webhooks), that's a new ADR, not a","+retrofit of this one."," ","-- **Context:** FR-12 needs both an externally-cron-triggered HTTP endpoint","-  (`POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`, bearer-guarded by `CRON_SECRET`,","-  NFR-8) and a `npm run worker:email` script that loops every 15s for local/","-  simple deployments.","-- **Decision:** Both are thin callers of the same `dispatchEmails()` (ADR-4).","-  The loop script is a plain `setInterval`-driven Node script","-  (`scripts/worker-email.ts` run via `tsx`, matching the existing","-  `db:seed` pattern) — no cron library, no job-scheduler dependency.","-- **Consequences:** One dispatch implementation, two callers; adding a real","-  scheduler later (if deployment target changes) only touches the driver,","-  not `dispatchEmails()`.","+_Cites: FR-10, FR-13_"," ","-## ADR-6: Bell + preferences UI stays polling, built from existing MUI/MUI X — no new UI deps","+## ADR-3: Email queue & delivery guarantees — Postgres row-locking, no message broker"," ","-- **Context:** FR-14 (unread-count bell poll every 30s, paused on","-  `visibilitychange`), FR-15 (Popover + List), FR-16 (/notifications list +","-  filters), FR-18 (preferences table with per-row Switches) are all additive","-  screens; NFR-1 mandates MUI-only, and NFR-10 explicitly rules out","-  websockets.","-- **Decision:** Plain `fetch` + `setInterval`/`document.visibilityState` for","-  polling (no SWR/React Query/socket library added). All visible elements","-  come from already-installed `@mui/material`/`@mui/icons-material`","-  (Badge, IconButton, Popover, List, Table, Switch, Tabs) — same ESLint","-  `no-restricted-imports` MUI-only gate as prior PRDs enforces this.","-- **Consequences:** Zero new frontend dependencies; one shared polling hook","-  (e.g. `useUnreadCount`) can back both the bell and, if ever needed, the","-  list page — no premature abstraction beyond that single reuse point.","+**Context.** FR-10/FR-11/FR-12 need an outbox with retries/backoff and a","+dispatcher invocable both as an HTTP endpoint (external cron) and a local","+loop. NFR-4 requires no email is ever sent twice under concurrent","+dispatchers, and that rows stuck `SENDING` for >10 min self-heal back to","+`PENDING`."," ","-## ADR-7: Notification type registry is a plain TS const + literal union, not a DB table","+**Decision.** Model the queue as the `EmailOutbox` Prisma table (FR-1) and","+drive the dispatcher with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` (as a","+`prisma.$queryRaw` transaction) to claim a batch — Postgres supports this","+natively, so no Redis/SQS/BullMQ is needed. This keeps delivery state in","+the same transactional store as the rest of the app (one DB to operate,","+backup, and reason about) and satisfies the \"never sent twice\" guarantee","+via row-level locks instead of a distributed lock service. The 10-minute","+stuck-`SENDING` recovery (NFR-4) is a plain `UPDATE ... WHERE status =","+'SENDING' AND updatedAt < now() - interval '10 minutes'` run at the top of","+each dispatch call — no separate sweeper process."," ","-- **Context:** FR-2/FR-3 need a typed registry (key → label/description/","-  category/defaults/roles) where `notify()` rejects unknown type keys **at","-  compile time**.","-- **Decision:** `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` is a `const` object in","-  `src/server/notifications/types.ts`; its key union","-  (`keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES`) is the type parameter `notify()`","-  accepts. No database-backed type table, no runtime registry lookup for","-  validity — compile-time exhaustiveness is free and stronger than a runtime","-  check.","-- **Consequences:** Adding a notification type is a code change + PR review","-  (desired: it drives UI copy and default routing), not a migration or admin","-  screen.","+**Consequences.** Dispatch throughput is bounded by what a single Postgres","+table can do under `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`; fine at this app's scale","+(schools, not millions of emails/day — NFR-2's 1,000-recipient/2s bar is the","+stated ceiling). If volume ever outgrows a single-table queue, promoting to","+a real broker is a new ADR, not a patch to this one.","+`ponytail: in-memory last-run tracking for purgeOldNotifications() (FR-17)","+is a single-process assumption — acceptable per the PRD's own note that","+re-running after a restart is fine; move to a DB-tracked cursor only if a","+multi-instance deploy makes double-purges a real cost.`"," ","-## ADR-8: purgeOldNotifications scheduling stays in-memory, no job table","+_Cites: FR-1, FR-10, FR-11, FR-12, NFR-2, NFR-4_"," ","-- **Context:** FR-17 needs a daily purge of notifications >180 days old;","-  explicitly \"acceptable to re-run after a restart.\"","-- **Decision:** Track last-run time in a module-level variable inside the","-  dispatch route handler (checked on each `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`","-  call, which already fires every 15s–few minutes via the cron/worker); no","-  `CronJob`/`LastRun` table, no separate scheduler.","-- **Consequences:** Matches the stated tolerance for restart-driven re-runs;","-  avoids a persistence concept this PRD doesn't otherwise need.","+## ADR-4: Dispatcher trigger — HTTP endpoint + optional local loop, no cron infra"," ","-## ADR-9: MUI-only UI surface, enforced by the existing ESLint rule (carried over)","+**Context.** FR-12 asks for both `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`","+(bearer-protected, for an external scheduler) and `npm run worker:email`","+(a local loop calling `dispatchEmails` every 15s) \"for local/simple","+deployments.\""," ","-- **Context:** NFR-1 requires every visible element to come from","-  `@mui/material`/`@mui/icons-material`/MUI X, with banned libraries blocked","-  by `no-restricted-imports`; email templates are the sole, explicit","-  exception (ADR-3).","-- **Decision:** Reuse the PRD-00 ESLint config as-is for all app UI; the","-  exception glob only needs to cover `src/server/email/templates/**` so","-  `@react-email/components` imports aren't flagged there.","-- **Consequences:** Consistent look/feel with PRD-00..03; the one new","-  dependency surface (react-email) is scoped and fenced by lint, not by","-  convention alone.","+**Decision.** Ship both as thin callers of the same `dispatchEmails()`","+function — the route handler for the HTTP path, and a small `tsx` script","+(`scripts/worker-email.ts`, matching the existing `tsx`-based scripts","+pattern already used for `db:seed`) for the loop. No new process manager,","+no node-cron dependency: `setInterval`-in-a-script is sufficient for a","+15-second local loop, and the HTTP path is what production actually relies","+on (external scheduler hits the bearer-protected route). Auth is the","+existing bearer-token-against-`CRON_SECRET` pattern (NFR-8), no new auth","+primitive.","+","+**Consequences.** Production deployments must wire an external scheduler","+(cron, platform's scheduled-task feature, etc.) to hit the dispatch route —","+this PRD does not provide one. That's explicitly in scope per FR-12's","+wording (\"for an external scheduler\") and out of scope per NFR-10 (no new","+infra beyond what's listed).","+","+_Cites: FR-12, NFR-8_","+","+## ADR-5: Notification type safety — literal union derived from the registry, no new validation library","+","+**Context.** FR-2/FR-3 require a typed registry (`NOTIFICATION_TYPES`) and","+`notify()` rejecting unknown type keys at compile time.","+","+**Decision.** Define `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` as a `const` object and derive the","+key union via `keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES` (standard TS pattern,","+already idiomatic in a `strict: true` codebase — no new dependency). Runtime","+validation of request-boundary inputs (e.g. the `type` field arriving in an","+API body) uses `zod`, already a dependency, matching the rest of the","+codebase's API-input-validation convention.","+","+**Consequences.** None beyond normal TS discipline — adding a notification","+type later means editing the one registry object, and every caller of","+`notify()` gets the new key for free (or a compile error if they typo it).","+","+_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, NFR-9_","+","+## ADR-6: No new dependency for polling/popover UI","+","+**Context.** FR-14/FR-15 need a 30s-interval unread-count poll (paused on","+`visibilitychange`) and a popover with a notification list.","+","+**Decision.** Use plain `useEffect` + `setInterval` + the","+`document.visibilitychange` event (native browser APIs) for polling, and","+MUI's own `Popover`/`List`/`Badge` components (NFR-1) for the UI — no","+polling library (no `swr`/`react-query` in the dependency list today, and","+adding one for a single 30s interval is disproportionate to the need).","+","+**Consequences.** If a later PRD needs broader client-side data-fetching","+/caching (multiple polled resources, dedup, retry), that's the point to","+introduce `swr` or `@tanstack/react-query` as a real ADR — not pre-empted","+here for one endpoint.","+","+_Cites: FR-14, FR-15, NFR-1, NFR-10_","+","+## Summary of new dependencies","+","+| Package | Why | Alternative considered & rejected |","+|---|---|---|","+| `nodemailer` | SMTP client for the dispatcher | Provider-specific ESP SDK — rejected, over-fits to one vendor; PRD only asks for generic SMTP env vars |","+| `@react-email/components`, `@react-email/render` | Email-client-safe HTML+text templates | Hand-written HTML strings — rejected, harder to keep DRY across 3+ templates and the shared base layout; MUI — rejected per NFR-1's explicit email exemption |","+","+No other new dependencies. Everything else (queueing, retries, locking,","+scheduling, type-safety, polling) is covered by Postgres, Prisma, the","+TypeScript compiler, and native browser/Node APIs already in this stack."]}],"originalFile":"# Tech Stack Decisions — ADR Log\n\nTicket: abcd-04 | Phase: architect (`arch-research-stack`) | Status: complete\n\nBrownfield PRD-04 (Notifications & Email) on top of PRD-00..03. Per\n`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` (confidence 100%) the base stack is fixed:\nNext.js 14 (App Router), React 18, TypeScript strict, MUI v9 (+emotion) and\nMUI X, Auth.js v5, Prisma/PostgreSQL, Zod, React Hook Form, Vitest/Testing\nLibrary/Playwright. `handoff-to-architect.json` reports 97% confidence and no\nblockers. This PRD's only real stack question is the email layer (FR-7..13);\neverything else slots into the existing app. NFR-10 explicitly caps scope\n(no SMS/push/websockets/digests), which rules out several heavier options\nbelow.\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack, no new framework\n\n- **Context:** FR-1..FR-24 are additive feature work inside an existing\n  brownfield app; NFR-10 forbids scope expansion (no SMS/push, no realtime\n  websockets, no digest emails).\n- **Decision:** Build entirely on the detected stack (Next.js API routes,\n  Prisma, MUI, Zod, RHF, Auth.js). No new runtime, ORM, or UI framework.\n- **Consequences:** Zero migration cost; new code must match existing `src/`\n  conventions (response envelopes, `requireUser`/`requireRole` from PRD-00/01).\n\n## ADR-2: Nodemailer as the one SMTP client, promoted to a direct dependency\n\n- **Context:** FR-10/FR-13 need an SMTP sender with env-driven config\n  (`SMTP_HOST`/`PORT`/`USER`/`PASS`/`SECURE`, `EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp|console`).\n  `nodemailer` is already present in `package-lock.json` only as a transitive\n  dependency of `next-auth`'s email provider plumbing — it is not a direct\n  dependency and must not be assumed available at a pinned version.\n- **Decision:** Add `nodemailer` (+ `@types/nodemailer`) as a direct\n  dependency. One transport module (`src/server/email/transport.ts`) builds\n  either a real SMTP transport or a `console` transport (logs subject,\n  recipient, text body) based on `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`, defaulting to `console`\n  in development per FR-13.\n- **Consequences:** No ambiguity about which nodemailer version ships; no\n  second mail library introduced for the console/dev path.\n\n## ADR-3: @react-email/components + @react-email/render for templates\n\n- **Context:** FR-7/FR-8 require branded templates (shared base layout,\n  invite/password-reset/generic) rendered to **both** HTML and plain text,\n  built with `@react-email/components` per NFR-1 (email templates are the\n  one UI surface explicitly exempted from the MUI-only rule, since MUI isn't\n  email-client-safe markup).\n- **Decision:** Add `@react-email/components` and `@react-email/render` as\n  direct dependencies. Each template is a React component under\n  `src/server/email/templates/`, sharing one `BaseLayout` component (logo,\n  content slot, footer with address + disclosure + preferences link). Render\n  call sites use `render(<Template .../>)` for HTML and\n  `render(<Template .../>, { plainText: true })` for the text part — no\n  separate hand-written text templates, so the two parts can't drift.\n- **Consequences:** One rendering path backs both EmailOutbox columns\n  (`html`, `text`); template changes automatically stay HTML/text consistent.\n\n## ADR-4: EmailOutbox dispatch via raw `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, no queue library\n\n- **Context:** FR-10/NFR-4 require concurrency-safe batch claiming (no email\n  sent twice by two dispatchers) without a message broker — Prisma's query\n  builder has no `SKIP LOCKED` support, and NFR-10 rules out adding\n  infrastructure (Redis/BullMQ/SQS) for a feature capped at polling-scale\n  volume.\n- **Decision:** `dispatchEmails()` uses one `prisma.$transaction` containing\n  a `$queryRaw` `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` to claim a batch of\n  PENDING rows (flip to SENDING), then sends each via Nodemailer outside the\n  lock, then updates status (SENT, or retry backoff per FR-11, or FAILED\n  after attempt 5). A second periodic query recovers rows stuck in SENDING\n  for >10 minutes back to PENDING (NFR-4).\n- **Consequences:** No new infra; the only raw SQL in the codebase is this\n  one claim query, isolated behind `dispatchEmails()` — every other read/\n  write stays on the Prisma client API.\n\n## ADR-5: Two thin drivers over one dispatch function — HTTP route + loop script, no scheduler\n\n- **Context:** FR-12 needs both an externally-cron-triggered HTTP endpoint\n  (`POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`, bearer-guarded by `CRON_SECRET`,\n  NFR-8) and a `npm run worker:email` script that loops every 15s for local/\n  simple deployments.\n- **Decision:** Both are thin callers of the same `dispatchEmails()` (ADR-4).\n  The loop script is a plain `setInterval`-driven Node script\n  (`scripts/worker-email.ts` run via `tsx`, matching the existing\n  `db:seed` pattern) — no cron library, no job-scheduler dependency.\n- **Consequences:** One dispatch implementation, two callers; adding a real\n  scheduler later (if deployment target changes) only touches the driver,\n  not `dispatchEmails()`.\n\n## ADR-6: Bell + preferences UI stays polling, built from existing MUI/MUI X — no new UI deps\n\n- **Context:** FR-14 (unread-count bell poll every 30s, paused on\n  `visibilitychange`), FR-15 (Popover + List), FR-16 (/notifications list +\n  filters), FR-18 (preferences table with per-row Switches) are all additive\n  screens; NFR-1 mandates MUI-only, and NFR-10 explicitly rules out\n  websockets.\n- **Decision:** Plain `fetch` + `setInterval`/`document.visibilityState` for\n  polling (no SWR/React Query/socket library added). All visible elements\n  come from already-installed `@mui/material`/`@mui/icons-material`\n  (Badge, IconButton, Popover, List, Table, Switch, Tabs) — same ESLint\n  `no-restricted-imports` MUI-only gate as prior PRDs enforces this.\n- **Consequences:** Zero new frontend dependencies; one shared polling hook\n  (e.g. `useUnreadCount`) can back both the bell and, if ever needed, the\n  list page — no premature abstraction beyond that single reuse point.\n\n## ADR-7: Notification type registry is a plain TS const + literal union, not a DB table\n\n- **Context:** FR-2/FR-3 need a typed registry (key → label/description/\n  category/defaults/roles) where `notify()` rejects unknown type keys **at\n  compile time**.\n- **Decision:** `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` is a `const` object in\n  `src/server/notifications/types.ts`; its key union\n  (`keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES`) is the type parameter `notify()`\n  accepts. No database-backed type table, no runtime registry lookup for\n  validity — compile-time exhaustiveness is free and stronger than a runtime\n  check.\n- **Consequences:** Adding a notification type is a code change + PR review\n  (desired: it drives UI copy and default routing), not a migration or admin\n  screen.\n\n## ADR-8: purgeOldNotifications scheduling stays in-memory, no job table\n\n- **Context:** FR-17 needs a daily purge of notifications >180 days old;\n  explicitly \"acceptable to re-run after a restart.\"\n- **Decision:** Track last-run time in a module-level variable inside the\n  dispatch route handler (checked on each `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`\n  call, which already fires every 15s–few minutes via the cron/worker); no\n  `CronJob`/`LastRun` table, no separate scheduler.\n- **Consequences:** Matches the stated tolerance for restart-driven re-runs;\n  avoids a persistence concept this PRD doesn't otherwise need.\n\n## ADR-9: MUI-only UI surface, enforced by the existing ESLint rule (carried over)\n\n- **Context:** NFR-1 requires every visible element to come from\n  `@mui/material`/`@mui/icons-material`/MUI X, with banned libraries blocked\n  by `no-restricted-imports`; email templates are the sole, explicit\n  exception (ADR-3).\n- **Decision:** Reuse the PRD-00 ESLint config as-is for all app UI; the\n  exception glob only needs to cover `src/server/email/templates/**` so\n  `@react-email/components` imports aren't flagged there.\n- **Consequences:** Consistent look/feel with PRD-00..03; the one new\n  dependency surface (react-email) is scoped and fenced by lint, not by\n  convention alone.\n","userModified":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CgCyTZp6Xt1cnwiX16Qvs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Wrote `artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md` for ticket abcd-04 (arch-research-stack), overwriting the stale prior-ticket version. Six ADRs, each citing real FR/NFR ids from the fresh `requirements.json`:\n\n1. `@react-email/components` for email templates (MUI exempted per NFR-1)\n2. Nodemailer + console-transport dev fallback\n3. Postgres `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` outbox instead of a message broker\n4. HTTP dispatch route + `tsx` loop script instead of cron infra\n5. Literal-union type registry instead of a new validation lib (zod for runtime, already a dep)\n6. Native `setInterval`/`visibilitychange` polling instead of swr/react-query\n\nChecked actual repo state first (package.json deps, prisma datasource, existing `sendInvite()` stub) so every decision reflects what's really there, not assumptions. Only two new deps needed: `nodemailer`, `@react-email/components`(+render) — both already flagged by intake's detection notes."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3926,"cache_read_input_tokens":38187,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3926},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d16cd034-ac55-45e5-b91d-ca59b3040df6","uuid":"079c7f6b-163b-4d17-86e7-e616604215b7","timestamp":"2026-10-20T05:56:52.954Z","request_id":"req_011CgCyTZZTNC1Me3rDoeWfR"}
