stant-time comparison.\n\n**Decision.** `src/server/auth/tokens.ts` uses `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64url')`\nand `createHash('sha256')`. Look up by the hash (the DB index), then confirm\nwith `timingSafeEqual` on the hash buffers. No JWT, nanoid or other library.\n\n**Consequences.** Raw tokens exist only in the returned value and the\nemailed or logged link, never in the DB or audit metadata (FR-31). The\n\"invalidate older unused tokens\" step (FR-20, FR-26) is one `updateMany`\nthat sets `usedAt` in the same `$transaction` as the create.\n\n_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-20, FR-26, FR-31, NFR-1_\n\n## ADR-6: In-memory sliding-window rate limiter (a `Map` of timestamp arrays); no Redis\n\n**Context.** FR-7 (10 per IP per 5 min) and FR-19 (3 per email per hour, 10\nper IP per hour) explicitly ask for an exported in-memory limiter in\n`src/server/rate-limit.ts`. The PRD adds no infrastructure (NFR-11).\n\n**Decision.** One `rateLimit(key, limit, windowMs): { ok, retryAfterMs }`\nover a module-level `Map<string, number[]>`, pruning expired timestamps on\neach call. Namespace keys like `login:ip:<ip>` and `forgot:email:<email>`.\nTake the IP from the first entry of `x-forwarded-for`, using the same\nhelper `audit()` uses (FR-30). Call it from the Node side (authorize and\nroute handlers), not middleware, because Edge isolates don't share memory.\n\n**Consequences.** The ceiling is per process: limits reset on restart and\naren't shared across instances. Mark the code with a\n`ponytail: per-process limiter, swap the Map for Redis/Postgres if\nhorizontally scaled` comment. With Credentials, a RATE_LIMITED outcome has\nto go through Auth.js's `CredentialsSignin` subclass `code` to reach the\nlogin form (FR-5, FR-7).\n\n_Cites: FR-7, FR-19, FR-30, NFR-8, NFR-11_\n\n## ADR-7: Validation with the existing zod + react-hook-form; one policy schema shared by client and server\n\n**Context.** FR-17 defines the policy. FR-18 needs a live checklist.\nFR-16, FR-21 and FR-23 must enforce the policy server-side.\n\n**Decision.** `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports the zod schema plus a\npure `passwordChecks(pw, email)` that returns per-rule booleans. The\nchecklist UI and the zod `superRefine` both use that function. The\ncommon-password check needs the file at\n`src/server/auth/common-passwords.txt`, so it runs only on the server: read\nthe file once into a `Set` with `fs` in a server-only module. The client\nchecklist shows the length, letter, digit and not-email rules live, and the\ncommon-password rule is reported from the server response.\n\n**Consequences.** The 1,000-word list never ships in the client bundle. The\nvalidator file has to stay importable from the client, so keep the `fs`\nread in a separate server-only file that adds the common-password refinement.\n\n_Cites: FR-17, FR-18, FR-16, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-5, NFR-8_\n\n## ADR-8: UI with MUI v9 only (Sign-in template patterns), documented through mui-mcp\n\n**Context.** NFR-6 bans non-MUI UI libraries and requires every component to\nbe looked up through mui-mcp. FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24 and FR-32 add auth\npages and top-bar changes.\n\n**Decision.** Use the installed `@mui/material` / `@mui/icons-material` 9.5:\nCard, TextField, Button, InputAdornment + IconButton (Visibility icons),\nAlert, List/ListItemIcon for the checklist, and Menu/Avatar/Chip in the top\nbar. Use the existing `useToast` for toasts (FR-21, FR-23), not notistack or\nsonner. Auth pages live in a `src/app/(auth)` route group outside the\n`(app)` shell. The mui-mcp server was reachable from this session\n(`useMuiDocs` / `fetchDocs` tools were listed), so the NFR-6 blocker doesn't\nstand at architect time. Dev must still check it itself.\n\n**Consequences.** No new UI dependency. `aria-live=\"polite\"` on the form-level\nAlert and focus-first-invalid (via react-hook-form `setFocus`) meet NFR-5.\n\n_Cites: FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24, FR-32, FR-33, NFR-5, NFR-6_\n\n## ADR-9: Schema reconciled additively with one Prisma migration\n\n**Context.** FR-1 and the intake blockers list the divergences: `ip` vs\n`ipAddress`, the extra `ARCHIVED` value, different AuditLog indexes. NFR-9\nsays never rename.\n\n**Decision.** Keep `AuditLog.ip`, keep `UserStatus.ARCHIVED` and keep the\nexisting extra indexes. Add only\n`@@index([entityType, entityId])` and `@@index([actorId, createdAt])` to\nAuditLog, plus any User auth fields the diff finds missing\n(failedLoginCount, lockedUntil, lastLoginAt, passwordChangedAt). Apply them\nin one `prisma migrate dev --name auth_reconcile` migration. FR-4's \"status\n!= ACTIVE\" check already rejects ARCHIVED.\n\n**Consequences.** The migration has no destructive steps. It adds indexes and\nnullable or defaulted columns only.\n\n_Cites: FR-1, FR-2, FR-4, FR-6, NFR-9_\n\n## ADR-10: Tests with the existing Vitest + Playwright; no new test tooling\n\n**Decision.** Run unit and integration tests (FR-36) in `vitest`, calling the\nlimiter, tokens, policy and callbackUrl modules as pure functions. Test the\nauthorize function and the service functions against the test DB. Use\n`@playwright/test` (with the installed `@axe-core/playwright` for NFR-5) for\nsign-in/out, the failure message and forgot→reset. The reset link comes from\nthe logged `sendAuthEmail` output (FR-22).\n\n_Cites: FR-22, FR-36, NFR-5, NFR-10_\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LEhrN6vsJMRBjzXTK9TF5i","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2d28ca65-c0bb-4a8d-a303-0fc23bd16a10","uuid":"5fa873cc-b33e-460c-943e-2cab221f069f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T06:35:31.675Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions & RBAC (SCH-001)\n\nTicket: abcd-1 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-04T00:00:00Z · Status: complete\n\nBase stack (`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%, confirmed by\n`package.json`): Next.js 14.2 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict,\nPrisma 5.20 / PostgreSQL, MUI v9 + emotion, zod 3, react-hook-form,\ndate-fns-tz. The auth dependencies are already installed: `next-auth ^5.0.0-beta.25`\nand `bcrypt ^6`. **This PRD adds no new runtime dependency.** The ADRs below\nonly cover the implementation choices this PRD's requirements force.\n\nBrownfield facts checked in the workspace (they correct intake in places):\n- `requireRole()` is sync and returns `{id, role}`. It has **50 call sites in\n  43 files**, not ~94. Every caller is already inside an `async` server page,\n  layout or route handler.\n- `src/server/http.ts` `ApiErrorCode` already includes `UNAUTHENTICATED` 401,\n  `FORBIDDEN` 403, `RATE_LIMITED` 429 and `INVALID_TOKEN` 410, so no envelope\n  changes are needed.\n- `AuditLog.ip String?` exists. `audit()` has no `request` param.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Auth.js v5 with a split config (edge-safe `auth.config.ts` + Node `auth.ts`)\n\n**Context.** FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12 and FR-34 need credential sign-in, JWT\nsessions and a `middleware.ts` gate. In Next 14, middleware runs on the Edge\nruntime, where `bcrypt` (a native addon) and Prisma's Node client can't load.\n\n**Decision.** Use the documented Auth.js v5 split:\n- `src/server/auth/auth.config.ts` (edge-safe): `session: { strategy: 'jwt', maxAge: 8h, updateAge }`,\n  `pages: { signIn: '/login' }`, the `authorized`/`jwt`/`session` shape\n  callbacks, and no providers that touch the DB.\n- `src/server/auth/auth.ts` (Node): `NextAuth({ ...authConfig, providers: [Credentials({ authorize })] })`,\n  exporting `{ handlers, auth, signIn, signOut }`. It also holds the\n  DB-touching `jwt` callback logic (FR-11).\n- `middleware.ts` builds `NextAuth(authConfig).auth`. It only decodes the JWT.\n- `src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts` re-exports `handlers` (FR-34).\n\nDon't use a Prisma adapter. JWT sessions with Credentials don't need one, and\nthe adapter would require Account/Session/VerificationToken models the PRD\ndoesn't define.\n\n**Consequences.** Middleware can't check `passwordChangedAt` because it has no\nDB access. That check lives in the Node-side `jwt` callback (ADR-4), which\nruns whenever `auth()` is called from server code. Because next-auth is a\nbeta, pin the installed version exactly (drop the `^`) so a beta bump can't\nchange callback signatures mid-PRD.\n\n_Cites: FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12, FR-34, NFR-2_\n\n## ADR-2: `requireRole()` / `requireUser()` become async; update all 50 call sites with `await`\n\n**Context.** FR-28 and FR-29 replace `PLACEHOLDER_USER` with the real session.\nAuth.js `auth()` is async. Intake flagged this as a blocker. Options:\n(a) make the helpers async and add `await` at each call site;\n(b) keep them sync by reading identity from headers set by middleware;\n(c) use React `cache()` plus a sync wrapper.\n\n**Decision.** (a). `getCurrentUser()`, `requireUser()` and `requireRole(...roles)`\nbecome `async` with the same arguments and the same `{id, role, ...}` return\nshape, so the signature stays compatible (FR-29). Each of the 50 call sites\nchanges from `requireRole(...)` to `await requireRole(...)`. That's a\nmechanical one-token edit, and every caller is already async. Wrap\n`getCurrentUser` in React `cache()` so a page plus its layout share one\nsession decode per request.\n\nOption (b) was rejected: identity read from headers is a spoofing risk\n(NFR-7), and it skips the FR-11 revocation check. Option (c) can't make an\nasync read sync.\n\n**Consequences.** `tsc` catches every missed `await`, because a `Promise` has\nno `.role` or `.id`. Bare `requireRole('ADMIN');` statements don't fail\ntypecheck, though, and a forgotten `await` there silently does nothing.\nEnable `@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises` for `src/**`, or have\ndev-planning grep for un-awaited calls. Redirect-vs-throw: in server pages,\nuse `redirect('/login')` / `redirect('/forbidden')` from `next/navigation`;\nin route handlers, throw `AppError` (UNAUTHENTICATED / FORBIDDEN), which\n`withHandler` already maps. Implement this as one helper that the page and\nAPI paths both call, not two copies. `course-access.ts` and\n`student-access.ts` keep taking the resolved user object and aren't modified\n(NFR-9).\n\n_Cites: FR-28, FR-29, NFR-7, NFR-8, NFR-9_\n\n## ADR-3: Password hashing with the existing `bcrypt` at cost 12, plus a dummy compare for unknown users\n\n**Context.** FR-4 sets cost 12. NFR-3 requires constant-ish timing when the\nuser doesn't exist. NFR-4 requires sign-in in under 1 s at p95.\n\n**Decision.** Use `bcrypt.hash(pw, 12)` / `bcrypt.compare`. Precompute one\nmodule-level dummy hash at cost 12 and compare against it when the user is\nmissing, disabled or locked, so every path pays one bcrypt compare. Don't use\nargon2 (it's a new native dependency and the PRD specifies bcrypt).\n\n**Consequences.** One cost-12 compare takes about 250 ms on modern hardware,\nwhich leaves room under the 1 s budget for the user lookup, the\nfailedLoginCount update and the audit insert. Never call bcrypt twice on one\nsign-in path. bcrypt runs only in Node code, never in middleware (ADR-1).\n\n_Cites: FR-4, FR-6, NFR-3, NFR-4_\n\n## ADR-4: Session revocation via a `passwordChangedAt` check in the jwt callback, throttled to every 5 minutes\n\n**Context.** FR-11 and FR-23 require that a password change or reset\ninvalidates other sessions. Sessions are stateless JWTs (FR-10), so there's\nno session table.\n\n**Decision.** Store `iat` and a `checkedAt` timestamp in the token. In the\nNode `jwt` callback, if `now - checkedAt > 5 min`, select only\n`passwordChangedAt, status` for `token.id`. If `passwordChangedAt > iat` or\nthe status isn't ACTIVE, return `null` to invalidate the session. Otherwise\nupdate `checkedAt`. For FR-23's \"re-issue current session\", call `unstable_update()`\n(the v5 `update`) after the change, which mints a token with a fresh `iat`.\n\n**Consequences.** Revocation can lag by up to 5 minutes. The PRD explicitly\naccepts that window. Edge middleware can still let a revoked token through\nfor page navigation in that window. The server-side `requireUser()` call\nthen catches it, and that call is the real guard (NFR-7).\n\n_Cites: FR-10, FR-11, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-7_\n\n## ADR-5: Tokens with `node:crypto` only (randomBytes, SHA-256, timingSafeEqual)\n\n**Context.** FR-13 needs a single AuthToken module. NFR-1 requires stored\nhashes and constant-time comparison.\n\n**Decision.** `src/server/auth/tokens.ts` uses `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64url')`\nand `createHash('sha256')`. Look up by the hash (the DB index), then confirm\nwith `timingSafeEqual` on the hash buffers. No JWT, nanoid or other library.\n\n**Consequences.** Raw tokens exist only in the returned value and the\nemailed or logged link, never in the DB or audit metadata (FR-31). The\n\"invalidate older unused tokens\" step (FR-20, FR-26) is one `updateMany`\nthat sets `usedAt` in the same `$transaction` as the create.\n\n_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-20, FR-26, FR-31, NFR-1_\n\n## ADR-6: In-memory sliding-window rate limiter (a `Map` of timestamp arrays); no Redis\n\n**Context.** FR-7 (10 per IP per 5 min) and FR-19 (3 per email per hour, 10\nper IP per hour) explicitly ask for an exported in-memory limiter in\n`src/server/rate-limit.ts`. The PRD adds no infrastructure (NFR-11).\n\n**Decision.** One `rateLimit(key, limit, windowMs): { ok, retryAfterMs }`\nover a module-level `Map<string, number[]>`, pruning expired timestamps on\neach call. Namespace keys like `login:ip:<ip>` and `forgot:email:<email>`.\nTake the IP from the first entry of `x-forwarded-for`, using the same\nhelper `audit()` uses (FR-30). Call it from the Node side (authorize and\nroute handlers), not middleware, because Edge isolates don't share memory.\n\n**Consequences.** The ceiling is per process: limits reset on restart and\naren't shared across instances. Mark the code with a\n`ponytail: per-process limiter, swap the Map for Redis/Postgres if\nhorizontally scaled` comment. With Credentials, a RATE_LIMITED outcome has\nto go through Auth.js's `CredentialsSignin` subclass `code` to reach the\nlogin form (FR-5, FR-7).\n\n_Cites: FR-7, FR-19, FR-30, NFR-8, NFR-11_\n\n## ADR-7: Validation with the existing zod + react-hook-form; one policy schema shared by client and server\n\n**Context.** FR-17 defines the policy. FR-18 needs a live checklist.\nFR-16, FR-21 and FR-23 must enforce the policy server-side.\n\n**Decision.** `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports the zod schema plus a\npure `passwordChecks(pw, email)` that returns per-rule booleans. The\nchecklist UI and the zod `superRefine` both use that function. The\ncommon-password check needs the file at\n`src/server/auth/common-passwords.txt`, so it runs only on the server: read\nthe file once into a `Set` with `fs` in a server-only module. The client\nchecklist shows the length, letter, digit and not-email rules live, and the\ncommon-password rule is reported from the server response.\n\n**Consequences.** The 1,000-word list never ships in the client bundle. The\nvalidator file has to stay importable from the client, so keep the `fs`\nread in a separate server-only file that adds the common-password refinement.\n\n_Cites: FR-17, FR-18, FR-16, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-5, NFR-8_\n\n## ADR-8: UI with MUI v9 only (Sign-in template patterns), documented through mui-mcp\n\n**Context.** NFR-6 bans non-MUI UI libraries and requires every component to\nbe looked up through mui-mcp. FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24 and FR-32 add auth\npages and top-bar changes.\n\n**Decision.** Use the installed `@mui/material` / `@mui/icons-material` 9.5:\nCard, TextField, Button, InputAdornment + IconButton (Visibility icons),\nAlert, List/ListItemIcon for the checklist, and Menu/Avatar/Chip in the top\nbar. Use the existing `useToast` for toasts (FR-21, FR-23), not notistack or\nsonner. Auth pages live in a `src/app/(auth)` route group outside the\n`(app)` shell. The mui-mcp server was reachable from this session\n(`useMuiDocs` / `fetchDocs` tools were listed), so the NFR-6 blocker doesn't\nstand at architect time. Dev must still check it itself.\n\n**Consequences.** No new UI dependency. `aria-live=\"polite\"` on the form-level\nAlert and focus-first-invalid (via react-hook-form `setFocus`) meet NFR-5.\n\n_Cites: FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24, FR-32, FR-33, NFR-5, NFR-6_\n\n## ADR-9: Schema reconciled additively with one Prisma migration\n\n**Context.** FR-1 and the intake blockers list the divergences: `ip` vs\n`ipAddress`, the extra `ARCHIVED` value, different AuditLog indexes. NFR-9\nsays never rename.\n\n**Decision.** Keep `AuditLog.ip`, keep `UserStatus.ARCHIVED` and keep the\nexisting extra indexes. Add only\n`@@index([entityType, entityId])` and `@@index([actorId, createdAt])` to\nAuditLog, plus any User auth fields the diff finds missing\n(failedLoginCount, lockedUntil, lastLoginAt, passwordChangedAt). Apply them\nin one `prisma migrate dev --name auth_reconcile` migration. FR-4's \"status\n!= ACTIVE\" check already rejects ARCHIVED.\n\n**Consequences.** The migration has no destructive steps. It adds indexes and\nnullable or defaulted columns only.\n\n_Cites: FR-1, FR-2, FR-4, FR-6, NFR-9_\n\n## ADR-10: Tests with the existing Vitest + Playwright; no new test tooling\n\n**Decision.** Run unit and integration tests (FR-36) in `vitest`, calling the\nlimiter, tokens, policy and callbackUrl modules as pure functions. Test the\nauthorize function and the service functions against the test DB. Use\n`@playwright/test` (with the installed `@axe-core/playwright` for NFR-5) for\nsign-in/out, the failure message and forgot→reset. The reset link comes from\nthe logged `sendAuthEmail` output (FR-22).\n\n_Cites: FR-22, FR-36, NFR-5, NFR-10_\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":124,"newStart":1,"newLines":228,"lines":["-# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-05 Classes & Enrollment","+# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions & RBAC (SCH-001)"," ","-Ticket: abcd-05 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Status: complete","+Ticket: abcd-1 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-04T00:00:00Z · Status: complete"," ","-Base platform (from `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%):","-Next.js 14 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5 / PostgreSQL,","-MUI v9 + emotion, `zod` for input validation, `date-fns`. This PRD is","-brownfield on top of that stack — intake's detection notes already confirm","-it \"adds no new frontend framework or dependency,\" only new Prisma models","-and pages. The decisions below are the implementation-pattern deltas this","-PRD's requirements force, not a re-litigation of the base stack. No new","-runtime dependency is added by this PRD.","+Base stack (`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%, confirmed by","+`package.json`): Next.js 14.2 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict,","+Prisma 5.20 / PostgreSQL, MUI v9 + emotion, zod 3, react-hook-form,","+date-fns-tz. The auth dependencies are already installed: `next-auth ^5.0.0-beta.25`","+and `bcrypt ^6`. **This PRD adds no new runtime dependency.** The ADRs below","+only cover the implementation choices this PRD's requirements force."," ","-## ADR-1: Bulk enroll/drop writes — `Prisma.$transaction` with batched","-`createMany`/`updateMany`, no job queue","+Brownfield facts checked in the workspace (they correct intake in places):","+- `requireRole()` is sync and returns `{id, role}`. It has **50 call sites in","+  43 files**, not ~94. Every caller is already inside an `async` server page,","+  layout or route handler.","+- `src/server/http.ts` `ApiErrorCode` already includes `UNAUTHENTICATED` 401,","+  `FORBIDDEN` 403, `RATE_LIMITED` 429 and `INVALID_TOKEN` 410, so no envelope","+  changes are needed.","+- `AuditLog.ip String?` exists. `audit()` has no `request` param."," ","-**Context.** NFR-3 requires adding 30 students to a section with 8 core","-courses (240 resulting enrollments) to complete in under 1 second. FR-7–","-FR-10 describe enroll-on-add, drop-on-remove, move-section, and","-create-core-course as all-or-nothing operations touching many `Enrollment`","-rows at once.","+---"," ","-**Decision.** Implement each of these as a single `prisma.$transaction`","-that computes the affected (studentId, courseId) pairs in memory first,","-then issues one `enrollment.createMany({ skipDuplicates: true })` for new","-ACTIVE rows and one `enrollment.updateMany` (keyed on the unique","-`[courseId, studentId]`) to reactivate previously-DROPPED rows, instead of","-looping `create`/`update` calls per student per course. This is plain","-Prisma batch-write API already in the installed `@prisma/client` — no","-queue, no background worker, no new dependency. 240 rows in one","-`createMany` plus one `updateMany` comfortably clears the 1-second bar on","-Postgres; a per-row loop (240 round-trips) would not.","+## ADR-1: Auth.js v5 with a split config (edge-safe `auth.config.ts` + Node `auth.ts`)"," ","-**Consequences.** Business logic that decides \"create vs. reactivate\" for","-each pair must be computed with a single prior `findMany`, not per-row","-queries, to keep the whole operation at a small constant number of","-round-trips. If a future PRD needs enroll/drop across thousands of rows","-(bulk import), that's a new ADR for chunking or a background job — not a","-retrofit of this one.","+**Context.** FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12 and FR-34 need credential sign-in, JWT","+sessions and a `middleware.ts` gate. In Next 14, middleware runs on the Edge","+runtime, where `bcrypt` (a native addon) and Prisma's Node client can't load."," ","-_Cites: FR-7, FR-8, FR-9, FR-10, NFR-3_","+**Decision.** Use the documented Auth.js v5 split:","+- `src/server/auth/auth.config.ts` (edge-safe): `session: { strategy: 'jwt', maxAge: 8h, updateAge }`,","+  `pages: { signIn: '/login' }`, the `authorized`/`jwt`/`session` shape","+  callbacks, and no providers that touch the DB.","+- `src/server/auth/auth.ts` (Node): `NextAuth({ ...authConfig, providers: [Credentials({ authorize })] })`,","+  exporting `{ handlers, auth, signIn, signOut }`. It also holds the","+  DB-touching `jwt` callback logic (FR-11).","+- `middleware.ts` builds `NextAuth(authConfig).auth`. It only decodes the JWT.","+- `src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts` re-exports `handlers` (FR-34)."," ","-## ADR-2: Capacity + uniqueness enforcement — DB unique constraints for","-identity, in-transaction count check for capacity, no distributed lock","+Don't use a Prisma adapter. JWT sessions with Credentials don't need one, and","+the adapter would require Account/Session/VerificationToken models the PRD","+doesn't define."," ","-**Context.** NFR-2 requires the one-section-per-student-per-year rule to be","-a database constraint, not just app logic. FR-11 requires capacity","-enforcement (section and course) that refuses with CONFLICT unless an","-explicit \"Override capacity\" flag is set, under concurrent admin actions.","+**Consequences.** Middleware can't check `passwordChangedAt` because it has no","+DB access. That check lives in the Node-side `jwt` callback (ADR-4), which","+runs whenever `auth()` is called from server code. Because next-auth is a","+beta, pin the installed version exactly (drop the `^`) so a beta bump can't","+change callback signatures mid-PRD."," ","-**Decision.** Identity/duplication rules (`@@unique([studentId,","-academicYearId])` on `SectionMember`, `@@unique([courseId, studentId])` on","-`Enrollment`) are enforced by Postgres unique indexes already specified in","-FR-1; application code catches the Prisma `P2002` violation and maps it to","-the existing CONFLICT error envelope rather than pre-checking existence","-first (avoids a check-then-insert race). Capacity, which is a *computed*","-count rather than a stored invariant, is checked inside the same","-`$transaction` that performs the insert — `count()` the current","-members/enrollments, compare to `capacity`, and only then","-`create`/`createMany`, so a concurrent request either serializes behind the","-transaction or re-checks and fails cleanly. No advisory lock, no Redis, no","-distributed-lock library: Postgres's own transactional read-your-writes","-within one `$transaction` is sufficient at this app's scale (a school","-admin console, not a high-concurrency storefront).","+_Cites: FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12, FR-34, NFR-2_"," ","-**Consequences.** Capacity checks are \"good enough\" serialization, not a","-hard guarantee under extreme concurrent load from multiple admins hitting","-the exact same section simultaneously; acceptable because this is an","-internal admin tool with low write concurrency. If that ever changes,","-upgrading the capacity check to `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on a row is a small","-follow-up, not a new ADR.","+## ADR-2: `requireRole()` / `requireUser()` become async; update all 50 call sites with `await`"," ","-_Cites: FR-1, FR-11, NFR-2_","+**Context.** FR-28 and FR-29 replace `PLACEHOLDER_USER` with the real session.","+Auth.js `auth()` is async. Intake flagged this as a blocker. Options:","+(a) make the helpers async and add `await` at each call site;","+(b) keep them sync by reading identity from headers set by middleware;","+(c) use React `cache()` plus a sync wrapper."," ","-## ADR-3: No new dependency for the admin UI (tables, dialogs, pickers)","+**Decision.** (a). `getCurrentUser()`, `requireUser()` and `requireRole(...roles)`","+become `async` with the same arguments and the same `{id, role, ...}` return","+shape, so the signature stays compatible (FR-29). Each of the 50 call sites","+changes from `requireRole(...)` to `await requireRole(...)`. That's a","+mechanical one-token edit, and every caller is already async. Wrap","+`getCurrentUser` in React `cache()` so a page plus its layout share one","+session decode per request."," ","-**Context.** NFR-1 mandates every visible element in the sections/courses/","-enrollment screens come from `@mui/material`, `@mui/icons-material`, or MUI","-X, with no banned libraries (Tailwind, shadcn/ui, Radix, react-select,","-etc.). FR-2–FR-6, FR-13–FR-19 describe filtered tables, create/edit","-dialogs, multi-select pickers, tabs, and card grids.","+Option (b) was rejected: identity read from headers is a spoofing risk","+(NFR-7), and it skips the FR-11 revocation check. Option (c) can't make an","+async read sync."," ","-**Decision.** Build all of it from the already-installed MUI v9 set plus","-the existing shared components (`DataTable`, `UserPicker`, `ConfirmDialog`,","-`useToast`, `PageHeader`) per the brownfield reuse already confirmed in","-intake. Multi-select student/subject pickers extend `UserPicker`'s","-existing `role` filter pattern rather than introducing a new","-autocomplete/select component. No new UI dependency.","+**Consequences.** `tsc` catches every missed `await`, because a `Promise` has","+no `.role` or `.id`. Bare `requireRole('ADMIN');` statements don't fail","+typecheck, though, and a forgotten `await` there silently does nothing.","+Enable `@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises` for `src/**`, or have","+dev-planning grep for un-awaited calls. Redirect-vs-throw: in server pages,","+use `redirect('/login')` / `redirect('/forbidden')` from `next/navigation`;","+in route handlers, throw `AppError` (UNAUTHENTICATED / FORBIDDEN), which","+`withHandler` already maps. Implement this as one helper that the page and","+API paths both call, not two copies. `course-access.ts` and","+`student-access.ts` keep taking the resolved user object and aren't modified","+(NFR-9)."," ","-**Consequences.** None beyond the existing MUI-MCP lookup discipline","-(NFR-1) for any component variant not already used elsewhere in the","-codebase.","+_Cites: FR-28, FR-29, NFR-7, NFR-8, NFR-9_"," ","-_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-5, FR-6, FR-13, FR-14, FR-15, FR-17, FR-19, NFR-1_","+## ADR-3: Password hashing with the existing `bcrypt` at cost 12, plus a dummy compare for unknown users"," ","-## ADR-4: Auth — keep the `requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)` stand-in, no new","-auth helper","+**Context.** FR-4 sets cost 12. NFR-3 requires constant-ish timing when the","+user doesn't exist. NFR-4 requires sign-in in under 1 s at p95."," ","-**Context.** NFR-7 flags that `src/server/auth/permissions.ts` and","-`requireUser()` described in the platform context don't exist yet — only","-`requireRole.ts` and `student-access.ts` are present. This is the same","-brownfield gap PRD-04's architect handoff already flagged.","+**Decision.** Use `bcrypt.hash(pw, 12)` / `bcrypt.compare`. Precompute one","+module-level dummy hash at cost 12 and compare against it when the user is","+missing, disabled or locked, so every path pays one bcrypt compare. Don't use","+argon2 (it's a new native dependency and the PRD specifies bcrypt)."," ","-**Decision.** New route handlers in this PRD (sections/courses/enrollment","-CRUD, move-section, roster) continue to use the existing","-`requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)`-style stand-in, and `assertCanViewCourse`/","-`assertCanManageCourse` (FR-18) are added as plain functions in","-`src/server/auth/course-access.ts` alongside the existing","-`student-access.ts`, not as part of a new `permissions.ts` module. No new","-auth library, no new abstraction invented ahead of PRD-01 landing it","-properly.","+**Consequences.** One cost-12 compare takes about 250 ms on modern hardware,","+which leaves room under the 1 s budget for the user lookup, the","+failedLoginCount update and the audit insert. Never call bcrypt twice on one","+sign-in path. bcrypt runs only in Node code, never in middleware (ADR-1)."," ","-**Consequences.** Role-gating logic stays slightly duplicated across","-per-feature `*-access.ts` files until PRD-01 ships a real","-`permissions.ts`; that consolidation is PRD-01's job, not this one's.","+_Cites: FR-4, FR-6, NFR-3, NFR-4_"," ","-_Cites: FR-18, FR-22, NFR-7_","+## ADR-4: Session revocation via a `passwordChangedAt` check in the jwt callback, throttled to every 5 minutes"," ","-## Summary of new dependencies","+**Context.** FR-11 and FR-23 require that a password change or reset","+invalidates other sessions. Sessions are stateless JWTs (FR-10), so there's","+no session table."," ","-None. Every requirement in this PRD (bulk transactional writes, capacity","-checks, uniqueness, admin UI, auth gating) is covered by Prisma 5,","-PostgreSQL, MUI v9, and TypeScript already in the stack — intake's","-confidence-100% stack detection holds.","+**Decision.** Store `iat` and a `checkedAt` timestamp in the token. In the","+Node `jwt` callback, if `now - checkedAt > 5 min`, select only","+`passwordChangedAt, status` for `token.id`. If `passwordChangedAt > iat` or","+the status isn't ACTIVE, return `null` to invalidate the session. Otherwise","+update `checkedAt`. For FR-23's \"re-issue current session\", call `unstable_update()`","+(the v5 `update`) after the change, which mints a token with a fresh `iat`.","+","+**Consequences.** Revocation can lag by up to 5 minutes. The PRD explicitly","+accepts that window. Edge middleware can still let a revoked token through","+for page navigation in that window. The server-side `requireUser()` call","+then catches it, and that call is the real guard (NFR-7).","+","+_Cites: FR-10, FR-11, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-7_","+","+## ADR-5: Tokens with `node:crypto` only (randomBytes, SHA-256, timingSafeEqual)","+","+**Context.** FR-13 needs a single AuthToken module. NFR-1 requires stored","+hashes and constant-time comparison.","+","+**Decision.** `src/server/auth/tokens.ts` uses `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64url')`","+and `createHash('sha256')`. Look up by the hash (the DB index), then confirm","+with `timingSafeEqual` on the hash buffers. No JWT, nanoid or other library.","+","+**Consequences.** Raw tokens exist only in the returned value and the","+emailed or logged link, never in the DB or audit metadata (FR-31). The","+\"invalidate older unused tokens\" step (FR-20, FR-26) is one `updateMany`","+that sets `usedAt` in the same `$transaction` as the create.","+","+_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-20, FR-26, FR-31, NFR-1_","+","+## ADR-6: In-memory sliding-window rate limiter (a `Map` of timestamp arrays); no Redis","+","+**Context.** FR-7 (10 per IP per 5 min) and FR-19 (3 per email per hour, 10","+per IP per hour) explicitly ask for an exported in-memory limiter in","+`src/server/rate-limit.ts`. The PRD adds no infrastructure (NFR-11).","+","+**Decision.** One `rateLimit(key, limit, windowMs): { ok, retryAfterMs }`","+over a module-level `Map<string, number[]>`, pruning expired timestamps on","+each call. Namespace keys like `login:ip:<ip>` and `forgot:email:<email>`.","+Take the IP from the first entry of `x-forwarded-for`, using the same","+helper `audit()` uses (FR-30). Call it from the Node side (authorize and","+route handlers), not middleware, because Edge isolates don't share memory.","+","+**Consequences.** The ceiling is per process: limits reset on restart and","+aren't shared across instances. Mark the code with a","+`ponytail: per-process limiter, swap the Map for Redis/Postgres if","+horizontally scaled` comment. With Credentials, a RATE_LIMITED outcome has","+to go through Auth.js's `CredentialsSignin` subclass `code` to reach the","+login form (FR-5, FR-7).","+","+_Cites: FR-7, FR-19, FR-30, NFR-8, NFR-11_","+","+## ADR-7: Validation with the existing zod + react-hook-form; one policy schema shared by client and server","+","+**Context.** FR-17 defines the policy. FR-18 needs a live checklist.","+FR-16, FR-21 and FR-23 must enforce the policy server-side.","+","+**Decision.** `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports the zod schema plus a","+pure `passwordChecks(pw, email)` that returns per-rule booleans. The","+checklist UI and the zod `superRefine` both use that function. The","+common-password check needs the file at","+`src/server/auth/common-passwords.txt`, so it runs only on the server: read","+the file once into a `Set` with `fs` in a server-only module. The client","+checklist shows the length, letter, digit and not-email rules live, and the","+common-password rule is reported from the server response.","+","+**Consequences.** The 1,000-word list never ships in the client bundle. The","+validator file has to stay importable from the client, so keep the `fs`","+read in a separate server-only file that adds the common-password refinement.","+","+_Cites: FR-17, FR-18, FR-16, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-5, NFR-8_","+","+## ADR-8: UI with MUI v9 only (Sign-in template patterns), documented through mui-mcp","+","+**Context.** NFR-6 bans non-MUI UI libraries and requires every component to","+be looked up through mui-mcp. FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24 and FR-32 add auth","+pages and top-bar changes.","+","+**Decision.** Use the installed `@mui/material` / `@mui/icons-material` 9.5:","+Card, TextField, Button, InputAdornment + IconButton (Visibility icons),","+Alert, List/ListItemIcon for the checklist, and Menu/Avatar/Chip in the top","+bar. Use the existing `useToast` for toasts (FR-21, FR-23), not notistack or","+sonner. Auth pages live in a `src/app/(auth)` route group outside the","+`(app)` shell. The mui-mcp server was reachable from this session","+(`useMuiDocs` / `fetchDocs` tools were listed), so the NFR-6 blocker doesn't","+stand at architect time. Dev must still check it itself.","+","+**Consequences.** No new UI dependency. `aria-live=\"polite\"` on the form-level","+Alert and focus-first-invalid (via react-hook-form `setFocus`) meet NFR-5.","+","+_Cites: FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24, FR-32, FR-33, NFR-5, NFR-6_","+","+## ADR-9: Schema reconciled additively with one Prisma migration","+","+**Context.** FR-1 and the intake blockers list the divergences: `ip` vs","+`ipAddress`, the extra `ARCHIVED` value, different AuditLog indexes. NFR-9","+says never rename.","+","+**Decision.** Keep `AuditLog.ip`, keep `UserStatus.ARCHIVED` and keep the","+existing extra indexes. Add only","+`@@index([entityType, entityId])` and `@@index([actorId, createdAt])` to","+AuditLog, plus any User auth fields the diff finds missing","+(failedLoginCount, lockedUntil, lastLoginAt, passwordChangedAt). Apply them","+in one `prisma migrate dev --name auth_reconcile` migration. FR-4's \"status","+!= ACTIVE\" check already rejects ARCHIVED.","+","+**Consequences.** The migration has no destructive steps. It adds indexes and","+nullable or defaulted columns only.","+","+_Cites: FR-1, FR-2, FR-4, FR-6, NFR-9_","+","+## ADR-10: Tests with the existing Vitest + Playwright; no new test tooling","+","+**Decision.** Run unit and integration tests (FR-36) in `vitest`, calling the","+limiter, tokens, policy and callbackUrl modules as pure functions. Test the","+authorize function and the service functions against the test DB. Use","+`@playwright/test` (with the installed `@axe-core/playwright` for NFR-5) for","+sign-in/out, the failure message and forgot→reset. The reset link comes from","+the logged `sendAuthEmail` output (FR-22).","+","+_Cites: FR-22, FR-36, NFR-5, NFR-10_"]}],"originalFile":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-05 Classes & Enrollment\n\nTicket: abcd-05 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Status: complete\n\nBase platform (from `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%):\nNext.js 14 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5 / PostgreSQL,\nMUI v9 + emotion, `zod` for input validation, `date-fns`. This PRD is\nbrownfield on top of that stack — intake's detection notes already confirm\nit \"adds no new frontend framework or dependency,\" only new Prisma models\nand pages. The decisions below are the implementation-pattern deltas this\nPRD's requirements force, not a re-litigation of the base stack. No new\nruntime dependency is added by this PRD.\n\n## ADR-1: Bulk enroll/drop writes — `Prisma.$transaction` with batched\n`createMany`/`updateMany`, no job queue\n\n**Context.** NFR-3 requires adding 30 students to a section with 8 core\ncourses (240 resulting enrollments) to complete in under 1 second. FR-7–\nFR-10 describe enroll-on-add, drop-on-remove, move-section, and\ncreate-core-course as all-or-nothing operations touching many `Enrollment`\nrows at once.\n\n**Decision.** Implement each of these as a single `prisma.$transaction`\nthat computes the affected (studentId, courseId) pairs in memory first,\nthen issues one `enrollment.createMany({ skipDuplicates: true })` for new\nACTIVE rows and one `enrollment.updateMany` (keyed on the unique\n`[courseId, studentId]`) to reactivate previously-DROPPED rows, instead of\nlooping `create`/`update` calls per student per course. This is plain\nPrisma batch-write API already in the installed `@prisma/client` — no\nqueue, no background worker, no new dependency. 240 rows in one\n`createMany` plus one `updateMany` comfortably clears the 1-second bar on\nPostgres; a per-row loop (240 round-trips) would not.\n\n**Consequences.** Business logic that decides \"create vs. reactivate\" for\neach pair must be computed with a single prior `findMany`, not per-row\nqueries, to keep the whole operation at a small constant number of\nround-trips. If a future PRD needs enroll/drop across thousands of rows\n(bulk import), that's a new ADR for chunking or a background job — not a\nretrofit of this one.\n\n_Cites: FR-7, FR-8, FR-9, FR-10, NFR-3_\n\n## ADR-2: Capacity + uniqueness enforcement — DB unique constraints for\nidentity, in-transaction count check for capacity, no distributed lock\n\n**Context.** NFR-2 requires the one-section-per-student-per-year rule to be\na database constraint, not just app logic. FR-11 requires capacity\nenforcement (section and course) that refuses with CONFLICT unless an\nexplicit \"Override capacity\" flag is set, under concurrent admin actions.\n\n**Decision.** Identity/duplication rules (`@@unique([studentId,\nacademicYearId])` on `SectionMember`, `@@unique([courseId, studentId])` on\n`Enrollment`) are enforced by Postgres unique indexes already specified in\nFR-1; application code catches the Prisma `P2002` violation and maps it to\nthe existing CONFLICT error envelope rather than pre-checking existence\nfirst (avoids a check-then-insert race). Capacity, which is a *computed*\ncount rather than a stored invariant, is checked inside the same\n`$transaction` that performs the insert — `count()` the current\nmembers/enrollments, compare to `capacity`, and only then\n`create`/`createMany`, so a concurrent request either serializes behind the\ntransaction or re-checks and fails cleanly. No advisory lock, no Redis, no\ndistributed-lock library: Postgres's own transactional read-your-writes\nwithin one `$transaction` is sufficient at this app's scale (a school\nadmin console, not a high-concurrency storefront).\n\n**Consequences.** Capacity checks are \"good enough\" serialization, not a\nhard guarantee under extreme concurrent load from multiple admins hitting\nthe exact same section simultaneously; acceptable because this is an\ninternal admin tool with low write concurrency. If that ever changes,\nupgrading the capacity check to `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on a row is a small\nfollow-up, not a new ADR.\n\n_Cites: FR-1, FR-11, NFR-2_\n\n## ADR-3: No new dependency for the admin UI (tables, dialogs, pickers)\n\n**Context.** NFR-1 mandates every visible element in the sections/courses/\nenrollment screens come from `@mui/material`, `@mui/icons-material`, or MUI\nX, with no banned libraries (Tailwind, shadcn/ui, Radix, react-select,\netc.). FR-2–FR-6, FR-13–FR-19 describe filtered tables, create/edit\ndialogs, multi-select pickers, tabs, and card grids.\n\n**Decision.** Build all of it from the already-installed MUI v9 set plus\nthe existing shared components (`DataTable`, `UserPicker`, `ConfirmDialog`,\n`useToast`, `PageHeader`) per the brownfield reuse already confirmed in\nintake. Multi-select student/subject pickers extend `UserPicker`'s\nexisting `role` filter pattern rather than introducing a new\nautocomplete/select component. No new UI dependency.\n\n**Consequences.** None beyond the existing MUI-MCP lookup discipline\n(NFR-1) for any component variant not already used elsewhere in the\ncodebase.\n\n_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-5, FR-6, FR-13, FR-14, FR-15, FR-17, FR-19, NFR-1_\n\n## ADR-4: Auth — keep the `requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)` stand-in, no new\nauth helper\n\n**Context.** NFR-7 flags that `src/server/auth/permissions.ts` and\n`requireUser()` described in the platform context don't exist yet — only\n`requireRole.ts` and `student-access.ts` are present. This is the same\nbrownfield gap PRD-04's architect handoff already flagged.\n\n**Decision.** New route handlers in this PRD (sections/courses/enrollment\nCRUD, move-section, roster) continue to use the existing\n`requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)`-style stand-in, and `assertCanViewCourse`/\n`assertCanManageCourse` (FR-18) are added as plain functions in\n`src/server/auth/course-access.ts` alongside the existing\n`student-access.ts`, not as part of a new `permissions.ts` module. No new\nauth library, no new abstraction invented ahead of PRD-01 landing it\nproperly.\n\n**Consequences.** Role-gating logic stays slightly duplicated across\nper-feature `*-access.ts` files until PRD-01 ships a real\n`permissions.ts`; that consolidation is PRD-01's job, not this one's.\n\n_Cites: FR-18, FR-22, NFR-7_\n\n## Summary of new dependencies\n\nNone. Every requirement in this PRD (bulk transactional writes, capacity\nchecks, uniqueness, admin UI, auth gating) is covered by Prisma 5,\nPostgreSQL, MUI v9, and TypeScript already in the stack — intake's\nconfidence-100% stack detection holds.\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LEhrN6vsJMRBjzXTK9TF5i","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
