{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XAP84zWknDnX79Fp6RAwJW","type":"tool_result","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions & RBAC (SCH-001)\n\nTicket: abcd-1 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-04T00:00:00Z · Status: complete\n\nBase stack (`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%, confirmed by\n`package.json`): Next.js 14.2 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict,\nPrisma 5.20 / PostgreSQL, MUI v9 + emotion, zod 3, react-hook-form,\ndate-fns-tz. The auth dependencies are already installed: `next-auth ^5.0.0-beta.25`\nand `bcrypt ^6`. **This PRD adds no new runtime dependency.** The ADRs below\nonly cover the implementation choices this PRD's requirements force.\n\nBrownfield facts checked in the workspace (they correct intake in places):\n- `requireRole()` is sync and returns `{id, role}`. It has **50 call sites in\n  43 files**, not ~94. Every caller is already inside an `async` server page,\n  layout or route handler.\n- `src/server/http.ts` `ApiErrorCode` already includes `UNAUTHENTICATED` 401,\n  `FORBIDDEN` 403, `RATE_LIMITED` 429 and `INVALID_TOKEN` 410, so no envelope\n  changes are needed.\n- `AuditLog.ip String?` exists. `audit()` has no `request` param.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Auth.js v5 with a split config (edge-safe `auth.config.ts` + Node `auth.ts`)\n\n**Context.** FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12 and FR-34 need credential sign-in, JWT\nsessions and a `middleware.ts` gate. In Next 14, middleware runs on the Edge\nruntime, where `bcrypt` (a native addon) and Prisma's Node client can't load.\n\n**Decision.** Use the documented Auth.js v5 split:\n- `src/server/auth/auth.config.ts` (edge-safe): `session: { strategy: 'jwt', maxAge: 8h, updateAge }`,\n  `pages: { signIn: '/login' }`, the `authorized`/`jwt`/`session` shape\n  callbacks, and no providers that touch the DB.\n- `src/server/auth/auth.ts` (Node): `NextAuth({ ...authConfig, providers: [Credentials({ authorize })] })`,\n  exporting `{ handlers, auth, signIn, signOut }`. It also holds the\n  DB-touching `jwt` callback logic (FR-11).\n- `middleware.ts` builds `NextAuth(authConfig).auth`. It only decodes the JWT.\n- `src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts` re-exports `handlers` (FR-34).\n\nDon't use a Prisma adapter. JWT sessions with Credentials don't need one, and\nthe adapter would require Account/Session/VerificationToken models the PRD\ndoesn't define.\n\n**Consequences.** Middleware can't check `passwordChangedAt` because it has no\nDB access. That check lives in the Node-side `jwt` callback (ADR-4), which\nruns whenever `auth()` is called from server code. Because next-auth is a\nbeta, pin the installed version exactly (drop the `^`) so a beta bump can't\nchange callback signatures mid-PRD.\n\n_Cites: FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12, FR-34, NFR-2_\n\n## ADR-2: `requireRole()` / `requireUser()` become async; update all 50 call sites with `await`\n\n**Context.** FR-28 and FR-29 replace `PLACEHOLDER_USER` with the real session.\nAuth.js `auth()` is async. Intake flagged this as a blocker. Options:\n(a) make the helpers async and add `await` at each call site;\n(b) keep them sync by reading identity from headers set by middleware;\n(c) use React `cache()` plus a sync wrapper.\n\n**Decision.** (a). `getCurrentUser()`, `requireUser()` and `requireRole(...roles)`\nbecome `async` with the same arguments and the same `{id, role, ...}` return\nshape, so the signature stays compatible (FR-29). Each of the 50 call sites\nchanges from `requireRole(...)` to `await requireRole(...)`. That's a\nmechanical one-token edit, and every caller is already async. Wrap\n`getCurrentUser` in React `cache()` so a page plus its layout share one\nsession decode per request.\n\nOption (b) was rejected: identity read from headers is a spoofing risk\n(NFR-7), and it skips the FR-11 revocation check. Option (c) can't make an\nasync read sync.\n\n**Consequences.** `tsc` catches every missed `await`, because a `Promise` has\nno `.role` or `.id`. Bare `requireRole('ADMIN');` statements don't fail\ntypecheck, though, and a forgotten `await` there silently does nothing.\nEnable `@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises` for `src/**`, or have\ndev-planning grep for un-awaited calls. Redirect-vs-throw: in server pages,\nuse `redirect('/login')` / `redirect('/forbidden')` from `next/navigation`;\nin route handlers, throw `AppError` (UNAUTHENTICATED / FORBIDDEN), which\n`withHandler` already maps. Implement this as one helper that the page and\nAPI paths both call, not two copies. `course-access.ts` and\n`student-access.ts` keep taking the resolved user object and aren't modified\n(NFR-9).\n\n_Cites: FR-28, FR-29, NFR-7, NFR-8, NFR-9_\n\n## ADR-3: Password hashing with the existing `bcrypt` at cost 12, plus a dummy compare for unknown users\n\n**Context.** FR-4 sets cost 12. NFR-3 requires constant-ish timing when the\nuser doesn't exist. NFR-4 requires sign-in in under 1 s at p95.\n\n**Decision.** Use `bcrypt.hash(pw, 12)` / `bcrypt.compare`. Precompute one\nmodule-level dummy hash at cost 12 and compare against it when the user is\nmissing, disabled or locked, so every path pays one bcrypt compare. Don't use\nargon2 (it's a new native dependency and the PRD specifies bcrypt).\n\n**Consequences.** One cost-12 compare takes about 250 ms on modern hardware,\nwhich leaves room under the 1 s budget for the user lookup, the\nfailedLoginCount update and the audit insert. Never call bcrypt twice on one\nsign-in path. bcrypt runs only in Node code, never in middleware (ADR-1).\n\n_Cites: FR-4, FR-6, NFR-3, NFR-4_\n\n## ADR-4: Session revocation via a `passwordChangedAt` check in the jwt callback, throttled to every 5 minutes\n\n**Context.** FR-11 and FR-23 require that a password change or reset\ninvalidates other sessions. Sessions are stateless JWTs (FR-10), so there's\nno session table.\n\n**Decision.** Store `iat` and a `checkedAt` timestamp in the token. In the\nNode `jwt` callback, if `now - checkedAt > 5 min`, select only\n`passwordChangedAt, status` for `token.id`. If `passwordChangedAt > iat` or\nthe status isn't ACTIVE, return `null` to invalidate the session. Otherwise\nupdate `checkedAt`. For FR-23's \"re-issue current session\", call `unstable_update()`\n(the v5 `update`) after the change, which mints a token with a fresh `iat`.\n\n**Consequences.** Revocation can lag by up to 5 minutes. The PRD explicitly\naccepts that window. Edge middleware can still let a revoked token through\nfor page navigation in that window. The server-side `requireUser()` call\nthen catches it, and that call is the real guard (NFR-7).\n\n_Cites: FR-10, FR-11, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-7_\n\n## ADR-5: Tokens with `node:crypto` only (randomBytes, SHA-256, timingSafeEqual)\n\n**Context.** FR-13 needs a single AuthToken module. NFR-1 requires stored\nhashes and constant-time comparison.\n\n**Decision.** `src/server/auth/tokens.ts` uses `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64url')`\nand `createHash('sha256')`. Look up by the hash (the DB index), then confirm\nwith `timingSafeEqual` on the hash buffers. No JWT, nanoid or other library.\n\n**Consequences.** Raw tokens exist only in the returned value and the\nemailed or logged link, never in the DB or audit metadata (FR-31). The\n\"invalidate older unused tokens\" step (FR-20, FR-26) is one `updateMany`\nthat sets `usedAt` in the same `$transaction` as the create.\n\n_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-20, FR-26, FR-31, NFR-1_\n\n## ADR-6: In-memory sliding-window rate limiter (a `Map` of timestamp arrays); no Redis\n\n**Context.** FR-7 (10 per IP per 5 min) and FR-19 (3 per email per hour, 10\nper IP per hour) explicitly ask for an exported in-memory limiter in\n`src/server/rate-limit.ts`. The PRD adds no infrastructure (NFR-11).\n\n**Decision.** One `rateLimit(key, limit, windowMs): { ok, retryAfterMs }`\nover a module-level `Map<string, number[]>`, pruning expired timestamps on\neach call. Namespace keys like `login:ip:<ip>` and `forgot:email:<email>`.\nTake the IP from the first entry of `x-forwarded-for`, using the same\nhelper `audit()` uses (FR-30). Call it from the Node side (authorize and\nroute handlers), not middleware, because Edge isolates don't share memory.\n\n**Consequences.** The ceiling is per process: limits reset on restart and\naren't shared across instances. Mark the code with a\n`ponytail: per-process limiter, swap the Map for Redis/Postgres if\nhorizontally scaled` comment. With Credentials, a RATE_LIMITED outcome has\nto go through Auth.js's `CredentialsSignin` subclass `code` to reach the\nlogin form (FR-5, FR-7).\n\n_Cites: FR-7, FR-19, FR-30, NFR-8, NFR-11_\n\n## ADR-7: Validation with the existing zod + react-hook-form; one policy schema shared by client and server\n\n**Context.** FR-17 defines the policy. FR-18 needs a live checklist.\nFR-16, FR-21 and FR-23 must enforce the policy server-side.\n\n**Decision.** `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports the zod schema plus a\npure `passwordChecks(pw, email)` that returns per-rule booleans. The\nchecklist UI and the zod `superRefine` both use that function. The\ncommon-password check needs the file at\n`src/server/auth/common-passwords.txt`, so it runs only on the server: read\nthe file once into a `Set` with `fs` in a server-only module. The client\nchecklist shows the length, letter, digit and not-email rules live, and the\ncommon-password rule is reported from the server response.\n\n**Consequences.** The 1,000-word list never ships in the client bundle. The\nvalidator file has to stay importable from the client, so keep the `fs`\nread in a separate server-only file that adds the common-password refinement.\n\n_Cites: FR-17, FR-18, FR-16, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-5, NFR-8_\n\n## ADR-8: UI with MUI v9 only (Sign-in template patterns), documented through mui-mcp\n\n**Context.** NFR-6 bans non-MUI UI libraries and requires every component to\nbe looked up through mui-mcp. FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24 and FR-32 add auth\npages and top-bar changes.\n\n**Decision.** Use the installed `@mui/material` / `@mui/icons-material` 9.5:\nCard, TextField, Button, InputAdornment + IconButton (Visibility icons),\nAlert, List/ListItemIcon for the checklist, and Menu/Avatar/Chip in the top\nbar. Use the existing `useToast` for toasts (FR-21, FR-23), not notistack or\nsonner. Auth pages live in a `src/app/(auth)` route group outside the\n`(app)` shell. The mui-mcp server was reachable from this session\n(`useMuiDocs` / `fetchDocs` tools were listed), so the NFR-6 blocker doesn't\nstand at architect time. Dev must still check it itself.\n\n**Consequences.** No new UI dependency. `aria-live=\"polite\"` on the form-level\nAlert and focus-first-invalid (via react-hook-form `setFocus`) meet NFR-5.\n\n_Cites: FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24, FR-32, FR-33, NFR-5, NFR-6_\n\n## ADR-9: Schema reconciled additively with one Prisma migration\n\n**Context.** FR-1 and the intake blockers list the divergences: `ip` vs\n`ipAddress`, the extra `ARCHIVED` value, different AuditLog indexes. NFR-9\nsays never rename.\n\n**Decision.** Keep `AuditLog.ip`, keep `UserStatus.ARCHIVED` and keep the\nexisting extra indexes. Add only\n`@@index([entityType, entityId])` and `@@index([actorId, createdAt])` to\nAuditLog, plus any User auth fields the diff finds missing\n(failedLoginCount, lockedUntil, lastLoginAt, passwordChangedAt). Apply them\nin one `prisma migrate dev --name auth_reconcile` migration. FR-4's \"status\n!= ACTIVE\" check already rejects ARCHIVED.\n\n**Consequences.** The migration has no destructive steps. It adds indexes and\nnullable or defaulted columns only.\n\n_Cites: FR-1, FR-2, FR-4, FR-6, NFR-9_\n\n## ADR-10: Tests with the existing Vitest + Playwright; no new test tooling\n\n**Decision.** Run unit and integration tests (FR-36) in `vitest`, calling the\nlimiter, tokens, policy and callbackUrl modules as pure functions. Test the\nauthorize function and the service functions against the test DB. Use\n`@playwright/test` (with the installed `@axe-core/playwright` for NFR-5) for\nsign-in/out, the failure message and forgot→reset. The reset link comes from\nthe logged `sendAuthEmail` output (FR-22).\n\n_Cites: FR-22, FR-36, NFR-5, NFR-10_\ntotal 176\ndrwxr-xr-x  8 ikramaansari  staff    256 Nov  4 11:40 .\ndrwxr-xr-x  7 ikramaansari  staff    224 Oct 16 20:20 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 ikramaansari  staff  13580 Nov  4 11:40 api-contracts.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 ikramaansari  staff   1152 Nov  4 11:40 handoff-to-dev-planning.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 ikramaansari  staff  17106 Nov  4 11:35 requirements-analysis-notes.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 ikramaansari  staff  30533 Nov  4 11:39 system-design.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 ikramaansari  staff  11861 Nov  4 11:35 tech-stack-decisions.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 ikramaansari  staff    340 Nov  4 11:40 threat-model-notes.json\n?? artifacts/","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43ba1665-ff77-440a-aa0d-eea7f83f9b17","uuid":"57e67fa5-a698-42cc-8bed-ae7e1f6fffa2","timestamp":"2026-11-04T06:59:16.741Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions & RBAC (SCH-001)\n\nTicket: abcd-1 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-04T00:00:00Z · Status: complete\n\nBase stack (`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%, confirmed by\n`package.json`): Next.js 14.2 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict,\nPrisma 5.20 / PostgreSQL, MUI v9 + emotion, zod 3, react-hook-form,\ndate-fns-tz. The auth dependencies are already installed: `next-auth ^5.0.0-beta.25`\nand `bcrypt ^6`. **This PRD adds no new runtime dependency.** The ADRs below\nonly cover the implementation choices this PRD's requirements force.\n\nBrownfield facts checked in the workspace (they correct intake in places):\n- `requireRole()` is sync and returns `{id, role}`. It has **50 call sites in\n  43 files**, not ~94. Every caller is already inside an `async` server page,\n  layout or route handler.\n- `src/server/http.ts` `ApiErrorCode` already includes `UNAUTHENTICATED` 401,\n  `FORBIDDEN` 403, `RATE_LIMITED` 429 and `INVALID_TOKEN` 410, so no envelope\n  changes are needed.\n- `AuditLog.ip String?` exists. `audit()` has no `request` param.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Auth.js v5 with a split config (edge-safe `auth.config.ts` + Node `auth.ts`)\n\n**Context.** FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12 and FR-34 need credential sign-in, JWT\nsessions and a `middleware.ts` gate. In Next 14, middleware runs on the Edge\nruntime, where `bcrypt` (a native addon) and Prisma's Node client can't load.\n\n**Decision.** Use the documented Auth.js v5 split:\n- `src/server/auth/auth.config.ts` (edge-safe): `session: { strategy: 'jwt', maxAge: 8h, updateAge }`,\n  `pages: { signIn: '/login' }`, the `authorized`/`jwt`/`session` shape\n  callbacks, and no providers that touch the DB.\n- `src/server/auth/auth.ts` (Node): `NextAuth({ ...authConfig, providers: [Credentials({ authorize })] })`,\n  exporting `{ handlers, auth, signIn, signOut }`. It also holds the\n  DB-touching `jwt` callback logic (FR-11).\n- `middleware.ts` builds `NextAuth(authConfig).auth`. It only decodes the JWT.\n- `src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts` re-exports `handlers` (FR-34).\n\nDon't use a Prisma adapter. JWT sessions with Credentials don't need one, and\nthe adapter would require Account/Session/VerificationToken models the PRD\ndoesn't define.\n\n**Consequences.** Middleware can't check `passwordChangedAt` because it has no\nDB access. That check lives in the Node-side `jwt` callback (ADR-4), which\nruns whenever `auth()` is called from server code. Because next-auth is a\nbeta, pin the installed version exactly (drop the `^`) so a beta bump can't\nchange callback signatures mid-PRD.\n\n_Cites: FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12, FR-34, NFR-2_\n\n## ADR-2: `requireRole()` / `requireUser()` become async; update all 50 call sites with `await`\n\n**Context.** FR-28 and FR-29 replace `PLACEHOLDER_USER` with the real session.\nAuth.js `auth()` is async. Intake flagged this as a blocker. Options:\n(a) make the helpers async and add `await` at each call site;\n(b) keep them sync by reading identity from headers set by middleware;\n(c) use React `cache()` plus a sync wrapper.\n\n**Decision.** (a). `getCurrentUser()`, `requireUser()` and `requireRole(...roles)`\nbecome `async` with the same arguments and the same `{id, role, ...}` return\nshape, so the signature stays compatible (FR-29). Each of the 50 call sites\nchanges from `requireRole(...)` to `await requireRole(...)`. That's a\nmechanical one-token edit, and every caller is already async. Wrap\n`getCurrentUser` in React `cache()` so a page plus its layout share one\nsession decode per request.\n\nOption (b) was rejected: identity read from headers is a spoofing risk\n(NFR-7), and it skips the FR-11 revocation check. Option (c) can't make an\nasync read sync.\n\n**Consequences.** `tsc` catches every missed `await`, because a `Promise` has\nno `.role` or `.id`. Bare `requireRole('ADMIN');` statements don't fail\ntypecheck, though, and a forgotten `await` there silently does nothing.\nEnable `@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises` for `src/**`, or have\ndev-planning grep for un-awaited calls. Redirect-vs-throw: in server pages,\nuse `redirect('/login')` / `redirect('/forbidden')` from `next/navigation`;\nin route handlers, throw `AppError` (UNAUTHENTICATED / FORBIDDEN), which\n`withHandler` already maps. Implement this as one helper that the page and\nAPI paths both call, not two copies. `course-access.ts` and\n`student-access.ts` keep taking the resolved user object and aren't modified\n(NFR-9).\n\n_Cites: FR-28, FR-29, NFR-7, NFR-8, NFR-9_\n\n## ADR-3: Password hashing with the existing `bcrypt` at cost 12, plus a dummy compare for unknown users\n\n**Context.** FR-4 sets cost 12. NFR-3 requires constant-ish timing when the\nuser doesn't exist. NFR-4 requires sign-in in under 1 s at p95.\n\n**Decision.** Use `bcrypt.hash(pw, 12)` / `bcrypt.compare`. Precompute one\nmodule-level dummy hash at cost 12 and compare against it when the user is\nmissing, disabled or locked, so every path pays one bcrypt compare. Don't use\nargon2 (it's a new native dependency and the PRD specifies bcrypt).\n\n**Consequences.** One cost-12 compare takes about 250 ms on modern hardware,\nwhich leaves room under the 1 s budget for the user lookup, the\nfailedLoginCount update and the audit insert. Never call bcrypt twice on one\nsign-in path. bcrypt runs only in Node code, never in middleware (ADR-1).\n\n_Cites: FR-4, FR-6, NFR-3, NFR-4_\n\n## ADR-4: Session revocation via a `passwordChangedAt` check in the jwt callback, throttled to every 5 minutes\n\n**Context.** FR-11 and FR-23 require that a password change or reset\ninvalidates other sessions. Sessions are stateless JWTs (FR-10), so there's\nno session table.\n\n**Decision.** Store `iat` and a `checkedAt` timestamp in the token. In the\nNode `jwt` callback, if `now - checkedAt > 5 min`, select only\n`passwordChangedAt, status` for `token.id`. If `passwordChangedAt > iat` or\nthe status isn't ACTIVE, return `null` to invalidate the session. Otherwise\nupdate `checkedAt`. For FR-23's \"re-issue current session\", call `unstable_update()`\n(the v5 `update`) after the change, which mints a token with a fresh `iat`.\n\n**Consequences.** Revocation can lag by up to 5 minutes. The PRD explicitly\naccepts that window. Edge middleware can still let a revoked token through\nfor page navigation in that window. The server-side `requireUser()` call\nthen catches it, and that call is the real guard (NFR-7).\n\n_Cites: FR-10, FR-11, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-7_\n\n## ADR-5: Tokens with `node:crypto` only (randomBytes, SHA-256, timingSafeEqual)\n\n**Context.** FR-13 needs a single AuthToken module. NFR-1 requires stored\nhashes and constant-time comparison.\n\n**Decision.** `src/server/auth/tokens.ts` uses `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64url')`\nand `createHash('sha256')`. Look up by the hash (the DB index), then confirm\nwith `timingSafeEqual` on the hash buffers. No JWT, nanoid or other library.\n\n**Consequences.** Raw tokens exist only in the returned value and the\nemailed or logged link, never in the DB or audit metadata (FR-31). The\n\"invalidate older unused tokens\" step (FR-20, FR-26) is one `updateMany`\nthat sets `usedAt` in the same `$transaction` as the create.\n\n_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-20, FR-26, FR-31, NFR-1_\n\n## ADR-6: In-memory sliding-window rate limiter (a `Map` of timestamp arrays); no Redis\n\n**Context.** FR-7 (10 per IP per 5 min) and FR-19 (3 per email per hour, 10\nper IP per hour) explicitly ask for an exported in-memory limiter in\n`src/server/rate-limit.ts`. The PRD adds no infrastructure (NFR-11).\n\n**Decision.** One `rateLimit(key, limit, windowMs): { ok, retryAfterMs }`\nover a module-level `Map<string, number[]>`, pruning expired timestamps on\neach call. Namespace keys like `login:ip:<ip>` and `forgot:email:<email>`.\nTake the IP from the first entry of `x-forwarded-for`, using the same\nhelper `audit()` uses (FR-30). Call it from the Node side (authorize and\nroute handlers), not middleware, because Edge isolates don't share memory.\n\n**Consequences.** The ceiling is per process: limits reset on restart and\naren't shared across instances. Mark the code with a\n`ponytail: per-process limiter, swap the Map for Redis/Postgres if\nhorizontally scaled` comment. With Credentials, a RATE_LIMITED outcome has\nto go through Auth.js's `CredentialsSignin` subclass `code` to reach the\nlogin form (FR-5, FR-7).\n\n_Cites: FR-7, FR-19, FR-30, NFR-8, NFR-11_\n\n## ADR-7: Validation with the existing zod + react-hook-form; one policy schema shared by client and server\n\n**Context.** FR-17 defines the policy. FR-18 needs a live checklist.\nFR-16, FR-21 and FR-23 must enforce the policy server-side.\n\n**Decision.** `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports the zod schema plus a\npure `passwordChecks(pw, email)` that returns per-rule booleans. The\nchecklist UI and the zod