` over `status: 'ACTIVE'`, with `courseId in [otherCourseIds]` and `studentId in` the new course's active student ids. Both are covered by the existing `@@index([studentId, status])`.\n- **Availability** (FR-36): load all slots in the course's academic year in one query, and the new course's ACTIVE student ids plus the ACTIVE enrollments of those students in one query. Then run the same pure rule functions for each of the up-to-84 cells (7 × 12) in memory. This is a fixed number of queries, not one per cell.\n- The rule functions are pure (`(candidate, others, overlapCounts) => string[]`), so NFR-11 unit tests need no database.\n- **Editor and personal grids** (FR-35, FR-38): one `findMany` per view with `include` (course → subject, teacher; room; period). There are no N+1 queries.\n\n**Consequences.** No `$queryRaw`, so everything stays type-safe under strict TS (NFR-10).\nCeiling: availability does its work in memory over the year's slots, roughly a few thousand rows\nfor a single school (NFR-16), which fits the target comfortably.\n`// ponytail: in-memory clash map over the year's slots; move to SQL aggregation if slot count reaches ~50k.`\n\n## ADR-5: The drag-and-drop grid uses `@dnd-kit/core` with pessimistic updates; keyboard users get an MUI Dialog path\n\n**Context.** FR-16–FR-23 describe the editor grid. FR-20 requires that a rejected drop snaps\nback. FR-19 tints clashing cells while dragging, and NFR-4 says colour must never be the only signal.\nFR-22 and NFR-4 require a keyboard alternative. NFR-5 and NFR-6 allow MUI-only visuals.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `DndContext` with `PointerSensor` only (activation distance about 5px, so a click still opens the FR-21 room Popover). Cells use `useDroppable`. BREAK/LUNCH rows and non-teaching days set `disabled: true` (FR-16). Course cards and placed slots use `useDraggable`. The drag preview is a `DragOverlay` holding an MUI `Paper` (FR-23).\n- **Pessimistic updates.** The grid changes only after the POST or PATCH succeeds. On a 409, nothing moves, so \"snap back\" (FR-20) needs no rollback code. The toast comes from `useToast().showToast({ message, severity: 'error' })` with `details.clashes` joined into the message.\n- The clash map from `GET /api/timetable/availability` is fetched once when a drag starts, cached per courseId while the editor is open, and dropped after any mutation. Cells in the map get a red tint, an ErrorOutline icon and an MUI `Tooltip` listing the messages (FR-19, NFR-4).\n- Keyboard: cells are `tabIndex=0`. Enter on an empty cell opens an MUI `Dialog` listing the section's courses (FR-22). Delete on a focused slot opens the existing `ConfirmDialog` (FR-18). dnd-kit's `KeyboardSensor` is **not** used, because the Dialog path is the required alternative and it avoids the cost of making sensor announcements accessible.\n- Data fetching uses plain `fetch` plus `useState`, as elsewhere in the repo. No SWR or React Query is added.\n\n**Consequences.** Each drop costs one round-trip of latency (no optimistic UI). That is acceptable\nfor an admin tool, and it removes a whole class of state-desync bugs. Every component must still\nbe looked up through mui-mcp before use (NFR-5). The mui-mcp server is connected in this\nenvironment, so the intake blocker is cleared for now, but dev must still call it.\n\n## ADR-6: Print layout uses native `@media print` and `window.print()`, with no PDF or print library\n\n**Context.** FR-29 requires a print-friendly A4 landscape page containing only the grid, the entity\nname and the academic year. NFR-16 requires print to work in the last two versions of Chrome, Edge,\nFirefox and Safari. NFR-6 bans extra UI libraries.\n\n**Decision.** The Print button calls `window.print()`. An MUI `GlobalStyles` block, rendered with\nthe grid, contains `@page { size: A4 landscape; margin: 10mm }`. Under `@media print`, an `sx` rule\nhides the app shell (nav drawer, app bar, sidebar, view selectors and buttons), and a print-only\nheading shows the entity name and academic year. Cells use `break-inside: avoid`. Cell background\ncolours need `print-color-adjust: exact`. Course code and teacher text carry the meaning anyway,\nso colour is not required (NFR-4).\n\n**Consequences.** No dependency is added. Firefox and Safari ignore some `@page` size hints,\nwhich is acceptable because the user picks the orientation in the print dialog. The Playwright\ncheck uses `page.emulateMedia({ media: 'print' })` and asserts that the shell is hidden.\n\n## ADR-7: Testing stays on the existing Vitest and Playwright setup\n\n**Context.** NFR-11 (unit tests), NFR-12 (concurrency integration test), NFR-13 (e2e test) and NFR-10 (quality gate).\n\n**Decision.**\n- `tests/unit/timetable-clash.test.ts`: the pure rule functions from ADR-4, period overlap validation (FR-6) and `getCurrentSlot`/Now-Next with injected `now` and `tz` (ADR-2).\n- `tests/integration/timetable-concurrency.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node`, gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (ADR-3).\n- `tests/e2e/timetable.spec.ts`: Playwright tests for dragging a course into a cell, a clash toast on a conflicting drop, and the student Today view, all against the FR-41–FR-44 seed. dnd-kit's PointerSensor works with Playwright `mouse.down/move/up` when there are intermediate `move` steps. `locator.dragTo` alone may not cross the activation distance.\n\n**Consequences.** No new test tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed fixtures (FR-44) being idempotent.\n\n---\n\n## Downstream notes (no stack impact; for `arch-design-system` to decide)\n\n- FR-21 free rooms: no stack choice is needed. Either intake's suggested `GET /api/timetable/free-rooms` or the availability payload works with ADR-4's query pattern. One query (rooms not archived, minus `roomId`s used at that day and period) is enough.\n- FR-7 slot count: intake's default (`GET /api/school-days` returns `slotCount` per day) is one `timetableSlot.groupBy({ by: ['dayOfWeek'] })`, which works fine with this stack.\n- FR-40 audit: `audit()` from `src/server/audit.ts` should be called inside the same transaction client where its signature allows. That is for design to confirm.\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014edccA3x5aL19x6U2X1BLE","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7632dfff-c76a-42bd-90d4-6365368b057b","uuid":"78d1c695-f8fd-4769-bb92-b696f00f3a5f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:15:24.829Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-06 Bell Schedule & Timetable (SCH-006)\n\nTicket: SH-6 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-05T05:40:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and\n`package.json` confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict,\nPrisma 5.20 on PostgreSQL, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons, x-date-pickers, x-data-grid) with emotion,\nzod 3.23.8, date-fns 3.6.0 / date-fns-tz 3.2.0, Vitest 2.1.1 and Playwright 1.47.2.\nThe stack fits every FR/NFR. **This PRD adds exactly one runtime dependency, `@dnd-kit/core`.**\nThe ADRs below record only the choices the requirements force.\n\n**Workspace facts I checked** (they confirm intake or add detail to it):\n\n- `@dnd-kit/core` is not installed. The current npm release is `6.3.1`, with peer deps `react >=16.8` and `react-dom >=16.8`, so it is compatible with React 18.3.1.\n- No SWR, React Query or TanStack package is installed. Client data fetching in the codebase is plain `fetch` plus local state.\n- `prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => …)` is already used (sections-service, courses-service). No code uses `isolationLevel` or handles `P2034` yet, so this PRD sets the precedent.\n- `src/lib/dates.ts` already exports `formatInSchoolTz(date, pattern, timezone)`, which wraps `formatInTimeZone`.\n- `.eslintrc` `no-restricted-imports` bans `@fullcalendar/*`, `react-datepicker`, `notistack`, `sonner`, `@radix-ui/*` and others. `@dnd-kit/*` is not banned, so the lint rule needs no change.\n- Vitest runs `tests/unit/**` and `tests/integration/**`. Integration tests are gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (precedent: `tests/integration/email-dispatch.test.ts`, which also tests a concurrent-race scenario).\n- No `@media print` or `GlobalStyles` print rule exists yet.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the declared stack; add `@dnd-kit/core@6.3.1` (exact pin) and nothing else\n\n**Context.** FR-23 requires drag-and-drop to use `@dnd-kit/core` only. NFR-6 allows that one\nnon-visual dependency, plus `@dnd-kit/sortable` only if needed. FR-5 period reorder could\njustify sortable, but intake's default for FR-5 is up/down buttons.\n\n**Decision.** Add `\"@dnd-kit/core\": \"6.3.1\"` with no caret, matching the repo's exact-pin\nconvention (`\"@mui/material\": \"9.5.0\"`). Do **not** add `@dnd-kit/sortable`,\n`@dnd-kit/utilities` or `@dnd-kit/modifiers`. Period reorder (FR-5, FR-33 `POST /api/periods/reorder`)\nuses MUI `IconButton`s with ArrowUpward and ArrowDownward icons.\n\n**Consequences.** One new lockfile entry. Leaving out sortable keeps the reorder logic\nserver-side and simple. The grid uses only `DndContext`, `useDraggable`, `useDroppable`,\n`DragOverlay` and `PointerSensor`. The ESLint ban list stays as it is.\n\n## ADR-2: Store and compare times as zero-padded `'HH:MM'` strings; derive \"now\" with the existing date-fns-tz helper\n\n**Context.** FR-1 stores `startTime` and `endTime` as `'HH:MM'` strings in the school timezone.\nFR-6 needs overlap and end-after-start checks. FR-31 and FR-45 need the current period and\ntoday's ISO weekday in `SchoolSettings.timezone`. NFR-11 asks for tests that cover multiple timezones.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Zod (FR-39) enforces `/^([01]\\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\\d$/`. Zero-padded 24-hour strings sort and compare correctly as plain strings, so `a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end` is the overlap test (FR-6). No parsing to `Date` is needed.\n- \"Now\" is `formatInSchoolTz(now, 'HH:mm', tz)` and the ISO weekday is `Number(formatInSchoolTz(now, 'i', tz))` (1=Mon…7=Sun, matching FR-2). The `period.startTime <= nowHHMM < period.endTime` comparison is again a string comparison (FR-31, FR-26 Now/Next, FR-24 highlight).\n- `getScheduleForDate` (FR-30) also derives the weekday with `'i'` in the school timezone.\n- MUI X `TimeField` (FR-5) binds a `Date` value. The form converts with date-fns `format(d, 'HH:mm')` and `parse(s, 'HH:mm', ref)`. Both functions are already installed, and the field's `Date` is wall-clock only.\n\n**Consequences.** No new date library and no timezone arithmetic on stored times. Unit tests\npass a fixed `now` and several `tz` values (e.g. `UTC`, `America/Los_Angeles`, `Asia/Kolkata`) into\npure functions. Ceiling: periods cannot cross midnight. That is acceptable for a school day,\nand the endTime-after-startTime check already rejects them.\n\n## ADR-3: Clash check and write in one SERIALIZABLE interactive transaction with bounded retry on `P2034`\n\n**Context.** NFR-1 requires clash validation and the insert or update to run in one SERIALIZABLE\ntransaction, with retry, so that two concurrent conflicting inserts yield exactly one success.\nNFR-12 needs an integration test for this. FR-37 requires that a clash saves nothing and returns 409.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Use `prisma.$transaction(fn, { isolationLevel: Prisma.TransactionIsolationLevel.Serializable })`, which Prisma 5.20 supports natively on PostgreSQL.\n- Retry the whole transaction up to 3 attempts when the error is `PrismaClientKnownRequestError` with `code === 'P2034'` (write conflict or deadlock). After the last attempt, rethrow so `withHandler` maps it to a 500. The retry loop is a small local function inside `src/server/timetable/timetable-service.ts`. Do not add a shared util until a second caller exists (NFR-9).\n- Clashes found inside the transaction throw `AppError('CONFLICT', msg, { clashes })` from `src/server/http.ts`. The throw rolls the transaction back, so nothing is saved (FR-37, NFR-8).\n- The FR-3 `@@unique([courseId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` stays as a last line of defence. Map `P2002` to 409 as well.\n- Teaching-day switch-off (FR-7) and period reorder (FR-5) also use one transaction, at the default isolation level. Reorder needs a two-phase write because `Period.order @unique` would collide mid-update: first set each order to `-(i+1)`, then to `i+1`.\n\n**Consequences.** Clash rules (FR-9–FR-13) must run their reads through `tx`, not the global\n`prisma`, or SERIALIZABLE protects nothing. `validateSlot` therefore takes a client parameter\nthat defaults to `prisma`, so FR-36 availability can reuse the same logic outside a write.\nThe NFR-12 test follows the `email-dispatch.test.ts` pattern: `describe.runIf(TEST_DATABASE_URL)`,\nthen `Promise.allSettled` of two conflicting creates, asserting exactly one is fulfilled.\n\n## ADR-4: Clash detection loads candidate slots with set-based Prisma queries and checks them in memory (no raw SQL)\n\n**Context.** FR-9–FR-14 define four clash rules plus the non-class period and non-teaching day\nrule, and `validateSlot` must return all of them. FR-36 availability must cover every\n(day, period) cell for one course in under 300 ms (NFR-3). The section week must load in under\n400 ms (NFR-2). The FR-3 index `@@index([academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` already exists for this.\n\n**Decision.**\n- **validateSlot** (single cell): one `findMany` of the other slots at `(academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId)`, excluding the moving slot's id on PATCH (FR-14). The query includes `course { teacherId, coTeacherIds, sectionId, code, subject, section }`, `room` and `period`. The teacher, room and section rules (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11) are array checks in TypeScript. Student overlap (FR-12) is one `enrollment.groupBy` / `count` over `status: 'ACTIVE'`, with `courseId in [otherCourseIds]` and `studentId in` the new course's active student ids. Both are covered by the existing `@@index([studentId, status])`.\n- **Availability** (FR-36): load all slots in the course's academic year in one query, and the new course's ACTIVE student ids plus the ACTIVE enrollments of those students in one query. Then run the same pure rule functions for each of the up-to-84 cells (7 × 12) in memory. This is a fixed number of queries, not one per cell.\n- The rule functions are pure (`(candidate, others, overlapCounts) => string[]`), so NFR-11 unit tests need no database.\n- **Editor and personal grids** (FR-35, FR-38): one `findMany` per view with `include` (course → subject, teacher; room; period). There are no N+1 queries.\n\n**Consequences.** No `$queryRaw`, so everything stays type-safe under strict TS (NFR-10).\nCeiling: availability does its work in memory over the year's slots, roughly a few thousand rows\nfor a single school (NFR-16), which fits the target comfortably.\n`// ponytail: in-memory clash map over the year's slots; move to SQL aggregation if slot count reaches ~50k.`\n\n## ADR-5: The drag-and-drop grid uses `@dnd-kit/core` with pessimistic updates; keyboard users get an MUI Dialog path\n\n**Context.** FR-16–FR-23 describe the editor grid. FR-20 requires that a rejected drop snaps\nback. FR-19 tints clashing cells while dragging, and NFR-4 says colour must never be the only signal.\nFR-22 and NFR-4 require a keyboard alternative. NFR-5 and NFR-6 allow MUI-only visuals.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `DndContext` with `PointerSensor` only (activation distance about 5px, so a click still opens the FR-21 room Popover). Cells use `useDroppable`. BREAK/LUNCH rows and non-teaching days set `disabled: true` (FR-16). Course cards and placed slots use `useDraggable`. The drag preview is a `DragOverlay` holding an MUI `Paper` (FR-23).\n- **Pessimistic updates.** The grid changes only after the POST or PATCH succeeds. On a 409, nothing moves, so \"snap back\" (FR-20) needs no rollback code. The toast comes from `useToast().showToast({ message, severity: 'error' })` with `details.clashes` joined into the message.\n- The clash map from `GET /api/timetable/availability` is fetched once when a drag starts, cached per courseId while the editor is open, and dropped after any mutation. Cells in the map get a red tint, an ErrorOutline icon and an MUI `Tooltip` listing the messages (FR-19, NFR-4).\n- Keyboard: cells are `tabIndex=0`. Enter on an empty cell opens an MUI `Dialog` listing the section's courses (FR-22). Delete on a focused slot opens the existing `ConfirmDialog` (FR-18). dnd-kit's `KeyboardSensor` is **not** used, because the Dialog path is the required alternative and it avoids the cost of making sensor announcements accessible.\n- Data fetching uses plain `fetch` plus `useState`, as elsewhere in the repo. No SWR or React Query is added.\n\n**Consequences.** Each drop costs one round-trip of latency (no optimistic UI). That is acceptable\nfor an admin tool, and it removes a whole class of state-desync bugs. Every component must still\nbe looked up through mui-mcp before use (NFR-5). The mui-mcp server is connected in this\nenvironment, so the intake blocker is cleared for now, but dev must still call it.\n\n## ADR-6: Print layout uses native `@media print` and `window.print()`, with no PDF or print library\n\n**Context.** FR-29 requires a print-friendly A4 landscape page containing only the grid, the entity\nname and the academic year. NFR-16 requires print to work in the last two versions of Chrome, Edge,\nFirefox and Safari. NFR-6 bans extra UI libraries.\n\n**Decision.** The Print button calls `window.print()`. An MUI `GlobalStyles` block, rendered with\nthe grid, contains `@page { size: A4 landscape; margin: 10mm }`. Under `@media print`, an `sx` rule\nhides the app shell (nav drawer, app bar, sidebar, view selectors and buttons), and a print-only\nheading shows the entity name and academic year. Cells use `break-inside: avoid`. Cell background\ncolours need `print-color-adjust: exact`. Course code and teacher text carry the meaning anyway,\nso colour is not required (NFR-4).\n\n**Consequences.** No dependency is added. Firefox and Safari ignore some `@page` size hints,\nwhich is acceptable because the user picks the orientation in the print dialog. The Playwright\ncheck uses `page.emulateMedia({ media: 'print' })` and asserts that the shell is hidden.\n\n## ADR-7: Testing stays on the existing Vitest and Playwright setup\n\n**Context.** NFR-11 (unit tests), NFR-12 (concurrency integration test), NFR-13 (e2e test) and NFR-10 (quality gate).\n\n**Decision.**\n- `tests/unit/timetable-clash.test.ts`: the pure rule functions from ADR-4, period overlap validation (FR-6) and `getCurrentSlot`/Now-Next with injected `now` and `tz` (ADR-2).\n- `tests/integration/timetable-concurrency.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node`, gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (ADR-3).\n- `tests/e2e/timetable.spec.ts`: Playwright tests for dragging a course into a cell, a clash toast on a conflicting drop, and the student Today view, all against the FR-41–FR-44 seed. dnd-kit's PointerSensor works with Playwright `mouse.down/move/up` when there are intermediate `move` steps. `locator.dragTo` alone may not cross the activation distance.\n\n**Consequences.** No new test tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed fixtures (FR-44) being idempotent.\n\n---\n\n## Downstream notes (no stack impact; for `arch-design-system` to decide)\n\n- FR-21 free rooms: no stack choice is needed. Either intake's suggested `GET /api/timetable/free-rooms` or the availability payload works with ADR-4's query pattern. One query (rooms not archived, minus `roomId`s used at that day and period) is enough.\n- FR-7 slot count: intake's default (`GET /api/school-days` returns `slotCount` per day) is one `timetableSlot.groupBy({ by: ['dayOfWeek'] })`, which works fine with this stack.\n- FR-40 audit: `audit()` from `src/server/audit.ts` should be called inside the same transaction client where its signature allows. That is for design to confirm.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":280,"newStart":1,"newLines":145,"lines":["-# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-04 Notifications & Email Infrastructure (SCH-004)","+# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-06 Bell Schedule & Timetable (SCH-006)"," ","-Ticket: SH-4 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-04T12:10:00Z · Status: complete","+Ticket: SH-6 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-05T05:40:00Z · Status: complete"," ","-**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports confidence 100%, and `package.json`","-confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3, TypeScript 5.5 strict,","-Prisma 5.20 / PostgreSQL, MUI 9.5 (material, icons, x-data-grid) + emotion,","-zod 3.23, date-fns 3.6 / date-fns-tz, Vitest 2.1 and Playwright 1.47.","-**This PRD adds no new runtime or dev dependency.** Everything it needs is","-already installed: `nodemailer ^6.9.14` + `@types/nodemailer`,","-`@react-email/components 0.0.25`, `@react-email/render 1.0.1` and `tsx 4.16`.","-The intake stack fits every FR/NFR, so the ADRs below record only the","-implementation choices the requirements force.","+**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and","+`package.json` confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict,","+Prisma 5.20 on PostgreSQL, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons, x-date-pickers, x-data-grid) with emotion,","+zod 3.23.8, date-fns 3.6.0 / date-fns-tz 3.2.0, Vitest 2.1.1 and Playwright 1.47.2.","+The stack fits every FR/NFR. **This PRD adds exactly one runtime dependency, `@dnd-kit/core`.**","+The ADRs below record only the choices the requirements force."," ","-**Brownfield facts checked in the workspace.** These confirm or sharpen intake:","-- `prisma/schema.prisma` declares `EmailStatus`, `Notification` (with","-  `@@index([userId, readAt, createdAt])`), `NotificationPreference` (composite","-  `@@id([userId, type])`) and `EmailOutbox` (with `@@index([status, nextAttemptAt])`, no FK","-  to User, and `updatedAt` documented as the SENDING timestamp). The 4 existing","-  migrations don't create these tables.","-- `@react-email/render@1.0.1`'s `render(el, { plainText?: true })` returns a","-  **`Promise<string>`**. It's async in 1.x, so every render call must be awaited.","-- `src/lib/env.ts` already validates `EMAIL_TRANSPORT` (`smtp|console`, default","-  `console`), `SMTP_*`, `EMAIL_FROM`, an optional `CRON_SECRET` and `APP_URL`.","-- `src/server/http.ts` already provides `withHandler`, `ok`, `paginated`,","-  `parsePagination`, `parseBody`, `parseQuery` and `AppError`. `src/lib/logger.ts`","-  provides `logger`. `src/lib/dates.ts` provides `formatInSchoolTz`. `src/server/db.ts`","-  exports the `prisma` singleton.","-- The existing `src/components/DataTable` wraps MUI X DataGrid with","-  URL-driven server pagination. `src/components/Toast/useToast.ts` exists.","-- Vitest `include` is `tests/unit/**` only, with `environment: 'jsdom'`.","-  There's no integration test folder yet.","+**Workspace facts I checked** (they confirm intake or add detail to it):"," ","+- `@dnd-kit/core` is not installed. The current npm release is `6.3.1`, with peer deps `react >=16.8` and `react-dom >=16.8`, so it is compatible with React 18.3.1.","+- No SWR, React Query or TanStack package is installed. Client data fetching in the codebase is plain `fetch` plus local state.","+- `prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => …)` is already used (sections-service, courses-service). No code uses `isolationLevel` or handles `P2034` yet, so this PRD sets the precedent.","+- `src/lib/dates.ts` already exports `formatInSchoolTz(date, pattern, timezone)`, which wraps `formatInTimeZone`.","+- `.eslintrc` `no-restricted-imports` bans `@fullcalendar/*`, `react-datepicker`, `notistack`, `sonner`, `@radix-ui/*` and others. `@dnd-kit/*` is not banned, so the lint rule needs no change.","+- Vitest runs `tests/unit/**` and `tests/integration/**`. Integration tests are gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (precedent: `tests/integration/email-dispatch.test.ts`, which also tests a concurrent-race scenario).","+- No `@media print` or `GlobalStyles` print rule exists yet.","+"," ---"," ","-## ADR-1: One additive Prisma migration for the existing PRD-04 models","+## ADR-1: Keep the declared stack; add `@dnd-kit/core@6.3.1` (exact pin) and nothing else"," ","-**Context.** FR-1 requires the tables. The models are already in","-`schema.prisma` but no migration creates them (an intake blocker). NFR-13","-says never rename and never re-scaffold. NFR-5 relies on `EmailOutbox.updatedAt`.","+**Context.** FR-23 requires drag-and-drop to use `@dnd-kit/core` only. NFR-6 allows that one","+non-visual dependency, plus `@dnd-kit/sortable` only if needed. FR-5 period reorder could","+justify sortable, but intake's default for FR-5 is up/down buttons."," ","-**Decision.** Run `prisma migrate dev --name notifications_email` without","-touching the schema. It generates the `EmailStatus` enum, the 3 tables, their","-indexes and the `Notification`/`NotificationPreference` → `User` cascade FKs.","-Keep the additive `updatedAt` columns exactly as they are declared.","+**Decision.** Add `\"@dnd-kit/core\": \"6.3.1\"` with no caret, matching the repo's exact-pin","+convention (`\"@mui/material\": \"9.5.0\"`). Do **not** add `@dnd-kit/sortable`,","+`@dnd-kit/utilities` or `@dnd-kit/modifiers`. Period reorder (FR-5, FR-33 `POST /api/periods/reorder`)","+uses MUI `IconButton`s with ArrowUpward and ArrowDownward icons."," ","-**Consequences.** This is one purely additive migration with nothing destructive. Prisma's","-`@updatedAt` sets the column on every update made through the Prisma client, **but not","-on `$executeRaw`/`$queryRaw` writes**. Any raw SQL that sets `status = 'SENDING'`","-must also set `\"updatedAt\" = now()` itself (see ADR-3).","+**Consequences.** One new lockfile entry. Leaving out sortable keeps the reorder logic","+server-side and simple. The grid uses only `DndContext`, `useDraggable`, `useDroppable`,","+`DragOverlay` and `PointerSensor`. The ESLint ban list stays as it is."," ","-_Cites: FR-1, NFR-5, NFR-13_","+## ADR-2: Store and compare times as zero-padded `'HH:MM'` strings; derive \"now\" with the existing date-fns-tz helper"," ","-## ADR-2: Registry as a `const` object with `satisfies`; `keyof typeof` drives notify()","+**Context.** FR-1 stores `startTime` and `endTime` as `'HH:MM'` strings in the school timezone.","+FR-6 needs overlap and end-after-start checks. FR-31 and FR-45 need the current period and","+today's ISO weekday in `SchoolSettings.timezone`. NFR-11 asks for tests that cover multiple timezones."," ","-**Context.** FR-2 through FR-5 need a typed registry that rejects unknown","-keys at compile time and that later PRDs can extend.","+**Decision.**","+- Zod (FR-39) enforces `/^([01]\\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\\d$/`. Zero-padded 24-hour strings sort and compare correctly as plain strings, so `a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end` is the overlap test (FR-6). No parsing to `Date` is needed.","+- \"Now\" is `formatInSchoolTz(now, 'HH:mm', tz)` and the ISO weekday is `Number(formatInSchoolTz(now, 'i', tz))` (1=Mon…7=Sun, matching FR-2). The `period.startTime <= nowHHMM < period.endTime` comparison is again a string comparison (FR-31, FR-26 Now/Next, FR-24 highlight).","+- `getScheduleForDate` (FR-30) also derives the weekday with `'i'` in the school timezone.","+- MUI X `TimeField` (FR-5) binds a `Date` value. The form converts with date-fns `format(d, 'HH:mm')` and `parse(s, 'HH:mm', ref)`. Both functions are already installed, and the field's `Date` is wall-clock only."," ","-**Decision.** In `src/server/notifications/types.ts`, define","-`export const NOTIFICATION_TYPES = { ... } as const satisfies Record<string, NotificationTypeDef>`","-and `export type NotificationType = keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES`. Express","-categories (FR-3) as a string-literal union, `NotificationCategory`, plus a","-`NOTIFICATION_CATEGORIES` tuple that the preferences page and its zod enum use.","-`NotificationTypeDef` adds `emailOnly?: true` / `hidden?: true` for the","-`account.*` types (FR-4, FR-28). Later PRDs extend the registry by adding","-keys to this one object. Don't build a runtime registration API (YAGNI).","+**Consequences.** No new date library and no timezone arithmetic on stored times. Unit tests","+pass a fixed `now` and several `tz` values (e.g. `UTC`, `America/Los_Angeles`, `Asia/Kolkata`) into","+pure functions. Ceiling: periods cannot cross midnight. That is acceptable for a school day,","+and the endTime-after-startTime check already rejects them."," ","-**Consequences.** `NotificationPreference.type` and `Notification.type` stay","-plain `String` columns, because the DB doesn't need an enum for a list that","-grows with every PRD. The zod validator for the preferences PUT (FR-38,","-FR-41) builds `z.enum(Object.keys(NOTIFICATION_TYPES) as [NotificationType, ...])`.","-It can't import server-only modules, so `types.ts` must stay free of server","-imports (no `prisma`, no `env`) to remain importable from","-`src/lib/validators/notifications.ts`.","+## ADR-3: Clash check and write in one SERIALIZABLE interactive transaction with bounded retry on `P2034`"," ","-_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-5, FR-28, FR-38, FR-41_","+**Context.** NFR-1 requires clash validation and the insert or update to run in one SERIALIZABLE","+transaction, with retry, so that two concurrent conflicting inserts yield exactly one success.","+NFR-12 needs an integration test for this. FR-37 requires that a clash saves nothing and returns 409."," ","-## ADR-3: Dispatcher claim through one raw-SQL `UPDATE … WHERE id IN (SELECT … FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED) RETURNING *`","-","-**Context.** FR-17 and NFR-4 require that concurrent dispatchers never send a row","-twice. NFR-5 requires recovering rows stuck in SENDING for more than 10 minutes. Prisma 5.20","-has no `SKIP LOCKED` in its query API.","-","-**Decision.** Each `dispatchEmails({ batchSize = 50 })` run:","-1. **Recover.** Run `prisma.emailOutbox.updateMany({ where: { status: 'SENDING', updatedAt: { lt: now - 10min } }, data: { status: 'PENDING' } })`.","-2. **Claim.** Use a single statement through `prisma.$queryRaw`:","-   ```sql","-   UPDATE \"EmailOutbox\" SET status = 'SENDING', \"updatedAt\" = now()","-   WHERE id IN (","-     SELECT id FROM \"EmailOutbox\"","-     WHERE status = 'PENDING' AND \"nextAttemptAt\" <= now()","-     ORDER BY \"nextAttemptAt\" LIMIT ${batchSize}","-     FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED)","-   RETURNING *;","-   ```","-   It runs as one autocommit statement, so the row lock is held only for the flip and is released","-   before any SMTP call. This avoids long transactions, and once a row is","-   SENDING no other dispatcher's `WHERE status='PENDING'` can match it.","-3. **Send each row**, then mark it SENT (`sentAt`) or reschedule it through Prisma `update` (ADR-4).","-","-**Consequences.** This is the only raw SQL in the PRD. Use tagged-template `$queryRaw`, never","-`$queryRawUnsafe`. Cast the enum literal (`'SENDING'::\"EmailStatus\"`) because","-Postgres won't implicitly cast text to an enum in a `SET`. A crash mid-send leaves the row SENDING,","-and step 1 recovers it after 10 minutes. That gives at-least-once delivery on crash, which","-the PRD accepts. The two-concurrent-callers and stuck-row behavior is covered","-by NFR-15 integration tests (ADR-9).","-","-_Cites: FR-17, NFR-4, NFR-5, NFR-15, AC-4_","-","-## ADR-4: Retry backoff as a pure lookup table","-","-**Context.** FR-18 sets backoff delays of 1/5/15/60/240 min for attempts 1–5","-and FAILED after the 5th failure. NFR-14 requires a unit test for it.","-","-**Decision.** `src/server/email/backoff.ts` exports `const BACKOFF_MIN = [1, 5, 15, 60, 240]`","-and a pure `nextRetry(attempts, now): { status: 'PENDING', nextAttemptAt } | { status: 'FAILED' }`,","-where `attempts` is the count after incrementing. No retry library.","-","-**Consequences.** This is a trivially unit-testable pure function. The admin","-\"Retry now\" action (FR-32) just resets `attempts=0, status=PENDING, nextAttemptAt=now()`.","-","-_Cites: FR-18, FR-32, NFR-14, AC-3_","-","-## ADR-5: Email transport: Nodemailer for SMTP, a logger-free console transport, a lazy singleton","-","-**Context.** FR-21 selects the transport by `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`. NFR-9 requires","-HTML + text parts and a `List-Unsubscribe` header. NFR-11 bans `console.log`","-except in the console transport.","-","-**Decision.** `src/server/email/transport.ts` exports `sendMail({ to, toName, subject, html, text })`.","-- `smtp`: `nodemailer.createTransport({ host, port, secure, auth })`, created","-  lazily once per process. It sends `{ from: EMAIL_FROM, html, text, headers: { 'List-Unsubscribe': '<${APP_URL}/account/notifications>' } }`.","-  Nodemailer builds `multipart/alternative` automatically when both `html` and `text` are set.","-  It throws a clear error at first send if `SMTP_HOST` or `EMAIL_FROM` is missing.","-- `console`: `console.log` of the subject, recipient and text body (the one sanctioned use, NFR-11).","-","-Use a plain `if` on the env value, with no transport interface or factory, because","-there are only two branches.","-","-**Consequences.** Dev and CI never need SMTP credentials. Tests can assert outbox rows","-and dispatcher state transitions without a mail server. The SMTP failure path","-(AC-3) is tested by stubbing `sendMail` with `vi.mock`.","-","-_Cites: FR-17, FR-21, NFR-9, NFR-11_","-","-## ADR-6: Email templates with @react-email/components, rendered at enqueue time","-","-**Context.** FR-13 through FR-15 and FR-16 require HTML and text emails. NFR-7 requires escaping user","-text. NFR-3 says no request may wait on SMTP.","-","-**Decision.** Templates are `.tsx` components in `src/server/email/templates/`","-(`BaseLayout`, `Invite`, `PasswordReset`, `GenericNotification`).","-`src/server/email/render.ts` exposes","-`renderEmail(template, data) → Promise<{ html, text }>`, which calls","-`await render(el)` and `await render(el, { plainText: true })` from","-`@react-email/render` 1.0.1. Rendering happens **inside the request** when","-`notify()`/`sendAuthEmail()` enqueue. The stored `html`/`text` columns","-are then a point-in-time snapshot, and the dispatcher only sends. `BaseLayout` reads","-`SchoolSettings` once per `notify()` call, not once per recipient.","-","-**Consequences.** Escaping (NFR-7) comes from React's JSX text escaping. Never use","-`dangerouslySetInnerHTML` in templates. Rendering is CPU work. For 1,000 recipients","-(NFR-1), render the `generic-notification` template **once per notify() call**,","-not per recipient. This works only if the template data has no per-recipient fields,","-so keep greetings generic in generic-notification. If a later PRD needs per-recipient","-personalisation, revisit NFR-1. Email templates are exempt from the MUI rule (NFR-10).","-","-_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-15, FR-16, NFR-1, NFR-3, NFR-7, NFR-10_","-","-## ADR-7: Background dispatch as a cron-hit HTTP route plus an optional tsx worker loop; no queue library","-","-**Context.** FR-19 and FR-20 require both a cron endpoint and a worker script. NFR-3 requires dispatch within 60 s.","-NFR-17 rules out new infrastructure (Redis/BullMQ are implicitly out).","-"," **Decision.**","-- `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch` compares `Authorization: Bearer <token>`","-  against `env.CRON_SECRET` with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length","-  buffers. If `CRON_SECRET` is unset, it always returns 401 (NFR-8). The route","-  calls `dispatchEmails()` and returns `{ sent, failed, retried }`, then","-  calls `purgeOldNotifications()` if more than 24 h have passed since the","-  module-level `lastPurgeAt` (FR-27).","-- `scripts/worker-email.ts` runs a `while (true) { await dispatchEmails(); await sleep(15_000) }`","-  loop with `try/catch` + `logger.error` around each tick. It calls the service","-  directly, not over HTTP, and catches SIGINT/SIGTERM to exit cleanly. It imports","-  through `tsx`, which resolves the `@/` tsconfig paths.","+- Use `prisma.$transaction(fn, { isolationLevel: Prisma.TransactionIsolationLevel.Serializable })`, which Prisma 5.20 supports natively on PostgreSQL.","+- Retry the whole transaction up to 3 attempts when the error is `PrismaClientKnownRequestError` with `code === 'P2034'` (write conflict or deadlock). After the last attempt, rethrow so `withHandler` maps it to a 500. The retry loop is a small local function inside `src/server/timetable/timetable-service.ts`. Do not add a shared util until a second caller exists (NFR-9).","+- Clashes found inside the transaction throw `AppError('CONFLICT', msg, { clashes })` from `src/server/http.ts`. The throw rolls the transaction back, so nothing is saved (FR-37, NFR-8).","+- The FR-3 `@@unique([courseId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` stays as a last line of defence. Map `P2002` to 409 as well.","+- Teaching-day switch-off (FR-7) and period reorder (FR-5) also use one transaction, at the default isolation level. Reorder needs a two-phase write because `Period.order @unique` would collide mid-update: first set each order to `-(i+1)`, then to `i+1`."," ","-**Consequences.** A 15 s worker tick, or a cron schedule of 1 minute or less, meets NFR-3.","-The `lastPurgeAt` value is tracked per process, so it resets on restart and","-purges run on every instance. The PRD accepts this. Mark it","-`ponytail: in-memory last-run, move to a DB row if purge cost matters`.","+**Consequences.** Clash rules (FR-9–FR-13) must run their reads through `tx`, not the global","+`prisma`, or SERIALIZABLE protects nothing. `validateSlot` therefore takes a client parameter","+that defaults to `prisma`, so FR-36 availability can reuse the same logic outside a write.","+The NFR-12 test follows the `email-dispatch.test.ts` pattern: `describe.runIf(TEST_DATABASE_URL)`,","+then `Promise.allSettled` of two conflicting creates, asserting exactly one is fulfilled."," ","-_Cites: FR-19, FR-20, FR-27, NFR-3, NFR-8, NFR-17, AC-6_","+## ADR-4: Clash detection loads candidate slots with set-based Prisma queries and checks them in memory (no raw SQL)"," ","-## ADR-8: Client UI with MUI v9 only, plain `fetch` polling, and no data-fetching library","+**Context.** FR-9–FR-14 define four clash rules plus the non-class period and non-teaching day","+rule, and `validateSlot` must return all of them. FR-36 availability must cover every","+(day, period) cell for one course in under 300 ms (NFR-3). The section week must load in under","+400 ms (NFR-2). The FR-3 index `@@index([academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` already exists for this."," ","-**Context.** FR-22 through FR-26, FR-28 through FR-31 and NFR-10 cover the UI. FR-23 requires a 30 s poll that pauses","-when the tab is hidden, and the PRD excludes websockets (NFR-17).","-"," **Decision.**","-- **Bell (FR-22–25).** A client component using `IconButton` + `Badge`","-  (`max={9}` renders \"9+\" natively) + `Popover` + `List`/`ListItemButton`.","-  Polling is a `setInterval(30_000)` started and stopped from a","-  `visibilitychange` listener in one `useEffect`. Don't add SWR or React Query,","-  because they aren't installed and one effect covers the need. Use `Typography` with","-  `sx={{ display: '-webkit-box', WebkitLineClamp: 2, WebkitBoxOrient: 'vertical', overflow: 'hidden' }}`","-  for the 2-line clamp.","-- **Relative time (FR-24).** Use `formatDistanceToNowStrict` from the installed","-  `date-fns`. Use `formatInSchoolTz` for absolute times (NFR-12).","-- **/notifications (FR-26).** Use MUI `Tabs` (All/Unread), a `Select` for the category, a `List`, and","-  `Pagination`.","-- **/account/notifications (FR-28–30).** Use `Table` with a full-width category","-  `TableRow` header per group (intake's default), and `Switch` + `Tooltip`","-  \"Required by the school\" on a disabled switch. Wrap the disabled switch in a `<span>` so the","-  tooltip still fires. Use optimistic local state with rollback, plus the existing `useToast`.","-- **/admin/email-log (FR-31–32).** Reuse the existing `src/components/DataTable`","-  (MUI X DataGrid, URL-driven pagination), with a status `Select` and a row-action `Button`.","+- **validateSlot** (single cell): one `findMany` of the other slots at `(academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId)`, excluding the moving slot's id on PATCH (FR-14). The query includes `course { teacherId, coTeacherIds, sectionId, code, subject, section }`, `room` and `period`. The teacher, room and section rules (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11) are array checks in TypeScript. Student overlap (FR-12) is one `enrollment.groupBy` / `count` over `status: 'ACTIVE'`, with `courseId in [otherCourseIds]` and `studentId in` the new course's active student ids. Both are covered by the existing `@@index([studentId, status])`.","+- **Availability** (FR-36): load all slots in the course's academic year in one query, and the new course's ACTIVE student ids plus the ACTIVE enrollments of those students in one query. Then run the same pure rule functions for each of the up-to-84 cells (7 × 12) in memory. This is a fixed number of queries, not one per cell.","+- The rule functions are pure (`(candidate, others, overlapCounts) => string[]`), so NFR-11 unit tests need no database.","+- **Editor and personal grids** (FR-35, FR-38): one `findMany` per view with `include` (course → subject, teacher; room; period). There are no N+1 queries."," ","-The mui-mcp server is reachable from this session (`useMuiDocs`,","-`fetchDocs` and `generateReactCode` tools are listed), so the NFR-10 blocker doesn't","-stand at architect time. Dev must still call `useMuiDocs` → `fetchDocs`","-per component before writing UI, and QA greps for banned imports.","+**Consequences.** No `$queryRaw`, so everything stays type-safe under strict TS (NFR-10).","+Ceiling: availability does its work in memory over the year's slots, roughly a few thousand rows","+for a single school (NFR-16), which fits the target comfortably.","+`// ponytail: in-memory clash map over the year's slots; move to SQL aggregation if slot count reaches ~50k.`"," ","-**Consequences.** No new UI dependency. The bell keeps its local state, and","-\"mark read\" refetches the count instead of introducing a shared store.","+## ADR-5: The drag-and-drop grid uses `@dnd-kit/core` with pessimistic updates; keyboard users get an MUI Dialog path"," ","-_Cites: FR-22, FR-23, FR-24, FR-25, FR-26, FR-28, FR-29, FR-30, FR-31, FR-32, NFR-10, NFR-12, NFR-17_","+**Context.** FR-16–FR-23 describe the editor grid. FR-20 requires that a rejected drop snaps","+back. FR-19 tints clashing cells while dragging, and NFR-4 says colour must never be the only signal.","+FR-22 and NFR-4 require a keyboard alternative. NFR-5 and NFR-6 allow MUI-only visuals."," ","-## ADR-9: Tests with the existing Vitest + Playwright; integration tests gated on a test DB","-","-**Context.** NFR-14 lists unit tests, NFR-15 integration tests against a DB,","-and NFR-16 Playwright tests. Vitest currently only includes `tests/unit/**`","-(jsdom), and NFR-13 requires `npm run test` to keep passing on machines without a DB.","-"," **Decision.**","-- Unit tests go in `tests/unit/`: preference resolution, `nextRetry`, link validation","-  and `notifyStudentAndParents` dedupe. These are pure functions, with Prisma mocked by `vi.mock` where needed.","-- Integration tests go in `tests/integration/*.test.ts`. Add that glob to `vitest.config.ts`","-  `include`, set `// @vitest-environment node` per file, and wrap each suite","-  in `describe.runIf(process.env.TEST_DATABASE_URL)` so a plain `npm run test`","-  skips it instead of failing. Write the concurrency test as","-  `Promise.all([dispatchEmails(), dispatchEmails()])` over 10 seeded PENDING rows,","-  with `sendMail` mocked to count calls, and assert each id is sent exactly once.","-- Playwright tests go in `tests/e2e/notifications.spec.ts`, covering the bell count after the admin","-  test button, mark-all-read and a persisted preference toggle.","+- `DndContext` with `PointerSensor` only (activation distance about 5px, so a click still opens the FR-21 room Popover). Cells use `useDroppable`. BREAK/LUNCH rows and non-teaching days set `disabled: true` (FR-16). Course cards and placed slots use `useDraggable`. The drag preview is a `DragOverlay` holding an MUI `Paper` (FR-23).","+- **Pessimistic updates.** The grid changes only after the POST or PATCH succeeds. On a 409, nothing moves, so \"snap back\" (FR-20) needs no rollback code. The toast comes from `useToast().showToast({ message, severity: 'error' })` with `details.clashes` joined into the message.","+- The clash map from `GET /api/timetable/availability` is fetched once when a drag starts, cached per courseId while the editor is open, and dropped after any mutation. Cells in the map get a red tint, an ErrorOutline icon and an MUI `Tooltip` listing the messages (FR-19, NFR-4).","+- Keyboard: cells are `tabIndex=0`. Enter on an empty cell opens an MUI `Dialog` listing the section's courses (FR-22). Delete on a focused slot opens the existing `ConfirmDialog` (FR-18). dnd-kit's `KeyboardSensor` is **not** used, because the Dialog path is the required alternative and it avoids the cost of making sensor announcements accessible.","+- Data fetching uses plain `fetch` plus `useState`, as elsewhere in the repo. No SWR or React Query is added."," ","-No new test tooling.","+**Consequences.** Each drop costs one round-trip of latency (no optimistic UI). That is acceptable","+for an admin tool, and it removes a whole class of state-desync bugs. Every component must still","+be looked up through mui-mcp before use (NFR-5). The mui-mcp server is connected in this","+environment, so the intake blocker is cleared for now, but dev must still call it."," ","-**Consequences.** CI must set `TEST_DATABASE_URL` for NFR-15 to actually run.","-Dev-planning should note this, because a green `npm run test` without it proves nothing about locking.","+## ADR-6: Print layout uses native `@media print` and `window.print()`, with no PDF or print library"," ","-_Cites: NFR-13, NFR-14, NFR-15, NFR-16, AC-4_","+**Context.** FR-29 requires a print-friendly A4 landscape page containing only the grid, the entity","+name and the academic year. NFR-16 requires print to work in the last two versions of Chrome, Edge,","+Firefox and Safari. NFR-6 bans extra UI libraries."," ","-## ADR-10: Link validation and field limits as shared pure helpers; validate in notify() without throwing","+**Decision.** The Print button calls `window.print()`. An MUI `GlobalStyles` block, rendered with","+the grid, contains `@page { size: A4 landscape; margin: 10mm }`. Under `@media print`, an `sx` rule","+hides the app shell (nav drawer, app bar, sidebar, view selectors and buttons), and a print-only","+heading shows the entity name and academic year. Cells use `break-inside: avoid`. Cell background","+colours need `print-color-adjust: exact`. Course code and teacher text carry the meaning anyway,","+so colour is not required (NFR-4)."," ","-**Context.** NFR-6 covers open-redirect-safe links, NFR-18 the title/body limits and","-FR-11 says notify() never throws.","+**Consequences.** No dependency is added. Firefox and Safari ignore some `@page` size hints,","+which is acceptable because the user picks the orientation in the print dialog. The Playwright","+check uses `page.emulateMedia({ media: 'print' })` and asserts that the shell is hidden."," ","-**Decision.** Add `isSafeRelativeLink(s)` to `src/lib/validators/notifications.ts`.","-It returns true only when the string starts with `/`, the second character is not `/` or `\\`, the","-string contains no `\\`, and it has no control characters. It's shared by notify(), the zod schemas and","-the bell's `router.push`. In notify(), an unsafe link is dropped (stored as `null`)","-with `logger.warn`, and title/body are truncated to 140/500 characters with `logger.warn`","-(the intake default). The whole notify() body is wrapped in `try/catch → logger.error`.","+## ADR-7: Testing stays on the existing Vitest and Playwright setup"," ","-**Consequences.** A bad link degrades to a notification that can't be clicked, never","-an exception in the caller's flow. A single regex-free function is trivially unit-tested (NFR-14).","+**Context.** NFR-11 (unit tests), NFR-12 (concurrency integration test), NFR-13 (e2e test) and NFR-10 (quality gate)."," ","-_Cites: FR-6, FR-11, NFR-6, NFR-14, NFR-18_","+**Decision.**","+- `tests/unit/timetable-clash.test.ts`: the pure rule functions from ADR-4, period overlap validation (FR-6) and `getCurrentSlot`/Now-Next with injected `now` and `tz` (ADR-2).","+- `tests/integration/timetable-concurrency.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node`, gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (ADR-3).","+- `tests/e2e/timetable.spec.ts`: Playwright tests for dragging a course into a cell, a clash toast on a conflicting drop, and the student Today view, all against the FR-41–FR-44 seed. dnd-kit's PointerSensor works with Playwright `mouse.down/move/up` when there are intermediate `move` steps. `locator.dragTo` alone may not cross the activation distance."," ","+**Consequences.** No new test tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed fixtures (FR-44) being idempotent.","+"," ---"," ","-### Notes for downstream sub-tasks","-- **Intake blocker FR-4/FR-16 (confirmed).** `sendAuthEmail()` renders the","-  invite/password-reset template and inserts one `EmailOutbox` row directly,","-  with no Notification row and no preference lookup. The `account.*` types are","-  `emailOnly` + `hidden`. The signature and both callers (users.ts:396, auth.ts:54) stay unchanged.","-- **NFR-1/FR-10 budget.** notify() makes 1 query each for users, preferences and","-  parent links where relevant, then 1 `createMany` for notifications and 1 for outbox","-  rows. That's within the limit of 3 round-trips each. Render once (ADR-6).","-- **No new dependencies anywhere in this PRD.**","+## Downstream notes (no stack impact; for `arch-design-system` to decide)","+","+- FR-21 free rooms: no stack choice is needed. Either intake's suggested `GET /api/timetable/free-rooms` or the availability payload works with ADR-4's query pattern. One query (rooms not archived, minus `roomId`s used at that day and period) is enough.","+- FR-7 slot count: intake's default (`GET /api/school-days` returns `slotCount` per day) is one `timetableSlot.groupBy({ by: ['dayOfWeek'] })`, which works fine with this stack.","+- FR-40 audit: `audit()` from `src/server/audit.ts` should be called inside the same transaction client where its signature allows. That is for design to confirm."]}],"originalFile":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-04 Notifications & Email Infrastructure (SCH-004)\n\nTicket: SH-4 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-04T12:10:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports confidence 100%, and `package.json`\nconfirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3, TypeScript 5.5 strict,\nPrisma 5.20 / PostgreSQL, MUI 9.5 (material, icons, x-data-grid) + emotion,\nzod 3.23, date-fns 3.6 / date-fns-tz, Vitest 2.1 and Playwright 1.47.\n**This PRD adds no new runtime or dev dependency.** Everything it needs is\nalready installed: `nodemailer ^6.9.14` + `@types/nodemailer`,\n`@react-email/components 0.0.25`, `@react-email/render 1.0.1` and `tsx 4.16`.\nThe intake stack fits every FR/NFR, so the ADRs below record only the\nimplementation choices the requirements force.\n\n**Brownfield facts checked in the workspace.** These confirm or sharpen intake:\n- `prisma/schema.prisma` declares `EmailStatus`, `Notification` (with\n  `@@index([userId, readAt, createdAt])`), `NotificationPreference` (composite\n  `@@id([userId, type])`) and `EmailOutbox` (with `@@index([status, nextAttemptAt])`, no FK\n  to User, and `updatedAt` documented as the SENDING timestamp). The 4 existing\n  migrations don't create these tables.\n- `@react-email/render@1.0.1`'s `render(el, { plainText?: true })` returns a\n  **`Promise<string>`**. It's async in 1.x, so every render call must be awaited.\n- `src/lib/env.ts` already validates `EMAIL_TRANSPORT` (`smtp|console`, default\n  `console`), `SMTP_*`, `EMAIL_FROM`, an optional `CRON_SECRET` and `APP_URL`.\n- `src/server/http.ts` already provides `withHandler`, `ok`, `paginated`,\n  `parsePagination`, `parseBody`, `parseQuery` and `AppError`. `src/lib/logger.ts`\n  provides `logger`. `src/lib/dates.ts` provides `formatInSchoolTz`. `src/server/db.ts`\n  exports the `prisma` singleton.\n- The existing `src/components/DataTable` wraps MUI X DataGrid with\n  URL-driven server pagination. `src/components/Toast/useToast.ts` exists.\n- Vitest `include` is `tests/unit/**` only, with `environment: 'jsdom'`.\n  There's no integration test folder yet.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: One additive Prisma migration for the existing PRD-04 models\n\n**Context.** FR-1 requires the tables. The models are already in\n`schema.prisma` but no migration creates them (an intake blocker). NFR-13\nsays never rename and never re-scaffold. NFR-5 relies on `EmailOutbox.updatedAt`.\n\n**Decision.** Run `prisma migrate dev --name notifications_email` without\ntouching the schema. It generates the `EmailStatus` enum, the 3 tables, their\nindexes and the `Notification`/`NotificationPreference` → `User` cascade FKs.\nKeep the additive `updatedAt` columns exactly as they are declared.\n\n**Consequences.** This is one purely additive migration with nothing destructive. Prisma's\n`@updatedAt` sets the column on every update made through the Prisma client, **but not\non `$executeRaw`/`$queryRaw` writes**. Any raw SQL that sets `status = 'SENDING'`\nmust also set `\"updatedAt\" = now()` itself (see ADR-3).\n\n_Cites: FR-1, NFR-5, NFR-13_\n\n## ADR-2: Registry as a `const` object with `satisfies`; `keyof typeof` drives notify()\n\n**Context.** FR-2 through FR-5 need a typed registry that rejects unknown\nkeys at compile time and that later PRDs can extend.\n\n**Decision.** In `src/server/notifications/types.ts`, define\n`export const NOTIFICATION_TYPES = { ... } as const satisfies Record<string, NotificationTypeDef>`\nand `export type NotificationType = keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES`. Express\ncategories (FR-3) as a string-literal union, `NotificationCategory`, plus a\n`NOTIFICATION_CATEGORIES` tuple that the preferences page and its zod enum use.\n`NotificationTypeDef` adds `emailOnly?: true` / `hidden?: true` for the\n`account.*` types (FR-4, FR-28). Later PRDs extend the registry by adding\nkeys to this one object. Don't build a runtime registration API (YAGNI).\n\n**Consequences.** `NotificationPreference.type` and `Notification.type` stay\nplain `String` columns, because the DB doesn't need an enum for a list that\ngrows with every PRD. The zod validator for the preferences PUT (FR-38,\nFR-41) builds `z.enum(Object.keys(NOTIFICATION_TYPES) as [NotificationType, ...])`.\nIt can't import server-only modules, so `types.ts` must stay free of server\nimports (no `prisma`, no `env`) to remain importable from\n`src/lib/validators/notifications.ts`.\n\n_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-5, FR-28, FR-38, FR-41_\n\n## ADR-3: Dispatcher claim through one raw-SQL `UPDATE … WHERE id IN (SELECT … FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED) RETURNING *`\n\n**Context.** FR-17 and NFR-4 require that concurrent dispatchers never send a row\ntwice. NFR-5 requires recovering rows stuck in SENDING for more than 10 minutes. Prisma 5.20\nhas no `SKIP LOCKED` in its query API.\n\n**Decision.** Each `dispatchEmails({ batchSize = 50 })` run:\n1. **Recover.** Run `prisma.emailOutbox.updateMany({ where: { status: 'SENDING', updatedAt: { lt: now - 10min } }, data: { status: 'PENDING' } })`.\n2. **Claim.** Use a single statement through `prisma.$queryRaw`:\n   ```sql\n   UPDATE \"EmailOutbox\" SET status = 'SENDING', \"updatedAt\" = now()\n   WHERE id IN (\n     SELECT id FROM \"EmailOutbox\"\n     WHERE status = 'PENDING' AND \"nextAttemptAt\" <= now()\n     ORDER BY \"nextAttemptAt\" LIMIT ${batchSize}\n     FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED)\n   RETURNING *;\n   ```\n   It runs as one autocommit statement, so the row lock is held only for the flip and is released\n   before any SMTP call. This avoids long transactions, and once a row is\n   SENDING no other dispatcher's `WHERE status='PENDING'` can match it.\n3. **Send each row**, then mark it SENT (`sentAt`) or reschedule it through Prisma `update` (ADR-4).\n\n**Consequences.** This is the only raw SQL in the PRD. Use tagged-template `$queryRaw`, never\n`$queryRawUnsafe`. Cast the enum literal (`'SENDING'::\"EmailStatus\"`) because\nPostgres won't implicitly cast text to an enum in a `SET`. A crash mid-send leaves the row SENDING,\nand step 1 recovers it after 10 minutes. That gives at-least-once delivery on crash, which\nthe PRD accepts. The two-concurrent-callers and stuck-row behavior is covered\nby NFR-15 integration tests (ADR-9).\n\n_Cites: FR-17, NFR-4, NFR-5, NFR-15, AC-4_\n\n## ADR-4: Retry backoff as a pure lookup table\n\n**Context.** FR-18 sets backoff delays of 1/5/15/60/240 min for attempts 1–5\nand FAILED after the 5th failure. NFR-14 requires a unit test for it.\n\n**Decision.** `src/server/email/backoff.ts` exports `const BACKOFF_MIN = [1, 5, 15, 60, 240]`\nand a pure `nextRetry(attempts, now): { status: 'PENDING', nextAttemptAt } | { status: 'FAILED' }`,\nwhere `attempts` is the count after incrementing. No retry library.\n\n**Consequences.** This is a trivially unit-testable pure function. The admin\n\"Retry now\" action (FR-32) just resets `attempts=0, status=PENDING, nextAttemptAt=now()`.\n\n_Cites: FR-18, FR-32, NFR-14, AC-3_\n\n## ADR-5: Email transport: Nodemailer for SMTP, a logger-free console transport, a lazy singleton\n\n**Context.** FR-21 selects the transport by `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`. NFR-9 requires\nHTML + text parts and a `List-Unsubscribe` header. NFR-11 bans `console.log`\nexcept in the console transport.\n\n**Decision.** `src/server/email/transport.ts` exports `sendMail({ to, toName, subject, html, text })`.\n- `smtp`: `nodemailer.createTransport({ host, port, secure, auth })`, created\n  lazily once per process. It sends `{ from: EMAIL_FROM, html, text, headers: { 'List-Unsubscribe': '<${APP_URL}/account/notifications>' } }`.\n  Nodemailer builds `multipart/alternative` automatically when both `html` and `text` are set.\n  It throws a clear error at first send if `SMTP_HOST` or `EMAIL_FROM` is missing.\n- `console`: `console.log` of the subject, recipient and text body (the one sanctioned use, NFR-11).\n\nUse a plain `if` on the env value, with no transport interface or factory, because\nthere are only two branches.\n\n**Consequences.** Dev and CI never need SMTP credentials. Tests can assert outbox rows\nand dispatcher state transitions without a mail server. The SMTP failure path\n(AC-3) is tested by stubbing `sendMail` with `vi.mock`.\n\n_Cites: FR-17, FR-21, NFR-9, NFR-11_\n\n## ADR-6: Email templates with @react-email/components, rendered at enqueue time\n\n**Context.** FR-13 through FR-15 and FR-16 require HTML and text emails. NFR-7 requires escaping user\ntext. NFR-3 says no request may wait on SMTP.\n\n**Decision.** Templates are `.tsx` components in `src/server/email/templates/`\n(`BaseLayout`, `Invite`, `PasswordReset`, `GenericNotification`).\n`src/server/email/render.ts` exposes\n`renderEmail(template, data) → Promise<{ html, text }>`, which calls\n`await render(el)` and `await render(el, { plainText: true })` from\n`@react-email/render` 1.0.1. Rendering happens **inside the request** when\n`notify()`/`sendAuthEmail()` enqueue. The stored `html`/`text` columns\nare then a point-in-time snapshot, and the dispatcher only sends. `BaseLayout` reads\n`SchoolSettings` once per `notify()` call, not once per recipient.\n\n**Consequences.** Escaping (NFR-7) comes from React's JSX text escaping. Never use\n`dangerouslySetInnerHTML` in templates. Rendering is CPU work. For 1,000 recipients\n(NFR-1), render the `generic-notification` template **once per notify() call**,\nnot per recipient. This works only if the template data has no per-recipient fields,\nso keep greetings generic in generic-notification. If a later PRD needs per-recipient\npersonalisation, revisit NFR-1. Email templates are exempt from the MUI rule (NFR-10).\n\n_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-15, FR-16, NFR-1, NFR-3, NFR-7, NFR-10_\n\n## ADR-7: Background dispatch as a cron-hit HTTP route plus an optional tsx worker loop; no queue library\n\n**Context.** FR-19 and FR-20 require both a cron endpoint and a worker script. NFR-3 requires dispatch within 60 s.\nNFR-17 rules out new infrastructure (Redis/BullMQ are implicitly out).\n\n**Decision.**\n- `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch` compares `Authorization: Bearer <token>`\n  against `env.CRON_SECRET` with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length\n  buffers. If `CRON_SECRET` is unset, it always returns 401 (NFR-8). The route\n  calls `dispatchEmails()` and returns `{ sent, failed, retried }`, then\n  calls `purgeOldNotifications()` if more than 24 h have passed since the\n  module-level `lastPurgeAt` (FR-27).\n- `scripts/worker-email.ts` runs a `while (true) { await dispatchEmails(); await sleep(15_000) }`\n  loop with `try/catch` + `logger.error` around each tick. It calls the service\n  directly, not over HTTP, and catches SIGINT/SIGTERM to exit cleanly. It imports\n  through `tsx`, which resolves the `@/` tsconfig paths.\n\n**Consequences.** A 15 s worker tick, or a cron schedule of 1 minute or less, meets NFR-3.\nThe `lastPurgeAt` value is tracked per process, so it resets on restart and\npurges run on every instance. The PRD accepts this. Mark it\n`ponytail: in-memory last-run, move to a DB row if purge cost matters`.\n\n_Cites: FR-19, FR-20, FR-27, NFR-3, NFR-8, NFR-17, AC-6_\n\n## ADR-8: Client UI with MUI v9 only, plain `fetch` polling, and no data-fetching library\n\n**Context.** FR-22 through FR-26, FR-28 through FR-31 and NFR-10 cover the UI. FR-23 requires a 30 s poll that pauses\nwhen the tab is hidden, and the PRD excludes websockets (NFR-17).\n\n**Decision.**\n- **Bell (FR-22–25).** A client component using `IconButton` + `Badge`\n  (`max={9}` renders \"9+\" natively) + `Popover` + `List`/`ListItemButton`.\n  Polling is a `setInterval(30_000)` started and stopped from a\n  `visibilitychange` listener in one `useEffect`. Don't add SWR or React Query,\n  because they aren't installed and one effect covers the need. Use `Typography` with\n  `sx={{ display: '-webkit-box', WebkitLineClamp: 2, WebkitBoxOrient: 'vertical', overflow: 'hidden' }}`\n  for the 2-line clamp.\n- **Relative time (FR-24).** Use `formatDistanceToNowStrict` from the installed\n  `date-fns`. Use `formatInSchoolTz` for absolute times (NFR-12).\n- **/notifications (FR-26).** Use MUI `Tabs` (All/Unread), a `Select` for the category, a `List`, and\n  `Pagination`.\n- **/account/notifications (FR-28–30).** Use `Table` with a full-width category\n  `TableRow` header per group (intake's default), and `Switch` + `Tooltip`\n  \"Required by the school\" on a disabled switch. Wrap the disabled switch in a `<span>` so the\n  tooltip still fires. Use optimistic local state with rollback, plus the existing `useToast`.\n- **/admin/email-log (FR-31–32).** Reuse the existing `src/components/DataTable`\n  (MUI X DataGrid, URL-driven pagination), with a status `Select` and a row-action `Button`.\n\nThe mui-mcp server is reachable from this session (`useMuiDocs`,\n`fetchDocs` and `generateReactCode` tools are listed), so the NFR-10 blocker doesn't\nstand at architect time. Dev must still call `useMuiDocs` → `fetchDocs`\nper component before writing UI, and QA greps for banned imports.\n\n**Consequences.** No new UI dependency. The bell keeps its local state, and\n\"mark read\" refetches the count instead of introducing a shared store.\n\n_Cites: FR-22, FR-23, FR-24, FR-25, FR-26, FR-28, FR-29, FR-30, FR-31, FR-32, NFR-10, NFR-12, NFR-17_\n\n## ADR-9: Tests with the existing Vitest + Playwright; integration tests gated on a test DB\n\n**Context.** NFR-14 lists unit tests, NFR-15 integration tests against a DB,\nand NFR-16 Playwright tests. Vitest currently only includes `tests/unit/**`\n(jsdom), and NFR-13 requires `npm run test` to keep passing on machines without a DB.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Unit tests go in `tests/unit/`: preference resolution, `nextRetry`, link validation\n  and `notifyStudentAndParents` dedupe. These are pure functions, with Prisma mocked by `vi.mock` where needed.\n- Integration tests go in `tests/integration/*.test.ts`. Add that glob to `vitest.config.ts`\n  `include`, set `// @vitest-environment node` per file, and wrap each suite\n  in `describe.runIf(process.env.TEST_DATABASE_URL)` so a plain `npm run test`\n  skips it instead of failing. Write the concurrency test as\n  `Promise.all([dispatchEmails(), dispatchEmails()])` over 10 seeded PENDING rows,\n  with `sendMail` mocked to count calls, and assert each id is sent exactly once.\n- Playwright tests go in `tests/e2e/notifications.spec.ts`, covering the bell count after the admin\n  test button, mark-all-read and a persisted preference toggle.\n\nNo new test tooling.\n\n**Consequences.** CI must set `TEST_DATABASE_URL` for NFR-15 to actually run.\nDev-planning should note this, because a green `npm run test` without it proves nothing about locking.\n\n_Cites: NFR-13, NFR-14, NFR-15, NFR-16, AC-4_\n\n## ADR-10: Link validation and field limits as shared pure helpers; validate in notify() without throwing\n\n**Context.** NFR-6 covers open-redirect-safe links, NFR-18 the title/body limits and\nFR-11 says notify() never throws.\n\n**Decision.** Add `isSafeRelativeLink(s)` to `src/lib/validators/notifications.ts`.\nIt returns true only when the string starts with `/`, the second character is not `/` or `\\`, the\nstring contains no `\\`, and it has no control characters. It's shared by notify(), the zod schemas and\nthe bell's `router.push`. In notify(), an unsafe link is dropped (stored as `null`)\nwith `logger.warn`, and title/body are truncated to 140/500 characters with `logger.warn`\n(the intake default). The whole notify() body is wrapped in `try/catch → logger.error`.\n\n**Consequences.** A bad link degrades to a notification that can't be clicked, never\nan exception in the caller's flow. A single regex-free function is trivially unit-tested (NFR-14).\n\n_Cites: FR-6, FR-11, NFR-6, NFR-14, NFR-18_\n\n---\n\n### Notes for downstream sub-tasks\n- **Intake blocker FR-4/FR-16 (confirmed).** `sendAuthEmail()` renders the\n  invite/password-reset template and inserts one `EmailOutbox` row directly,\n  with no Notification row and no preference lookup. The `account.*` types are\n  `emailOnly` + `hidden`. The signature and both callers (users.ts:396, auth.ts:54) stay unchanged.\n- **NFR-1/FR-10 budget.** notify() makes 1 query each for users, preferences and\n  parent links where relevant, then 1 `createMany` for notifications and 1 for outbox\n  rows. That's within the limit of 3 round-trips each. Render once (ADR-6).\n- **No new dependencies anywhere in this PRD.**\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014edccA3x5aL19x6U2X1BLE","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
