{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjKYABMyRASqzGMgGrmf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've finished checking the SH-1 workspace and am now writing the ADR. The existing `tech-stack-decisions.md` is left over from SH-6, so I'm overwriting it because this sub-task owns that file."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":603,"cache_read_input_tokens":51423,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":603},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a9ff751b-b713-4810-9c30-1cad791c6970","uuid":"4efbfab3-7919-44c8-9794-9cbe0082aa6f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:37:57.013Z","request_id":"req_011CgjKY9r2V9nFp2SLQjUh8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjKYABMyRASqzGMgGrmf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0191GqWT3ybQYtFZ3trphWrG","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions & RBAC (SCH-001)\n\nTicket: SH-1 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-05T11:45:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and `package.json`\nconfirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL,\nnext-auth 5.0.0-beta.25 (Credentials, JWT), bcrypt ^6, zod 3.23.8, react-hook-form 7.53.0,\nMUI 9.5.0 with emotion, Vitest 2.1.1, Playwright 1.47.2 and @axe-core/playwright 4.10.0.\nThe stack fits every FR and NFR. **This ticket adds no dependency** (NFR-7, NFR-13). The ADRs below\nrecord only the choices the requirements force for the gaps intake listed: the auth pages, the shell\nwiring, the seed, the index migration and the tests.\n\n**Workspace facts I checked** (they confirm intake or add detail to it):\n\n- `src/server/auth/credentials.ts`: `AuthError extends CredentialsSignin` and sets `.code` to one of `invalid`, `disabled`, `rate_limited` or `locked:<N>`. A `DUMMY_HASH` compare runs on every path, so NFR-3 is already met.\n- `src/server/auth/auth.ts` exports `handlers, auth, signIn, signOut, unstable_update`. `auth.config.ts` already sanitises redirects through `safeCallbackUrl` and sets `pages.signIn = '/login'`. The cookies are the Auth.js defaults: HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, `__Secure-` prefix in production (NFR-2).\n- **No `SessionProvider`, `useSession` or `next-auth/react` import exists anywhere in `src/`.** `src/app/(app)/layout.tsx` is an async Server Component, and it renders the client `AppShell` from `shell.tsx`, which still contains `PLACEHOLDER_USER` (lines 40, 78, 162–167).\n- `acceptInvite()` returns `{ email }` and does not sign the user in. `POST /api/account/password` already calls `unstable_update({ reauth: true })`.\n- `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports `signInSchema`, `forgotPasswordSchema`, `setPasswordSchema(email)`, `acceptInviteSchema`, `resetPasswordSchema`, `changePasswordFormSchema(email)`, `passwordChecks`, `PASSWORD_RULES` and `safeCallbackUrl`. These cover every form.\n- `src/server/auth/email.ts` exports `authLink(kind, rawToken)`. `sendAuthEmail` enqueues to EmailOutbox and never logs the link.\n- `prisma/schema.prisma`: `AuditLog.ip String?`. `User` has `@@index([lastName, firstName, email])` and does not have `@@index([role, status])`. The latest migration is `20261105000000_timetable`.\n- Tests: the unit tests for tokens, lockout, rate-limit, password-policy and auth-validators exist. `tests/integration/` has the `TEST_DATABASE_URL`-gated precedent `email-dispatch.test.ts`. `tests/e2e/` has no auth spec. `playwright.config.ts` runs `npm run start` against `APP_URL`.\n- The `mui-mcp` server is connected in this environment, so the NFR-6 blocker is clear for now. Dev must still call it before writing UI.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the declared stack unchanged and add no dependency\n\n**Context.** NFR-13 requires no scope expansion and no rebuild of the existing auth modules. NFR-7 bans\nextra UI libraries. Every backend piece the gaps need already exists (FR-1, FR-3, FR-5, FR-9–FR-12,\nFR-15, FR-18, FR-22–FR-24).\n\n**Decision.** Keep `package.json` as it is. Do not add an OTP/2FA library, a password-strength library\nsuch as zxcvbn, a form wizard, or a toast library. The strength checklist comes from the existing\n`passwordChecks()` and `PASSWORD_RULES` (FR-16). Toasts use the existing `useToast()` (NFR-6).\n\n**Consequences.** The lockfile does not change. All new code is pages, shell wiring, the seed, one\nmigration and tests.\n\n## ADR-2: Sign-in uses the client `signIn('credentials', { redirect: false })` from `next-auth/react`, with errors mapped from `result.code`\n\n**Context.** FR-2 requires a `/login` form that submits through Auth.js `signIn('credentials')`.\nFR-4 and FR-6 need the server's specific messages (locked N minutes, disabled, rate-limited) shown\nin an Alert. FR-7 requires the callbackUrl to be sanitised. NFR-5 requires aria-live errors and focus\non the first invalid field. NFR-11 requires RHF with `zodResolver(signInSchema)`. NFR-2 requires CSRF.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `/login/page.tsx` is a Server Component that reads `searchParams` and renders a client `LoginForm`.\n- `LoginForm` calls `signIn('credentials', { email, password, redirect: false })` from `next-auth/react`. In beta.25 the result carries `code` from `AuthError`. A small pure function, `loginErrorMessage(code)`, maps the codes. `locked:N` becomes \"This account is temporarily locked. Try again in N minutes.\" `disabled` becomes the disabled message. `rate_limited` becomes \"Too many attempts…\". Every other code becomes \"Email or password is incorrect.\" (FR-4, FR-6). Keep the function in the form file and export it for a unit test.\n- On success, call `router.replace(safeCallbackUrl(searchParams.callbackUrl))` and then `router.refresh()` (FR-7). The function already exists, and the server `redirect` callback enforces the same rule a second time.\n- The client `signIn` fetches the Auth.js CSRF token itself (NFR-2). There is no hand-rolled CSRF.\n- Do **not** use a Server Action with the server `signIn`. In beta.25 that throws `CredentialsSignin` through `redirect` plumbing, and the error is harder to surface. The client path is the documented way to show credential errors.\n\n**Consequences.** `next-auth/react` is part of the installed `next-auth`, so nothing is added. The client\ncall does not need a `SessionProvider`, because `signIn` and `signOut` from `next-auth/react` work\nwithout one.\n\n## ADR-3: The shell gets the current user from the server `auth()` through a prop; there is no `SessionProvider`\n\n**Context.** FR-25 says to delete `PLACEHOLDER_USER` and keep exactly one source of \"current user\".\nFR-26 needs the name, role badge, initials and Sign out. FR-27 needs `navigationForRole` to use the\nsession role. NFR-9 requires server enforcement. `getCurrentUser()` / `requirePageUser()` already exist (FR-23).\n\n**Decision.**\n- `src/app/(app)/layout.tsx` (already an async Server Component) calls `requirePageUser()`, which redirects to `/login` when there is no session. The middleware already handles that case (FR-11), so this is a second guard. The layout passes `user={{ id, firstName, lastName, role }}` to `AppShell`.\n- `AppShell` takes a `user` prop, uses `navigationForRole(user.role)`, and shows initials, the name and a role `Chip`. Its menu has an \"Account security\" `MenuItem` that links to `/account/security` (FR-21) and a \"Sign out\" item that calls `signOut({ callbackUrl: '/login' })` from `next-auth/react` (FR-26).\n- Do **not** add a `SessionProvider` or `useSession`. That would create a second \"current user\" source with its own polling and would go against FR-25.\n\n**Consequences.** The user is read once per navigation on the server, where a JWT decode costs almost\nnothing. Role or name changes appear after the next 5-minute recheck (FR-10), which is the same\nfreshness the JWT already has. Any shell test has to pass a `user` prop instead of mocking a session.\n\n## ADR-4: The token pages verify on the server first; set-password forms are client RHF forms that post to the existing JSON APIs\n\n**Context.** FR-13 says `/invite/[token]` must show the invalid-link message, or the name and email\nread-only. FR-14 says that after acceptance the user is signed in and sent to `/dashboard`. FR-19\ncovers `/reset-password/[token]` → `/login` with a success toast. FR-20 covers `/account/security`\n→ \"Password updated\", or a field error on a wrong current password. FR-17 covers `/forgot-password`\nwith a constant message. FR-12 and NFR-1 say tokens are only touched through `tokens.ts`. NFR-10 requires thin handlers.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `invite/[token]/page.tsx` and `reset-password/[token]/page.tsx` are Server Components. They call `verifyToken(params.token, 'INVITE' | 'PASSWORD_RESET')` from `tokens.ts` without consuming the token. If it returns null, they render the invalid-link Alert. Otherwise they render a client form with the user's email (and name, for invites). The raw token is passed only as a prop. It is never logged and never put in audit metadata (NFR-8).\n- Forms use RHF with `zodResolver(setPasswordSchema(email))` or `changePasswordFormSchema(email)`, so client and server share one schema (NFR-11). They `fetch` the existing routes: `POST /api/auth/accept-invite`, `/api/auth/reset-password`, `/api/auth/forgot-password` and `/api/account/password`. Server `VALIDATION_ERROR` `details` are mapped back with `setError` (FR-20 \"Current password is incorrect.\").\n- Invite success: call `signIn('credentials', { email, password, redirect: false })` with the password just set, then `router.replace('/dashboard')` (FR-14). The service already consumed the token, so a later visit shows the invalid-link message.\n- Reset success: `router.replace('/login?reset=1')`. `LoginForm` shows `useToast` \"Password updated, sign in with your new password\" once on mount when `reset=1` is present (FR-19).\n- `/forgot-password` always shows the same success Alert after any 2xx or 429 response, so there is no enumeration (FR-17).\n- One shared client component, `PasswordChecklist({ password, email })`, renders `PASSWORD_RULES` as an MUI `List` with CheckCircle and RadioButtonUnchecked icons plus text. Colour is never the only signal. It is used under every new-password field (FR-16).\n- Accessibility: one `Alert role=\"alert\"` / `aria-live=\"polite\"` region per form. Field helperText comes from RHF. RHF's default `shouldFocusError: true` moves focus to the first invalid field (NFR-5). No extra code is needed.\n\n**Consequences.** No new API routes and no new services. Only pages and client forms are added.\nVerifying the token on the server before render means that an expired link never shows the form.\n\n## ADR-5: Seed users are upserted with bcrypt cost 12; the invite link is logged through the logger\n\n**Context.** FR-28 requires six ACTIVE users with password `SchoolHub123!`, created idempotently. FR-29\nand FR-30 require one INVITED user whose invite link is logged. FR-31 says not to change `sendAuthEmail`.\nNFR-12 bans `console.log`.\n\n**Decision.**\n- In `prisma/seed.ts`, add a users block **before** the existing PRD-04 notification block, which skips users that don't exist. Hash once with `bcrypt.hash('SchoolHub123!', 12)`. Then call `prisma.user.upsert({ where: { email }, create: {..., status: 'ACTIVE', passwordHash, passwordChangedAt: now }, update: {} })` for each user. `update: {}` keeps re-runs idempotent and leaves edited users alone.\n- `invited@schoolhub.test`: upsert with `status: 'INVITED'` and `passwordHash: null`. Then call `createInviteToken(user.id)` from `services/users.ts` (it invalidates older invites) and `logger.info({ link: authLink('invite', raw) }, 'seed invite link')`. Call these only while the user is still INVITED. Do not call `sendAuthEmail`.\n\n**Consequences.** Each re-seed issues a fresh invite link and invalidates the previous one, which is the\nexpected dev behaviour. The bcrypt hash adds about 250 ms to the seed run.\n\n## ADR-6: Add `@@index([role, status])` in a new additive migration; keep `AuditLog.ip`\n\n**Context.** FR-33 says the PRD's User model declares `@@index([role, status])`. FR-32 says not to rename\nexisting fields, so `ip` stays. NFR-13 requires no renames.\n\n**Decision.** Add `@@index([role, status])` to `User`, and generate\n`prisma/migrations/2026110600000_user_role_status_index/` with `prisma migrate dev --create-only`.\nIt contains a single `CREATE INDEX`. `AuditLog.ip` does not change. FR-32 remains flagged for human sign-off.\n\n**Consequences.** The migration is additive and does not touch existing data. Use a migration\ntimestamp later than `20261105000000_timetable`.\n\n## ADR-7: Tests use the existing Vitest and Playwright setup; e2e reads reset links from EmailOutbox\n\n**Context.** FR-34 asks for integration tests for authorize, accept-invite, reset-password and\nchange-password, plus Playwright tests for sign-in/out, the failed-login message and forgot → reset.\nNFR-5 covers accessibility. NFR-13 requires `lint`, `typecheck` and `test` to stay green.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `tests/integration/auth.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node` and `describe.runIf(process.env.TEST_DATABASE_URL)`, the same as `email-dispatch.test.ts`. It calls `authorizeCredentials` and the services directly with a stub `Request`, covering each branch (invalid, disabled, locked:N, lockout at 5, rate_limited, success resets the count) and each service's happy path and token reuse. Call `__resetRateLimits()` in `beforeEach`.\n- `tests/unit/login-error-message.test.ts`: covers the `loginErrorMessage` code map from ADR-2.\n- `tests/e2e/auth.spec.ts`: covers sign-in as the seed admin, sign-out, the wrong-password message, and the redirect for an unauthenticated `/dashboard`. For forgot → reset, the spec reads the newest `EmailOutbox` row for the address through `@prisma/client` in the test process and extracts the `/reset-password/…` URL from the body. That way it needs no test-only API route in the app. Run `@axe-core/playwright` `AxeBuilder` once on `/login` (NFR-5).\n\n**Consequences.** No new tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed (ADR-5) and on `DATABASE_URL`\nbeing visible to the Playwright process. Because the specs change shared users (password reset,\nlockout), mark `auth.spec.ts` as `test.describe.configure({ mode: 'serial' })`. The\nreset flow should use `student2@` so that admin sign-in in parallel specs is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Downstream notes for `arch-design-system`\n\n- Intake blockers that system-design must surface: the brownfield \"design gaps only\" rule, FR-32 (`ip`, LOW confidence), FR-33 (additive index), FR-30/FR-31 (the seed logs the link, `sendAuthEmail` stays as it is) and NFR-6 (the mui-mcp lookup is mandatory).\n- No new API endpoints are implied. `api-contracts` should list the existing routes the new pages consume: `/api/auth/[...nextauth]`, `forgot-password`, `reset-password`, `accept-invite` and `/api/account/password`.\n- Do not modify `course-access.ts` or `student-access.ts` (NFR-13).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":603,"cache_read_input_tokens":51423,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":603},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a9ff751b-b713-4810-9c30-1cad791c6970","uuid":"3a387ead-7c14-4260-bfee-9d137c638fc2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:38:46.146Z","request_id":"req_011CgjKY9r2V9nFp2SLQjUh8","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0191GqWT3ybQYtFZ3trphWrG":{"file_path":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions & RBAC (SCH-001)\n\nTicket: SH-1 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-05T11:45:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and `package.json`\nconfirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL,\nnext-auth 5.0.0-beta.25 (Credentials, JWT), bcrypt ^6, zod 3.23.8, react-hook-form 7.53.0,\nMUI 9.5.0 with emotion, Vitest 2.1.1, Playwright 1.47.2 and @axe-core/playwright 4.10.0.\nThe stack fits every FR and NFR. **This ticket adds no dependency** (NFR-7, NFR-13). The ADRs below\nrecord only the choices the requirements force for the gaps intake listed: the auth pages, the shell\nwiring, the seed, the index migration and the tests.\n\n**Workspace facts I checked** (they confirm intake or add detail to it):\n\n- `src/server/auth/credentials.ts`: `AuthError extends CredentialsSignin` and sets `.code` to one of `invalid`, `disabled`, `rate_limited` or `locked:<N>`. A `DUMMY_HASH` compare runs on every path, so NFR-3 is already met.\n- `src/server/auth/auth.ts` exports `handlers, auth, signIn, signOut, unstable_update`. `auth.config.ts` already sanitises redirects through `safeCallbackUrl` and sets `pages.signIn = '/login'`. The cookies are the Auth.js defaults: HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, `__Secure-` prefix in production (NFR-2).\n- **No `SessionProvider`, `useSession` or `next-auth/react` import exists anywhere in `src/`.** `src/app/(app)/layout.tsx` is an async Server Component, and it renders the client `AppShell` from `shell.tsx`, which still contains `PLACEHOLDER_USER` (lines 40, 78, 162–167).\n- `acceptInvite()` returns `{ email }` and does not sign the user in. `POST /api/account/password` already calls `unstable_update({ reauth: true })`.\n- `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports `signInSchema`, `forgotPasswordSchema`, `setPasswordSchema(email)`, `acceptInviteSchema`, `resetPasswordSchema`, `changePasswordFormSchema(email)`, `passwordChecks`, `PASSWORD_RULES` and `safeCallbackUrl`. These cover every form.\n- `src/server/auth/email.ts` exports `authLink(kind, rawToken)`. `sendAuthEmail` enqueues to EmailOutbox and never logs the link.\n- `prisma/schema.prisma`: `AuditLog.ip String?`. `User` has `@@index([lastName, firstName, email])` and does not have `@@index([role, status])`. The latest migration is `20261105000000_timetable`.\n- Tests: the unit tests for tokens, lockout, rate-limit, password-policy and auth-validators exist. `tests/integration/` has the `TEST_DATABASE_URL`-gated precedent `email-dispatch.test.ts`. `tests/e2e/` has no auth spec. `playwright.config.ts` runs `npm run start` against `APP_URL`.\n- The `mui-mcp` server is connected in this environment, so the NFR-6 blocker is clear for now. Dev must still call it before writing UI.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the declared stack unchanged and add no dependency\n\n**Context.** NFR-13 requires no scope expansion and no rebuild of the existing auth modules. NFR-7 bans\nextra UI libraries. Every backend piece the gaps need already exists (FR-1, FR-3, FR-5, FR-9–FR-12,\nFR-15, FR-18, FR-22–FR-24).\n\n**Decision.** Keep `package.json` as it is. Do not add an OTP/2FA library, a password-strength library\nsuch as zxcvbn, a form wizard, or a toast library. The strength checklist comes from the existing\n`passwordChecks()` and `PASSWORD_RULES` (FR-16). Toasts use the existing `useToast()` (NFR-6).\n\n**Consequences.** The lockfile does not change. All new code is pages, shell wiring, the seed, one\nmigration and tests.\n\n## ADR-2: Sign-in uses the client `signIn('credentials', { redirect: false })` from `next-auth/react`, with errors mapped from `result.code`\n\n**Context.** FR-2 requires a `/login` form that submits through Auth.js `signIn('credentials')`.\nFR-4 and FR-6 need the server's specific messages (locked N minutes, disabled, rate-limited) shown\nin an Alert. FR-7 requires the callbackUrl to be sanitised. NFR-5 requires aria-live errors and focus\non the first invalid field. NFR-11 requires RHF with `zodResolver(signInSchema)`. NFR-2 requires CSRF.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `/login/page.tsx` is a Server Component that reads `searchParams` and renders a client `LoginForm`.\n- `LoginForm` calls `signIn('credentials', { email, password, redirect: false })` from `next-auth/react`. In beta.25 the result carries `code` from `AuthError`. A small pure function, `loginErrorMessage(code)`, maps the codes. `locked:N` becomes \"This account is temporarily locked. Try again in N minutes.\" `disabled` becomes the disabled message. `rate_limited` becomes \"Too many attempts…\". Every other code becomes \"Email or password is incorrect.\" (FR-4, FR-6). Keep the function in the form file and export it for a unit test.\n- On success, call `router.replace(safeCallbackUrl(searchParams.callbackUrl))` and then `router.refresh()` (FR-7). The function already exists, and the server `redirect` callback enforces the same rule a second time.\n- The client `signIn` fetches the Auth.js CSRF token itself (NFR-2). There is no hand-rolled CSRF.\n- Do **not** use a Server Action with the server `signIn`. In beta.25 that throws `CredentialsSignin` through `redirect` plumbing, and the error is harder to surface. The client path is the documented way to show credential errors.\n\n**Consequences.** `next-auth/react` is part of the installed `next-auth`, so nothing is added. The client\ncall does not need a `SessionProvider`, because `signIn` and `signOut` from `next-auth/react` work\nwithout one.\n\n## ADR-3: The shell gets the current user from the server `auth()` through a prop; there is no `SessionProvider`\n\n**Context.** FR-25 says to delete `PLACEHOLDER_USER` and keep exactly one source of \"current user\".\nFR-26 needs the name, role badge, initials and Sign out. FR-27 needs `navigationForRole` to use the\nsession role. NFR-9 requires server enforcement. `getCurrentUser()` / `requirePageUser()` already exist (FR-23).\n\n**Decision.**\n- `src/app/(app)/layout.tsx` (already an async Server Component) calls `requirePageUser()`, which redirects to `/login` when there is no session. The middleware already handles that case (FR-11), so this is a second guard. The layout passes `user={{ id, firstName, lastName, role }}` to `AppShell`.\n- `AppShell` takes a `user` prop, uses `navigationForRole(user.role)`, and shows initials, the name and a role `Chip`. Its menu has an \"Account security\" `MenuItem` that links to `/account/security` (FR-21) and a \"Sign out\" item that calls `signOut({ callbackUrl: '/login' })` from `next-auth/react` (FR-26).\n- Do **not** add a `SessionProvider` or `useSession`. That would create a second \"current user\" source with its own polling and would go against FR-25.\n\n**Consequences.** The user is read once per navigation on the server, where a JWT decode costs almost\nnothing. Role or name changes appear after the next 5-minute recheck (FR-10), which is the same\nfreshness the JWT already has. Any shell test has to pass a `user` prop instead of mocking a session.\n\n## ADR-4: The token pages verify on the server first; set-password forms are client RHF forms that post to the existing JSON APIs\n\n**Context.** FR-13 says `/invite/[token]` must show the invalid-link message, or the name and email\nread-only. FR-14 says that after acceptance the user is signed in and sent to `/dashboard`. FR-19\ncovers `/reset-password/[token]` → `/login` with a success toast. FR-20 covers `/account/security`\n→ \"Password updated\", or a field error on a wrong current password. FR-17 covers `/forgot-password`\nwith a constant message. FR-12 and NFR-1 say tokens are only touched through `tokens.ts`. NFR-10 requires thin handlers.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `invite/[token]/page.tsx` and `reset-password/[token]/page.tsx` are Server Components. They call `verifyToken(params.token, 'INVITE' | 'PASSWORD_RESET')` from `tokens.ts` without consuming the token. If it returns null, they render the invalid-link Alert. Otherwise they render a client form with the user's email (and name, for invites). The raw token is passed only as a prop. It is never logged and never put in audit metadata (NFR-8).\n- Forms use RHF with `zodResolver(setPasswordSchema(email))` or `changePasswordFormSchema(email)`, so client and server share one schema (NFR-11). They `fetch` the existing routes: `POST /api/auth/accept-invite`, `/api/auth/reset-password`, `/api/auth/forgot-password` and `/api/account/password`. Server `VALIDATION_ERROR` `details` are mapped back with `setError` (FR-20 \"Current password is incorrect.\").\n- Invite success: call `signIn('credentials', { email, password, redirect: false })` with the password just set, then `router.replace('/dashboard')` (FR-14). The service already consumed the token, so a later visit shows the invalid-link message.\n- Reset success: `router.replace('/login?reset=1')`. `LoginForm` shows `useToast` \"Password updated, sign in with your new password\" once on mount when `reset=1` is present (FR-19).\n- `/forgot-password` always shows the same success Alert after any 2xx or 429 response, so there is no enumeration (FR-17).\n- One shared client component, `PasswordChecklist({ password, email })`, renders `PASSWORD_RULES` as an MUI `List` with CheckCircle and RadioButtonUnchecked icons plus text. Colour is never the only signal. It is used under every new-password field (FR-16).\n- Accessibility: one `Alert role=\"alert\"` / `aria-live=\"polite\"` region per form. Field helperText comes from RHF. RHF's default `shouldFocusError: true` moves focus to the first invalid field (NFR-5). No extra code is needed.\n\n**Consequences.** No new API routes and no new services. Only pages and client forms are added.\nVerifying the token on the server before render means that an expired link never shows the form.\n\n## ADR-5: Seed users are upserted with bcrypt cost 12; the invite link is logged through the logger\n\n**Context.** FR-28 requires six ACTIVE users with password `SchoolHub123!`, created idempotently. FR-29\nand FR-30 require one INVITED user whose invite link is logged. FR-31 says not to change `sendAuthEmail`.\nNFR-12 bans `console.log`.\n\n**Decision.**\n- In `prisma/seed.ts`, add a users block **before** the existing PRD-04 notification block, which skips users that don't exist. Hash once with `bcrypt.hash('SchoolHub123!', 12)`. Then call `prisma.user.upsert({ where: { email }, create: {..., status: 'ACTIVE', passwordHash, passwordChangedAt: now }, update: {} })` for each user. `update: {}` keeps re-runs idempotent and leaves edited users alone.\n- `invited@schoolhub.test`: upsert with `status: 'INVITED'` and `passwordHash: null`. Then call `createInviteToken(user.id)` from `services/users.ts` (it invalidates older invites) and `logger.info({ link: authLink('invite', raw) }, 'seed invite link')`. Call these only while the user is still INVITED. Do not call `sendAuthEmail`.\n\n**Consequences.** Each re-seed issues a fresh invite link and invalidates the previous one, which is the\nexpected dev behaviour. The bcrypt hash adds about 250 ms to the seed run.\n\n## ADR-6: Add `@@index([role, status])` in a new additive migration; keep `AuditLog.ip`\n\n**Context.** FR-33 says the PRD's User model declares `@@index([role, status])`. FR-32 says not to rename\nexisting fields, so `ip` stays. NFR-13 requires no renames.\n\n**Decision.** Add `@@index([role, status])` to `User`, and generate\n`prisma/migrations/2026110600000_user_role_status_index/` with `prisma migrate dev --create-only`.\nIt contains a single `CREATE INDEX`. `AuditLog.ip` does not change. FR-32 remains flagged for human sign-off.\n\n**Consequences.** The migration is additive and does not touch existing data. Use a migration\ntimestamp later than `20261105000000_timetable`.\n\n## ADR-7: Tests use the existing Vitest and Playwright setup; e2e reads reset links from EmailOutbox\n\n**Context.** FR-34 asks for integration tests for authorize, accept-invite, reset-password and\nchange-password, plus Playwright tests for sign-in/out, the failed-login message and forgot → reset.\nNFR-5 covers accessibility. NFR-13 requires `lint`, `typecheck` and `test` to stay green.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `tests/integration/auth.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node` and `describe.runIf(process.env.TEST_DATABASE_URL)`, the same as `email-dispatch.test.ts`. It calls `authorizeCredentials` and the services directly with a stub `Request`, covering each branch (invalid, disabled, locked:N, lockout at 5, rate_limited, success resets the count) and each service's happy path and token reuse. Call `__resetRateLimits()` in `beforeEach`.\n- `tests/unit/login-error-message.test.ts`: covers the `loginErrorMessage` code map from ADR-2.\n- `tests/e2e/auth.spec.ts`: covers sign-in as the seed admin, sign-out, the wrong-password message, and the redirect for an unauthenticated `/dashboard`. For forgot → reset, the spec reads the newest `EmailOutbox` row for the address through `@prisma/client` in the test process and extracts the `/reset-password/…` URL from the body. That way it needs no test-only API route in the app. Run `@axe-core/playwright` `AxeBuilder` once on `/login` (NFR-5).\n\n**Consequences.** No new tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed (ADR-5) and on `DATABASE_URL`\nbeing visible to the Playwright process. Because the specs change shared users (password reset,\nlockout), mark `auth.spec.ts` as `test.describe.configure({ mode: 'serial' })`. The\nreset flow should use `student2@` so that admin sign-in in parallel specs is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Downstream notes for `arch-design-system`\n\n- Intake blockers that system-design must surface: the brownfield \"design gaps only\" rule, FR-32 (`ip`, LOW confidence), FR-33 (additive index), FR-30/FR-31 (the seed logs the link, `sendAuthEmail` stays as it is) and NFR-6 (the mui-mcp lookup is mandatory).\n- No new API endpoints are implied. `api-contracts` should list the existing routes the new pages consume: `/api/auth/[...nextauth]`, `forgot-password`, `reset-password`, `accept-invite` and `/api/account/password`.\n- Do not modify `course-access.ts` or `student-access.ts` (NFR-13).\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0191GqWT3ybQYtFZ3trphWrG","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a9ff751b-b713-4810-9c30-1cad791c6970","uuid":"6928e272-7976-4514-aaf3-0d8bbf2e3e97","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:38:46.233Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions & RBAC (SCH-001)\n\nTicket: SH-1 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-05T11:45:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and `package.json`\nconfirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL,\nnext-auth 5.0.0-beta.25 (Credentials, JWT), bcrypt ^6, zod 3.23.8, react-hook-form 7.53.0,\nMUI 9.5.0 with emotion, Vitest 2.1.1, Playwright 1.47.2 and @axe-core/playwright 4.10.0.\nThe stack fits every FR and NFR. **This ticket adds no dependency** (NFR-7, NFR-13). The ADRs below\nrecord only the choices the requirements force for the gaps intake listed: the auth pages, the shell\nwiring, the seed, the index migration and the tests.\n\n**Workspace facts I checked** (they confirm intake or add detail to it):\n\n- `src/server/auth/credentials.ts`: `AuthError extends CredentialsSignin` and sets `.code` to one of `invalid`, `disabled`, `rate_limited` or `locked:<N>`. A `DUMMY_HASH` compare runs on every path, so NFR-3 is already met.\n- `src/server/auth/auth.ts` exports `handlers, auth, signIn, signOut, unstable_update`. `auth.config.ts` already sanitises redirects through `safeCallbackUrl` and sets `pages.signIn = '/login'`. The cookies are the Auth.js defaults: HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, `__Secure-` prefix in production (NFR-2).\n- **No `SessionProvider`, `useSession` or `next-auth/react` import exists anywhere in `src/`.** `src/app/(app)/layout.tsx` is an async Server Component, and it renders the client `AppShell` from `shell.tsx`, which still contains `PLACEHOLDER_USER` (lines 40, 78, 162–167).\n- `acceptInvite()` returns `{ email }` and does not sign the user in. `POST /api/account/password` already calls `unstable_update({ reauth: true })`.\n- `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports `signInSchema`, `forgotPasswordSchema`, `setPasswordSchema(email)`, `acceptInviteSchema`, `resetPasswordSchema`, `changePasswordFormSchema(email)`, `passwordChecks`, `PASSWORD_RULES` and `safeCallbackUrl`. These cover every form.\n- `src/server/auth/email.ts` exports `authLink(kind, rawToken)`. `sendAuthEmail` enqueues to EmailOutbox and never logs the link.\n- `prisma/schema.prisma`: `AuditLog.ip String?`. `User` has `@@index([lastName, firstName, email])` and does not have `@@index([role, status])`. The latest migration is `20261105000000_timetable`.\n- Tests: the unit tests for tokens, lockout, rate-limit, password-policy and auth-validators exist. `tests/integration/` has the `TEST_DATABASE_URL`-gated precedent `email-dispatch.test.ts`. `tests/e2e/` has no auth spec. `playwright.config.ts` runs `npm run start` against `APP_URL`.\n- The `mui-mcp` server is connected in this environment, so the NFR-6 blocker is clear for now. Dev must still call it before writing UI.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the declared stack unchanged and add no dependency\n\n**Context.** NFR-13 requires no scope expansion and no rebuild of the existing auth modules. NFR-7 bans\nextra UI libraries. Every backend piece the gaps need already exists (FR-1, FR-3, FR-5, FR-9–FR-12,\nFR-15, FR-18, FR-22–FR-24).\n\n**Decision.** Keep `package.json` as it is. Do not add an OTP/2FA library, a password-strength library\nsuch as zxcvbn, a form wizard, or a toast library. The strength checklist comes from the existing\n`passwordChecks()` and `PASSWORD_RULES` (FR-16). Toasts use the existing `useToast()` (NFR-6).\n\n**Consequences.** The lockfile does not change. All new code is pages, shell wiring, the seed, one\nmigration and tests.\n\n## ADR-2: Sign-in uses the client `signIn('credentials', { redirect: false })` from `next-auth/react`, with errors mapped from `result.code`\n\n**Context.** FR-2 requires a `/login` form that submits through Auth.js `signIn('credentials')`.\nFR-4 and FR-6 need the server's specific messages (locked N minutes, disabled, rate-limited) shown\nin an Alert. FR-7 requires the callbackUrl to be sanitised. NFR-5 requires aria-live errors and focus\non the first invalid field. NFR-11 requires RHF with `zodResolver(signInSchema)`. NFR-2 requires CSRF.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `/login/page.tsx` is a Server Component that reads `searchParams` and renders a client `LoginForm`.\n- `LoginForm` calls `signIn('credentials', { email, password, redirect: false })` from `next-auth/react`. In beta.25 the result carries `code` from `AuthError`. A small pure function, `loginErrorMessage(code)`, maps the codes. `locked:N` becomes \"This account is temporarily locked. Try again in N minutes.\" `disabled` becomes the disabled message. `rate_limited` becomes \"Too many attempts…\". Every other code becomes \"Email or password is incorrect.\" (FR-4, FR-6). Keep the function in the form file and export it for a unit test.\n- On success, call `router.replace(safeCallbackUrl(searchParams.callbackUrl))` and then `router.refresh()` (FR-7). The function already exists, and the server `redirect` callback enforces the same rule a second time.\n- The client `signIn` fetches the Auth.js CSRF token itself (NFR-2). There is no hand-rolled CSRF.\n- Do **not** use a Server Action with the server `signIn`. In beta.25 that throws `CredentialsSignin` through `redirect` plumbing, and the error is harder to surface. The client path is the documented way to show credential errors.\n\n**Consequences.** `next-auth/react` is part of the installed `next-auth`, so nothing is added. The client\ncall does not need a `SessionProvider`, because `signIn` and `signOut` from `next-auth/react` work\nwithout one.\n\n## ADR-3: The shell gets the current user from the server `auth()` through a prop; there is no `SessionProvider`\n\n**Context.** FR-25 says to delete `PLACEHOLDER_USER` and keep exactly one source of \"current user\".\nFR-26 needs the name, role badge, initials and Sign out. FR-27 needs `navigationForRole` to use the\nsession role. NFR-9 requires server enforcement. `getCurrentUser()` / `requirePageUser()` already exist (FR-23).\n\n**Decision.**\n- `src/app/(app)/layout.tsx` (already an async Server Component) calls `requirePageUser()`, which redirects to `/login` when there is no session. The middleware already handles that case (FR-11), so this is a second guard. The layout passes `user={{ id, firstName, lastName, role }}` to `AppShell`.\n- `AppShell` takes a `user` prop, uses `navigationForRole(user.role)`, and shows initials, the name and a role `Chip`. Its menu has an \"Account security\" `MenuItem` that links to `/account/security` (FR-21) and a \"Sign out\" item that calls `signOut({ callbackUrl: '/login' })` from `next-auth/react` (FR-26).\n- Do **not** add a `SessionProvider` or `useSession`. That would create a second \"current user\" source with its own polling and would go against FR-25.\n\n**Consequences.** The user is read once per navigation on the server, where a JWT decode costs almost\nnothing. Role or name changes appear after the next 5-minute recheck (FR-10), which is the same\nfreshness the JWT already has. Any shell test has to pass a `user` prop instead of mocking a session.\n\n## ADR-4: The token pages verify on the server first; set-password forms are client RHF forms that post to the existing JSON APIs\n\n**Context.** FR-13 says `/invite/[token]` must show the invalid-link message, or the name and email\nread-only. FR-14 says that after acceptance the user is signed in and sent to `/dashboard`. FR-19\ncovers `/reset-password/[token]` → `/login` with a success toast. FR-20 covers `/account/security`\n→ \"Password updated\", or a field error on a wrong current password. FR-17 covers `/forgot-password`\nwith a constant message. FR-12 and NFR-1 say tokens are only touched through `tokens.ts`. NFR-10 requires thin handlers.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `invite/[token]/page.tsx` and `reset-password/[token]/page.tsx` are Server Components. They call `verifyToken(params.token, 'INVITE' | 'PASSWORD_RESET')` from `tokens.ts` without consuming the token. If it returns null, they render the invalid-link Alert. Otherwise they render a client form with the user's email (and name, for invites). The raw token is passed only as a prop. It is never logged and never put in audit metadata (NFR-8).\n- Forms use RHF with `zodResolver(setPasswordSchema(email))` or `changePasswordFormSchema(email)`, so client and server share one schema (NFR-11). They `fetch` the existing routes: `POST /api/auth/accept-invite`, `/api/auth/reset-password`, `/api/auth/forgot-password` and `/api/account/password`. Server `VALIDATION_ERROR` `details` are mapped back with `setError` (FR-20 \"Current password is incorrect.\").\n- Invite success: call `signIn('credentials', { email, password, redirect: false })` with the password just set, then `router.replace('/dashboard')` (FR-14). The service already consumed the token, so a later visit shows the invalid-link message.\n- Reset success: `router.replace('/login?reset=1')`. `LoginForm` shows `useToast` \"Password updated, sign in with your new password\" once on mount when `reset=1` is present (FR-19).\n- `/forgot-password` always shows the same success Alert after any 2xx or 429 response, so there is no enumeration (FR-17).\n- One shared client component, `PasswordChecklist({ password, email })`, renders `PASSWORD_RULES` as an MUI `List` with CheckCircle and RadioButtonUnchecked icons plus text. Colour is never the only signal. It is used under every new-password field (FR-16).\n- Accessibility: one `Alert role=\"alert\"` / `aria-live=\"polite\"` region per form. Field helperText comes from RHF. RHF's default `shouldFocusError: true` moves focus to the first invalid field (NFR-5). No extra code is needed.\n\n**Consequences.** No new API routes and no new services. Only pages and client forms are added.\nVerifying the token on the server before render means that an expired link never shows the form.\n\n## ADR-5: Seed users are upserted with bcrypt cost 12; the invite link is logged through the logger\n\n**Context.** FR-28 requires six ACTIVE users with password `SchoolHub123!`, created idempotently. FR-29\nand FR-30 require one INVITED user whose invite link is logged. FR-31 says not to change `sendAuthEmail`.\nNFR-12 bans `console.log`.\n\n**Decision.**\n- In `prisma/seed.ts`, add a users block **before** the existing PRD-04 notification block, which skips users that don't exist. Hash once with `bcrypt.hash('SchoolHub123!', 12)`. Then call `prisma.user.upsert({ where: { email }, create: {..., status: 'ACTIVE', passwordHash, passwordChangedAt: now }, update: {} })` for each user. `update: {}` keeps re-runs idempotent and leaves edited users alone.\n- `invited@schoolhub.test`: upsert with `status: 'INVITED'` and `passwordHash: null`. Then call `createInviteToken(user.id)` from `services/users.ts` (it invalidates older invites) and `logger.info({ link: authLink('invite', raw) }, 'seed invite link')`. Call these only while the user is still INVITED. Do not call `sendAuthEmail`.\n\n**Consequences.** Each re-seed issues a fresh invite link and invalidates the previous one, which is the\nexpected dev behaviour. The bcrypt hash adds about 250 ms to the seed run.\n\n## ADR-6: Add `@@index([role, status])` in a new additive migration; keep `AuditLog.ip`\n\n**Context.** FR-33 says the PRD's User model declares `@@index([role, status])`. FR-32 says not to rename\nexisting fields, so `ip` stays. NFR-13 requires no renames.\n\n**Decision.** Add `@@index([role, status])` to `User`, and generate\n`prisma/migrations/2026110600000_user_role_status_index/` with `prisma migrate dev --create-only`.\nIt contains a single `CREATE INDEX`. `AuditLog.ip` does not change. FR-32 remains flagged for human sign-off.\n\n**Consequences.** The migration is additive and does not touch existing data. Use a migration\ntimestamp later than `20261105000000_timetable`.\n\n## ADR-7: Tests use the existing Vitest and Playwright setup; e2e reads reset links from EmailOutbox\n\n**Context.** FR-34 asks for integration tests for authorize, accept-invite, reset-password and\nchange-password, plus Playwright tests for sign-in/out, the failed-login message and forgot → reset.\nNFR-5 covers accessibility. NFR-13 requires `lint`, `typecheck` and `test` to stay green.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `tests/integration/auth.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node` and `describe.runIf(process.env.TEST_DATABASE_URL)`, the same as `email-dispatch.test.ts`. It calls `authorizeCredentials` and the services directly with a stub `Request`, covering each branch (invalid, disabled, locked:N, lockout at 5, rate_limited, success resets the count) and each service's happy path and token reuse. Call `__resetRateLimits()` in `beforeEach`.\n- `tests/unit/login-error-message.test.ts`: covers the `loginErrorMessage` code map from ADR-2.\n- `tests/e2e/auth.spec.ts`: covers sign-in as the seed admin, sign-out, the wrong-password message, and the redirect for an unauthenticated `/dashboard`. For forgot → reset, the spec reads the newest `EmailOutbox` row for the address through `@prisma/client` in the test process and extracts the `/reset-password/…` URL from the body. That way it needs no test-only API route in the app. Run `@axe-core/playwright` `AxeBuilder` once on `/login` (NFR-5).\n\n**Consequences.** No new tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed (ADR-5) and on `DATABASE_URL`\nbeing visible to the Playwright process. Because the specs change shared users (password reset,\nlockout), mark `auth.spec.ts` as `test.describe.configure({ mode: 'serial' })`. The\nreset flow should use `student2@` so that admin sign-in in parallel specs is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Downstream notes for `arch-design-system`\n\n- Intake blockers that system-design must surface: the brownfield \"design gaps only\" rule, FR-32 (`ip`, LOW confidence), FR-33 (additive index), FR-30/FR-31 (the seed logs the link, `sendAuthEmail` stays as it is) and NFR-6 (the mui-mcp lookup is mandatory).\n- No new API endpoints are implied. `api-contracts` should list the existing routes the new pages consume: `/api/auth/[...nextauth]`, `forgot-password`, `reset-password`, `accept-invite` and `/api/account/password`.\n- Do not modify `course-access.ts` or `student-access.ts` (NFR-13).\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":145,"newStart":1,"newLines":140,"lines":["-# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-06 Bell Schedule & Timetable (SCH-006)","+# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions & RBAC (SCH-001)"," ","-Ticket: SH-6 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-05T05:40:00Z · Status: complete","+Ticket: SH-1 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-05T11:45:00Z · Status: complete"," ","-**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and","-`package.json` confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict,","-Prisma 5.20 on PostgreSQL, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons, x-date-pickers, x-data-grid) with emotion,","-zod 3.23.8, date-fns 3.6.0 / date-fns-tz 3.2.0, Vitest 2.1.1 and Playwright 1.47.2.","-The stack fits every FR/NFR. **This PRD adds exactly one runtime dependency, `@dnd-kit/core`.**","-The ADRs below record only the choices the requirements force.","+**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and `package.json`","+confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL,","+next-auth 5.0.0-beta.25 (Credentials, JWT), bcrypt ^6, zod 3.23.8, react-hook-form 7.53.0,","+MUI 9.5.0 with emotion, Vitest 2.1.1, Playwright 1.47.2 and @axe-core/playwright 4.10.0.","+The stack fits every FR and NFR. **This ticket adds no dependency** (NFR-7, NFR-13). The ADRs below","+record only the choices the requirements force for the gaps intake listed: the auth pages, the shell","+wiring, the seed, the index migration and the tests."," "," **Workspace facts I checked** (they confirm intake or add detail to it):"," ","-- `@dnd-kit/core` is not installed. The current npm release is `6.3.1`, with peer deps `react >=16.8` and `react-dom >=16.8`, so it is compatible with React 18.3.1.","-- No SWR, React Query or TanStack package is installed. Client data fetching in the codebase is plain `fetch` plus local state.","-- `prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => …)` is already used (sections-service, courses-service). No code uses `isolationLevel` or handles `P2034` yet, so this PRD sets the precedent.","-- `src/lib/dates.ts` already exports `formatInSchoolTz(date, pattern, timezone)`, which wraps `formatInTimeZone`.","-- `.eslintrc` `no-restricted-imports` bans `@fullcalendar/*`, `react-datepicker`, `notistack`, `sonner`, `@radix-ui/*` and others. `@dnd-kit/*` is not banned, so the lint rule needs no change.","-- Vitest runs `tests/unit/**` and `tests/integration/**`. Integration tests are gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (precedent: `tests/integration/email-dispatch.test.ts`, which also tests a concurrent-race scenario).","-- No `@media print` or `GlobalStyles` print rule exists yet.","+- `src/server/auth/credentials.ts`: `AuthError extends CredentialsSignin` and sets `.code` to one of `invalid`, `disabled`, `rate_limited` or `locked:<N>`. A `DUMMY_HASH` compare runs on every path, so NFR-3 is already met.","+- `src/server/auth/auth.ts` exports `handlers, auth, signIn, signOut, unstable_update`. `auth.config.ts` already sanitises redirects through `safeCallbackUrl` and sets `pages.signIn = '/login'`. The cookies are the Auth.js defaults: HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, `__Secure-` prefix in production (NFR-2).","+- **No `SessionProvider`, `useSession` or `next-auth/react` import exists anywhere in `src/`.** `src/app/(app)/layout.tsx` is an async Server Component, and it renders the client `AppShell` from `shell.tsx`, which still contains `PLACEHOLDER_USER` (lines 40, 78, 162–167).","+- `acceptInvite()` returns `{ email }` and does not sign the user in. `POST /api/account/password` already calls `unstable_update({ reauth: true })`.","+- `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports `signInSchema`, `forgotPasswordSchema`, `setPasswordSchema(email)`, `acceptInviteSchema`, `resetPasswordSchema`, `changePasswordFormSchema(email)`, `passwordChecks`, `PASSWORD_RULES` and `safeCallbackUrl`. These cover every form.","+- `src/server/auth/email.ts` exports `authLink(kind, rawToken)`. `sendAuthEmail` enqueues to EmailOutbox and never logs the link.","+- `prisma/schema.prisma`: `AuditLog.ip String?`. `User` has `@@index([lastName, firstName, email])` and does not have `@@index([role, status])`. The latest migration is `20261105000000_timetable`.","+- Tests: the unit tests for tokens, lockout, rate-limit, password-policy and auth-validators exist. `tests/integration/` has the `TEST_DATABASE_URL`-gated precedent `email-dispatch.test.ts`. `tests/e2e/` has no auth spec. `playwright.config.ts` runs `npm run start` against `APP_URL`.","+- The `mui-mcp` server is connected in this environment, so the NFR-6 blocker is clear for now. Dev must still call it before writing UI."," "," ---"," ","-## ADR-1: Keep the declared stack; add `@dnd-kit/core@6.3.1` (exact pin) and nothing else","+## ADR-1: Keep the declared stack unchanged and add no dependency"," ","-**Context.** FR-23 requires drag-and-drop to use `@dnd-kit/core` only. NFR-6 allows that one","-non-visual dependency, plus `@dnd-kit/sortable` only if needed. FR-5 period reorder could","-justify sortable, but intake's default for FR-5 is up/down buttons.","+**Context.** NFR-13 requires no scope expansion and no rebuild of the existing auth modules. NFR-7 bans","+extra UI libraries. Every backend piece the gaps need already exists (FR-1, FR-3, FR-5, FR-9–FR-12,","+FR-15, FR-18, FR-22–FR-24)."," ","-**Decision.** Add `\"@dnd-kit/core\": \"6.3.1\"` with no caret, matching the repo's exact-pin","-convention (`\"@mui/material\": \"9.5.0\"`). Do **not** add `@dnd-kit/sortable`,","-`@dnd-kit/utilities` or `@dnd-kit/modifiers`. Period reorder (FR-5, FR-33 `POST /api/periods/reorder`)","-uses MUI `IconButton`s with ArrowUpward and ArrowDownward icons.","+**Decision.** Keep `package.json` as it is. Do not add an OTP/2FA library, a password-strength library","+such as zxcvbn, a form wizard, or a toast library. The strength checklist comes from the existing","+`passwordChecks()` and `PASSWORD_RULES` (FR-16). Toasts use the existing `useToast()` (NFR-6)."," ","-**Consequences.** One new lockfile entry. Leaving out sortable keeps the reorder logic","-server-side and simple. The grid uses only `DndContext`, `useDraggable`, `useDroppable`,","-`DragOverlay` and `PointerSensor`. The ESLint ban list stays as it is.","+**Consequences.** The lockfile does not change. All new code is pages, shell wiring, the seed, one","+migration and tests."," ","-## ADR-2: Store and compare times as zero-padded `'HH:MM'` strings; derive \"now\" with the existing date-fns-tz helper","+## ADR-2: Sign-in uses the client `signIn('credentials', { redirect: false })` from `next-auth/react`, with errors mapped from `result.code`"," ","-**Context.** FR-1 stores `startTime` and `endTime` as `'HH:MM'` strings in the school timezone.","-FR-6 needs overlap and end-after-start checks. FR-31 and FR-45 need the current period and","-today's ISO weekday in `SchoolSettings.timezone`. NFR-11 asks for tests that cover multiple timezones.","+**Context.** FR-2 requires a `/login` form that submits through Auth.js `signIn('credentials')`.","+FR-4 and FR-6 need the server's specific messages (locked N minutes, disabled, rate-limited) shown","+in an Alert. FR-7 requires the callbackUrl to be sanitised. NFR-5 requires aria-live errors and focus","+on the first invalid field. NFR-11 requires RHF with `zodResolver(signInSchema)`. NFR-2 requires CSRF."," "," **Decision.**","-- Zod (FR-39) enforces `/^([01]\\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\\d$/`. Zero-padded 24-hour strings sort and compare correctly as plain strings, so `a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end` is the overlap test (FR-6). No parsing to `Date` is needed.","-- \"Now\" is `formatInSchoolTz(now, 'HH:mm', tz)` and the ISO weekday is `Number(formatInSchoolTz(now, 'i', tz))` (1=Mon…7=Sun, matching FR-2). The `period.startTime <= nowHHMM < period.endTime` comparison is again a string comparison (FR-31, FR-26 Now/Next, FR-24 highlight).","-- `getScheduleForDate` (FR-30) also derives the weekday with `'i'` in the school timezone.","-- MUI X `TimeField` (FR-5) binds a `Date` value. The form converts with date-fns `format(d, 'HH:mm')` and `parse(s, 'HH:mm', ref)`. Both functions are already installed, and the field's `Date` is wall-clock only.","+- `/login/page.tsx` is a Server Component that reads `searchParams` and renders a client `LoginForm`.","+- `LoginForm` calls `signIn('credentials', { email, password, redirect: false })` from `next-auth/react`. In beta.25 the result carries `code` from `AuthError`. A small pure function, `loginErrorMessage(code)`, maps the codes. `locked:N` becomes \"This account is temporarily locked. Try again in N minutes.\" `disabled` becomes the disabled message. `rate_limited` becomes \"Too many attempts…\". Every other code becomes \"Email or password is incorrect.\" (FR-4, FR-6). Keep the function in the form file and export it for a unit test.","+- On success, call `router.replace(safeCallbackUrl(searchParams.callbackUrl))` and then `router.refresh()` (FR-7). The function already exists, and the server `redirect` callback enforces the same rule a second time.","+- The client `signIn` fetches the Auth.js CSRF token itself (NFR-2). There is no hand-rolled CSRF.","+- Do **not** use a Server Action with the server `signIn`. In beta.25 that throws `CredentialsSignin` through `redirect` plumbing, and the error is harder to surface. The client path is the documented way to show credential errors."," ","-**Consequences.** No new date library and no timezone arithmetic on stored times. Unit tests","-pass a fixed `now` and several `tz` values (e.g. `UTC`, `America/Los_Angeles`, `Asia/Kolkata`) into","-pure functions. Ceiling: periods cannot cross midnight. That is acceptable for a school day,","-and the endTime-after-startTime check already rejects them.","+**Consequences.** `next-auth/react` is part of the installed `next-auth`, so nothing is added. The client","+call does not need a `SessionProvider`, because `signIn` and `signOut` from `next-auth/react` work","+without one."," ","-## ADR-3: Clash check and write in one SERIALIZABLE interactive transaction with bounded retry on `P2034`","+## ADR-3: The shell gets the current user from the server `auth()` through a prop; there is no `SessionProvider`"," ","-**Context.** NFR-1 requires clash validation and the insert or update to run in one SERIALIZABLE","-transaction, with retry, so that two concurrent conflicting inserts yield exactly one success.","-NFR-12 needs an integration test for this. FR-37 requires that a clash saves nothing and returns 409.","+**Context.** FR-25 says to delete `PLACEHOLDER_USER` and keep exactly one source of \"current user\".","+FR-26 needs the name, role badge, initials and Sign out. FR-27 needs `navigationForRole` to use the","+session role. NFR-9 requires server enforcement. `getCurrentUser()` / `requirePageUser()` already exist (FR-23)."," "," **Decision.**","-- Use `prisma.$transaction(fn, { isolationLevel: Prisma.TransactionIsolationLevel.Serializable })`, which Prisma 5.20 supports natively on PostgreSQL.","-- Retry the whole transaction up to 3 attempts when the error is `PrismaClientKnownRequestError` with `code === 'P2034'` (write conflict or deadlock). After the last attempt, rethrow so `withHandler` maps it to a 500. The retry loop is a small local function inside `src/server/timetable/timetable-service.ts`. Do not add a shared util until a second caller exists (NFR-9).","-- Clashes found inside the transaction throw `AppError('CONFLICT', msg, { clashes })` from `src/server/http.ts`. The throw rolls the transaction back, so nothing is saved (FR-37, NFR-8).","-- The FR-3 `@@unique([courseId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` stays as a last line of defence. Map `P2002` to 409 as well.","-- Teaching-day switch-off (FR-7) and period reorder (FR-5) also use one transaction, at the default isolation level. Reorder needs a two-phase write because `Period.order @unique` would collide mid-update: first set each order to `-(i+1)`, then to `i+1`.","+- `src/app/(app)/layout.tsx` (already an async Server Component) calls `requirePageUser()`, which redirects to `/login` when there is no session. The middleware already handles that case (FR-11), so this is a second guard. The layout passes `user={{ id, firstName, lastName, role }}` to `AppShell`.","+- `AppShell` takes a `user` prop, uses `navigationForRole(user.role)`, and shows initials, the name and a role `Chip`. Its menu has an \"Account security\" `MenuItem` that links to `/account/security` (FR-21) and a \"Sign out\" item that calls `signOut({ callbackUrl: '/login' })` from `next-auth/react` (FR-26).","+- Do **not** add a `SessionProvider` or `useSession`. That would create a second \"current user\" source with its own polling and would go against FR-25."," ","-**Consequences.** Clash rules (FR-9–FR-13) must run their reads through `tx`, not the global","-`prisma`, or SERIALIZABLE protects nothing. `validateSlot` therefore takes a client parameter","-that defaults to `prisma`, so FR-36 availability can reuse the same logic outside a write.","-The NFR-12 test follows the `email-dispatch.test.ts` pattern: `describe.runIf(TEST_DATABASE_URL)`,","-then `Promise.allSettled` of two conflicting creates, asserting exactly one is fulfilled.","+**Consequences.** The user is read once per navigation on the server, where a JWT decode costs almost","+nothing. Role or name changes appear after the next 5-minute recheck (FR-10), which is the same","+freshness the JWT already has. Any shell test has to pass a `user` prop instead of mocking a session."," ","-## ADR-4: Clash detection loads candidate slots with set-based Prisma queries and checks them in memory (no raw SQL)","+## ADR-4: The token pages verify on the server first; set-password forms are client RHF forms that post to the existing JSON APIs"," ","-**Context.** FR-9–FR-14 define four clash rules plus the non-class period and non-teaching day","-rule, and `validateSlot` must return all of them. FR-36 availability must cover every","-(day, period) cell for one course in under 300 ms (NFR-3). The section week must load in under","-400 ms (NFR-2). The FR-3 index `@@index([academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` already exists for this.","+**Context.** FR-13 says `/invite/[token]` must show the invalid-link message, or the name and email","+read-only. FR-14 says that after acceptance the user is signed in and sent to `/dashboard`. FR-19","+covers `/reset-password/[token]` → `/login` with a success toast. FR-20 covers `/account/security`","+→ \"Password updated\", or a field error on a wrong current password. FR-17 covers `/forgot-password`","+with a constant message. FR-12 and NFR-1 say tokens are only touched through `tokens.ts`. NFR-10 requires thin handlers."," "," **Decision.**","-- **validateSlot** (single cell): one `findMany` of the other slots at `(academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId)`, excluding the moving slot's id on PATCH (FR-14). The query includes `course { teacherId, coTeacherIds, sectionId, code, subject, section }`, `room` and `period`. The teacher, room and section rules (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11) are array checks in TypeScript. Student overlap (FR-12) is one `enrollment.groupBy` / `count` over `status: 'ACTIVE'`, with `courseId in [otherCourseIds]` and `studentId in` the new course's active student ids. Both are covered by the existing `@@index([studentId, status])`.","-- **Availability** (FR-36): load all slots in the course's academic year in one query, and the new course's ACTIVE student ids plus the ACTIVE enrollments of those students in one query. Then run the same pure rule functions for each of the up-to-84 cells (7 × 12) in memory. This is a fixed number of queries, not one per cell.","-- The rule functions are pure (`(candidate, others, overlapCounts) => string[]`), so NFR-11 unit tests need no database.","-- **Editor and personal grids** (FR-35, FR-38): one `findMany` per view with `include` (course → subject, teacher; room; period). There are no N+1 queries.","+- `invite/[token]/page.tsx` and `reset-password/[token]/page.tsx` are Server Components. They call `verifyToken(params.token, 'INVITE' | 'PASSWORD_RESET')` from `tokens.ts` without consuming the token. If it returns null, they render the invalid-link Alert. Otherwise they render a client form with the user's email (and name, for invites). The raw token is passed only as a prop. It is never logged and never put in audit metadata (NFR-8).","+- Forms use RHF with `zodResolver(setPasswordSchema(email))` or `changePasswordFormSchema(email)`, so client and server share one schema (NFR-11). They `fetch` the existing routes: `POST /api/auth/accept-invite`, `/api/auth/reset-password`, `/api/auth/forgot-password` and `/api/account/password`. Server `VALIDATION_ERROR` `details` are mapped back with `setError` (FR-20 \"Current password is incorrect.\").","+- Invite success: call `signIn('credentials', { email, password, redirect: false })` with the password just set, then `router.replace('/dashboard')` (FR-14). The service already consumed the token, so a later visit shows the invalid-link message.","+- Reset success: `router.replace('/login?reset=1')`. `LoginForm` shows `useToast` \"Password updated, sign in with your new password\" once on mount when `reset=1` is present (FR-19).","+- `/forgot-password` always shows the same success Alert after any 2xx or 429 response, so there is no enumeration (FR-17).","+- One shared client component, `PasswordChecklist({ password, email })`, renders `PASSWORD_RULES` as an MUI `List` with CheckCircle and RadioButtonUnchecked icons plus text. Colour is never the only signal. It is used under every new-password field (FR-16).","+- Accessibility: one `Alert role=\"alert\"` / `aria-live=\"polite\"` region per form. Field helperText comes from RHF. RHF's default `shouldFocusError: true` moves focus to the first invalid field (NFR-5). No extra code is needed."," ","-**Consequences.** No `$queryRaw`, so everything stays type-safe under strict TS (NFR-10).","-Ceiling: availability does its work in memory over the year's slots, roughly a few thousand rows","-for a single school (NFR-16), which fits the target comfortably.","-`// ponytail: in-memory clash map over the year's slots; move to SQL aggregation if slot count reaches ~50k.`","+**Consequences.** No new API routes and no new services. Only pages and client forms are added.","+Verifying the token on the server before render means that an expired link never shows the form."," ","-## ADR-5: The drag-and-drop grid uses `@dnd-kit/core` with pessimistic updates; keyboard users get an MUI Dialog path","+## ADR-5: Seed users are upserted with bcrypt cost 12; the invite link is logged through the logger"," ","-**Context.** FR-16–FR-23 describe the editor grid. FR-20 requires that a rejected drop snaps","-back. FR-19 tints clashing cells while dragging, and NFR-4 says colour must never be the only signal.","-FR-22 and NFR-4 require a keyboard alternative. NFR-5 and NFR-6 allow MUI-only visuals.","+**Context.** FR-28 requires six ACTIVE users with password `SchoolHub123!`, created idempotently. FR-29","+and FR-30 require one INVITED user whose invite link is logged. FR-31 says not to change `sendAuthEmail`.","+NFR-12 bans `console.log`."," "," **Decision.**","-- `DndContext` with `PointerSensor` only (activation distance about 5px, so a click still opens the FR-21 room Popover). Cells use `useDroppable`. BREAK/LUNCH rows and non-teaching days set `disabled: true` (FR-16). Course cards and placed slots use `useDraggable`. The drag preview is a `DragOverlay` holding an MUI `Paper` (FR-23).","-- **Pessimistic updates.** The grid changes only after the POST or PATCH succeeds. On a 409, nothing moves, so \"snap back\" (FR-20) needs no rollback code. The toast comes from `useToast().showToast({ message, severity: 'error' })` with `details.clashes` joined into the message.","-- The clash map from `GET /api/timetable/availability` is fetched once when a drag starts, cached per courseId while the editor is open, and dropped after any mutation. Cells in the map get a red tint, an ErrorOutline icon and an MUI `Tooltip` listing the messages (FR-19, NFR-4).","-- Keyboard: cells are `tabIndex=0`. Enter on an empty cell opens an MUI `Dialog` listing the section's courses (FR-22). Delete on a focused slot opens the existing `ConfirmDialog` (FR-18). dnd-kit's `KeyboardSensor` is **not** used, because the Dialog path is the required alternative and it avoids the cost of making sensor announcements accessible.","-- Data fetching uses plain `fetch` plus `useState`, as elsewhere in the repo. No SWR or React Query is added.","+- In `prisma/seed.ts`, add a users block **before** the existing PRD-04 notification block, which skips users that don't exist. Hash once with `bcrypt.hash('SchoolHub123!', 12)`. Then call `prisma.user.upsert({ where: { email }, create: {..., status: 'ACTIVE', passwordHash, passwordChangedAt: now }, update: {} })` for each user. `update: {}` keeps re-runs idempotent and leaves edited users alone.","+- `invited@schoolhub.test`: upsert with `status: 'INVITED'` and `passwordHash: null`. Then call `createInviteToken(user.id)` from `services/users.ts` (it invalidates older invites) and `logger.info({ link: authLink('invite', raw) }, 'seed invite link')`. Call these only while the user is still INVITED. Do not call `sendAuthEmail`."," ","-**Consequences.** Each drop costs one round-trip of latency (no optimistic UI). That is acceptable","-for an admin tool, and it removes a whole class of state-desync bugs. Every component must still","-be looked up through mui-mcp before use (NFR-5). The mui-mcp server is connected in this","-environment, so the intake blocker is cleared for now, but dev must still call it.","+**Consequences.** Each re-seed issues a fresh invite link and invalidates the previous one, which is the","+expected dev behaviour. The bcrypt hash adds about 250 ms to the seed run."," ","-## ADR-6: Print layout uses native `@media print` and `window.print()`, with no PDF or print library","+## ADR-6: Add `@@index([role, status])` in a new additive migration; keep `AuditLog.ip`"," ","-**Context.** FR-29 requires a print-friendly A4 landscape page containing only the grid, the entity","-name and the academic year. NFR-16 requires print to work in the last two versions of Chrome, Edge,","-Firefox and Safari. NFR-6 bans extra UI libraries.","+**Context.** FR-33 says the PRD's User model declares `@@index([role, status])`. FR-32 says not to rename","+existing fields, so `ip` stays. NFR-13 requires no renames."," ","-**Decision.** The Print button calls `window.print()`. An MUI `GlobalStyles` block, rendered with","-the grid, contains `@page { size: A4 landscape; margin: 10mm }`. Under `@media print`, an `sx` rule","-hides the app shell (nav drawer, app bar, sidebar, view selectors and buttons), and a print-only","-heading shows the entity name and academic year. Cells use `break-inside: avoid`. Cell background","-colours need `print-color-adjust: exact`. Course code and teacher text carry the meaning anyway,","-so colour is not required (NFR-4).","+**Decision.** Add `@@index([role, status])` to `User`, and generate","+`prisma/migrations/2026110600000_user_role_status_index/` with `prisma migrate dev --create-only`.","+It contains a single `CREATE INDEX`. `AuditLog.ip` does not change. FR-32 remains flagged for human sign-off."," ","-**Consequences.** No dependency is added. Firefox and Safari ignore some `@page` size hints,","-which is acceptable because the user picks the orientation in the print dialog. The Playwright","-check uses `page.emulateMedia({ media: 'print' })` and asserts that the shell is hidden.","+**Consequences.** The migration is additive and does not touch existing data. Use a migration","+timestamp later than `20261105000000_timetable`."," ","-## ADR-7: Testing stays on the existing Vitest and Playwright setup","+## ADR-7: Tests use the existing Vitest and Playwright setup; e2e reads reset links from EmailOutbox"," ","-**Context.** NFR-11 (unit tests), NFR-12 (concurrency integration test), NFR-13 (e2e test) and NFR-10 (quality gate).","+**Context.** FR-34 asks for integration tests for authorize, accept-invite, reset-password and","+change-password, plus Playwright tests for sign-in/out, the failed-login message and forgot → reset.","+NFR-5 covers accessibility. NFR-13 requires `lint`, `typecheck` and `test` to stay green."," "," **Decision.**","-- `tests/unit/timetable-clash.test.ts`: the pure rule functions from ADR-4, period overlap validation (FR-6) and `getCurrentSlot`/Now-Next with injected `now` and `tz` (ADR-2).","-- `tests/integration/timetable-concurrency.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node`, gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (ADR-3).","-- `tests/e2e/timetable.spec.ts`: Playwright tests for dragging a course into a cell, a clash toast on a conflicting drop, and the student Today view, all against the FR-41–FR-44 seed. dnd-kit's PointerSensor works with Playwright `mouse.down/move/up` when there are intermediate `move` steps. `locator.dragTo` alone may not cross the activation distance.","+- `tests/integration/auth.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node` and `describe.runIf(process.env.TEST_DATABASE_URL)`, the same as `email-dispatch.test.ts`. It calls `authorizeCredentials` and the services directly with a stub `Request`, covering each branch (invalid, disabled, locked:N, lockout at 5, rate_limited, success resets the count) and each service's happy path and token reuse. Call `__resetRateLimits()` in `beforeEach`.","+- `tests/unit/login-error-message.test.ts`: covers the `loginErrorMessage` code map from ADR-2.","+- `tests/e2e/auth.spec.ts`: covers sign-in as the seed admin, sign-out, the wrong-password message, and the redirect for an unauthenticated `/dashboard`. For forgot → reset, the spec reads the newest `EmailOutbox` row for the address through `@prisma/client` in the test process and extracts the `/reset-password/…` URL from the body. That way it needs no test-only API route in the app. Run `@axe-core/playwright` `AxeBuilder` once on `/login` (NFR-5)."," ","-**Consequences.** No new test tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed fixtures (FR-44) being idempotent.","+**Consequences.** No new tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed (ADR-5) and on `DATABASE_URL`","+being visible to the Playwright process. Because the specs change shared users (password reset,","+lockout), mark `auth.spec.ts` as `test.describe.configure({ mode: 'serial' })`. The","+reset flow should use `student2@` so that admin sign-in in parallel specs is not affected."," "," ---"," ","-## Downstream notes (no stack impact; for `arch-design-system` to decide)","+## Downstream notes for `arch-design-system`"," ","-- FR-21 free rooms: no stack choice is needed. Either intake's suggested `GET /api/timetable/free-rooms` or the availability payload works with ADR-4's query pattern. One query (rooms not archived, minus `roomId`s used at that day and period) is enough.","-- FR-7 slot count: intake's default (`GET /api/school-days` returns `slotCount` per day) is one `timetableSlot.groupBy({ by: ['dayOfWeek'] })`, which works fine with this stack.","-- FR-40 audit: `audit()` from `src/server/audit.ts` should be called inside the same transaction client where its signature allows. That is for design to confirm.","+- Intake blockers that system-design must surface: the brownfield \"design gaps only\" rule, FR-32 (`ip`, LOW confidence), FR-33 (additive index), FR-30/FR-31 (the seed logs the link, `sendAuthEmail` stays as it is) and NFR-6 (the mui-mcp lookup is mandatory).","+- No new API endpoints are implied. `api-contracts` should list the existing routes the new pages consume: `/api/auth/[...nextauth]`, `forgot-password`, `reset-password`, `accept-invite` and `/api/account/password`.","+- Do not modify `course-access.ts` or `student-access.ts` (NFR-13)."]}],"originalFile":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-06 Bell Schedule & Timetable (SCH-006)\n\nTicket: SH-6 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-05T05:40:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and\n`package.json` confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict,\nPrisma 5.20 on PostgreSQL, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons, x-date-pickers, x-data-grid) with emotion,\nzod 3.23.8, date-fns 3.6.0 / date-fns-tz 3.2.0, Vitest 2.1.1 and Playwright 1.47.2.\nThe stack fits every FR/NFR. **This PRD adds exactly one runtime dependency, `@dnd-kit/core`.**\nThe ADRs below record only the choices the requirements force.\n\n**Workspace facts I checked** (they confirm intake or add detail to it):\n\n- `@dnd-kit/core` is not installed. The current npm release is `6.3.1`, with peer deps `react >=16.8` and `react-dom >=16.8`, so it is compatible with React 18.3.1.\n- No SWR, React Query or TanStack package is installed. Client data fetching in the codebase is plain `fetch` plus local state.\n- `prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => …)` is already used (sections-service, courses-service). No code uses `isolationLevel` or handles `P2034` yet, so this PRD sets the precedent.\n- `src/lib/dates.ts` already exports `formatInSchoolTz(date, pattern, timezone)`, which wraps `formatInTimeZone`.\n- `.eslintrc` `no-restricted-imports` bans `@fullcalendar/*`, `react-datepicker`, `notistack`, `sonner`, `@radix-ui/*` and others. `@dnd-kit/*` is not banned, so the lint rule needs no change.\n- Vitest runs `tests/unit/**` and `tests/integration/**`. Integration tests are gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (precedent: `tests/integration/email-dispatch.test.ts`, which also tests a concurrent-race scenario).\n- No `@media print` or `GlobalStyles` print rule exists yet.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the declared stack; add `@dnd-kit/core@6.3.1` (exact pin) and nothing else\n\n**Context.** FR-23 requires drag-and-drop to use `@dnd-kit/core` only. NFR-6 allows that one\nnon-visual dependency, plus `@dnd-kit/sortable` only if needed. FR-5 period reorder could\njustify sortable, but intake's default for FR-5 is up/down buttons.\n\n**Decision.** Add `\"@dnd-kit/core\": \"6.3.1\"` with no caret, matching the repo's exact-pin\nconvention (`\"@mui/material\": \"9.5.0\"`). Do **not** add `@dnd-kit/sortable`,\n`@dnd-kit/utilities` or `@dnd-kit/modifiers`. Period reorder (FR-5, FR-33 `POST /api/periods/reorder`)\nuses MUI `IconButton`s with ArrowUpward and ArrowDownward icons.\n\n**Consequences.** One new lockfile entry. Leaving out sortable keeps the reorder logic\nserver-side and simple. The grid uses only `DndContext`, `useDraggable`, `useDroppable`,\n`DragOverlay` and `PointerSensor`. The ESLint ban list stays as it is.\n\n## ADR-2: Store and compare times as zero-padded `'HH:MM'` strings; derive \"now\" with the existing date-fns-tz helper\n\n**Context.** FR-1 stores `startTime` and `endTime` as `'HH:MM'` strings in the school timezone.\nFR-6 needs overlap and end-after-start checks. FR-31 and FR-45 need the current period and\ntoday's ISO weekday in `SchoolSettings.timezone`. NFR-11 asks for tests that cover multiple timezones.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Zod (FR-39) enforces `/^([01]\\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\\d$/`. Zero-padded 24-hour strings sort and compare correctly as plain strings, so `a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end` is the overlap test (FR-6). No parsing to `Date` is needed.\n- \"Now\" is `formatInSchoolTz(now, 'HH:mm', tz)` and the ISO weekday is `Number(formatInSchoolTz(now, 'i', tz))` (1=Mon…7=Sun, matching FR-2). The `period.startTime <= nowHHMM < period.endTime` comparison is again a string comparison (FR-31, FR-26 Now/Next, FR-24 highlight).\n- `getScheduleForDate` (FR-30) also derives the weekday with `'i'` in the school timezone.\n- MUI X `TimeField` (FR-5) binds a `Date` value. The form converts with date-fns `format(d, 'HH:mm')` and `parse(s, 'HH:mm', ref)`. Both functions are already installed, and the field's `Date` is wall-clock only.\n\n**Consequences.** No new date library and no timezone arithmetic on stored times. Unit tests\npass a fixed `now` and several `tz` values (e.g. `UTC`, `America/Los_Angeles`, `Asia/Kolkata`) into\npure functions. Ceiling: periods cannot cross midnight. That is acceptable for a school day,\nand the endTime-after-startTime check already rejects them.\n\n## ADR-3: Clash check and write in one SERIALIZABLE interactive transaction with bounded retry on `P2034`\n\n**Context.** NFR-1 requires clash validation and the insert or update to run in one SERIALIZABLE\ntransaction, with retry, so that two concurrent conflicting inserts yield exactly one success.\nNFR-12 needs an integration test for this. FR-37 requires that a clash saves nothing and returns 409.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Use `prisma.$transaction(fn, { isolationLevel: Prisma.TransactionIsolationLevel.Serializable })`, which Prisma 5.20 supports natively on PostgreSQL.\n- Retry the whole transaction up to 3 attempts when the error is `PrismaClientKnownRequestError` with `code === 'P2034'` (write conflict or deadlock). After the last attempt, rethrow so `withHandler` maps it to a 500. The retry loop is a small local function inside `src/server/timetable/timetable-service.ts`. Do not add a shared util until a second caller exists (NFR-9).\n- Clashes found inside the transaction throw `AppError('CONFLICT', msg, { clashes })` from `src/server/http.ts`. The throw rolls the transaction back, so nothing is saved (FR-37, NFR-8).\n- The FR-3 `@@unique([courseId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` stays as a last line of defence. Map `P2002` to 409 as well.\n- Teaching-day switch-off (FR-7) and period reorder (FR-5) also use one transaction, at the default isolation level. Reorder needs a two-phase write because `Period.order @unique` would collide mid-update: first set each order to `-(i+1)`, then to `i+1`.\n\n**Consequences.** Clash rules (FR-9–FR-13) must run their reads through `tx`, not the global\n`prisma`, or SERIALIZABLE protects nothing. `validateSlot` therefore takes a client parameter\nthat defaults to `prisma`, so FR-36 availability can reuse the same logic outside a write.\nThe NFR-12 test follows the `email-dispatch.test.ts` pattern: `describe.runIf(TEST_DATABASE_URL)`,\nthen `Promise.allSettled` of two conflicting creates, asserting exactly one is fulfilled.\n\n## ADR-4: Clash detection loads candidate slots with set-based Prisma queries and checks them in memory (no raw SQL)\n\n**Context.** FR-9–FR-14 define four clash rules plus the non-class period and non-teaching day\nrule, and `validateSlot` must return all of them. FR-36 availability must cover every\n(day, period) cell for one course in under 300 ms (NFR-3). The section week must load in under\n400 ms (NFR-2). The FR-3 index `@@index([academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` already exists for this.\n\n**Decision.**\n- **validateSlot** (single cell): one `findMany` of the other slots at `(academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId)`, excluding the moving slot's id on PATCH (FR-14). The query includes `course { teacherId, coTeacherIds, sectionId, code, subject, section }`, `room` and `period`. The teacher, room and section rules (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11) are array checks in TypeScript. Student overlap (FR-12) is one `enrollment.groupBy` / `count` over `status: 'ACTIVE'`, with `courseId in [otherCourseIds]` and `studentId in` the new course's active student ids. Both are covered by the existing `@@index([studentId, status])`.\n- **Availability** (FR-36): load all slots in the course's academic year in one query, and the new course's ACTIVE student ids plus the ACTIVE enrollments of those students in one query. Then run the same pure rule functions for each of the up-to-84 cells (7 × 12) in memory. This is a fixed number of queries, not one per cell.\n- The rule functions are pure (`(candidate, others, overlapCounts) => string[]`), so NFR-11 unit tests need no database.\n- **Editor and personal grids** (FR-35, FR-38): one `findMany` per view with `include` (course → subject, teacher; room; period). There are no N+1 queries.\n\n**Consequences.** No `$queryRaw`, so everything stays type-safe under strict TS (NFR-10).\nCeiling: availability does its work in memory over the year's slots, roughly a few thousand rows\nfor a single school (NFR-16), which fits the target comfortably.\n`// ponytail: in-memory clash map over the year's slots; move to SQL aggregation if slot count reaches ~50k.`\n\n## ADR-5: The drag-and-drop grid uses `@dnd-kit/core` with pessimistic updates; keyboard users get an MUI Dialog path\n\n**Context.** FR-16–FR-23 describe the editor grid. FR-20 requires that a rejected drop snaps\nback. FR-19 tints clashing cells while dragging, and NFR-4 says colour must never be the only signal.\nFR-22 and NFR-4 require a keyboard alternative. NFR-5 and NFR-6 allow MUI-only visuals.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `DndContext` with `PointerSensor` only (activation distance about 5px, so a click still opens the FR-21 room Popover). Cells use `useDroppable`. BREAK/LUNCH rows and non-teaching days set `disabled: true` (FR-16). Course cards and placed slots use `useDraggable`. The drag preview is a `DragOverlay` holding an MUI `Paper` (FR-23).\n- **Pessimistic updates.** The grid changes only after the POST or PATCH succeeds. On a 409, nothing moves, so \"snap back\" (FR-20) needs no rollback code. The toast comes from `useToast().showToast({ message, severity: 'error' })` with `details.clashes` joined into the message.\n- The clash map from `GET /api/timetable/availability` is fetched once when a drag starts, cached per courseId while the editor is open, and dropped after any mutation. Cells in the map get a red tint, an ErrorOutline icon and an MUI `Tooltip` listing the messages (FR-19, NFR-4).\n- Keyboard: cells are `tabIndex=0`. Enter on an empty cell opens an MUI `Dialog` listing the section's courses (FR-22). Delete on a focused slot opens the existing `ConfirmDialog` (FR-18). dnd-kit's `KeyboardSensor` is **not** used, because the Dialog path is the required alternative and it avoids the cost of making sensor announcements accessible.\n- Data fetching uses plain `fetch` plus `useState`, as elsewhere in the repo. No SWR or React Query is added.\n\n**Consequences.** Each drop costs one round-trip of latency (no optimistic UI). That is acceptable\nfor an admin tool, and it removes a whole class of state-desync bugs. Every component must still\nbe looked up through mui-mcp before use (NFR-5). The mui-mcp server is connected in this\nenvironment, so the intake blocker is cleared for now, but dev must still call it.\n\n## ADR-6: Print layout uses native `@media print` and `window.print()`, with no PDF or print library\n\n**Context.** FR-29 requires a print-friendly A4 landscape page containing only the grid, the entity\nname and the academic year. NFR-16 requires print to work in the last two versions of Chrome, Edge,\nFirefox and Safari. NFR-6 bans extra UI libraries.\n\n**Decision.** The Print button calls `window.print()`. An MUI `GlobalStyles` block, rendered with\nthe grid, contains `@page { size: A4 landscape; margin: 10mm }`. Under `@media print`, an `sx` rule\nhides the app shell (nav drawer, app bar, sidebar, view selectors and buttons), and a print-only\nheading shows the entity name and academic year. Cells use `break-inside: avoid`. Cell background\ncolours need `print-color-adjust: exact`. Course code and teacher text carry the meaning anyway,\nso colour is not required (NFR-4).\n\n**Consequences.** No dependency is added. Firefox and Safari ignore some `@page` size hints,\nwhich is acceptable because the user picks the orientation in the print dialog. The Playwright\ncheck uses `page.emulateMedia({ media: 'print' })` and asserts that the shell is hidden.\n\n## ADR-7: Testing stays on the existing Vitest and Playwright setup\n\n**Context.** NFR-11 (unit tests), NFR-12 (concurrency integration test), NFR-13 (e2e test) and NFR-10 (quality gate).\n\n**Decision.**\n- `tests/unit/timetable-clash.test.ts`: the pure rule functions from ADR-4, period overlap validation (FR-6) and `getCurrentSlot`/Now-Next with injected `now` and `tz` (ADR-2).\n- `tests/integration/timetable-concurrency.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node`, gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (ADR-3).\n- `tests/e2e/timetable.spec.ts`: Playwright tests for dragging a course into a cell, a clash toast on a conflicting drop, and the student Today view, all against the FR-41–FR-44 seed. dnd-kit's PointerSensor works with Playwright `mouse.down/move/up` when there are intermediate `move` steps. `locator.dragTo` alone may not cross the activation distance.\n\n**Consequences.** No new test tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed fixtures (FR-44) being idempotent.\n\n---\n\n## Downstream notes (no stack impact; for `arch-design-system` to decide)\n\n- FR-21 free rooms: no stack choice is needed. Either intake's suggested `GET /api/timetable/free-rooms` or the availability payload works with ADR-4's query pattern. One query (rooms not archived, minus `roomId`s used at that day and period) is enough.\n- FR-7 slot count: intake's default (`GET /api/school-days` returns `slotCount` per day) is one `timetableSlot.groupBy({ by: ['dayOfWeek'] })`, which works fine with this stack.\n- FR-40 audit: `audit()` from `src/server/audit.ts` should be called inside the same transaction client where its signature allows. That is for design to confirm.\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0191GqWT3ybQYtFZ3trphWrG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
