{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnB6VbXFuAUYEm5acEuV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C4MM1QDpYnQNZ1tnbtUL5J","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions: PRD-08 File Storage & Course Content (SCH-008)\n\nTicket: SH-8 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-06T06:30:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and the workspace confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict (`moduleResolution: bundler`), Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL 16, Auth.js v5 beta.25, Zod 3.23.8, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons-material, material-nextjs, x-data-grid, x-date-pickers with `AdapterDateFns` already in `src/app/providers.tsx`, x-charts), @dnd-kit/core 6.3.1, date-fns/date-fns-tz, Vitest 2.1.1 (jsdom) and Playwright 1.47.2. `package.json` declares `engines.node >=18.18.0`, and local Node is 24.11.1. Nothing in the requirements calls for a different stack. The ADRs below cover how this feature fits into the existing stack and pin the new dependencies.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack and add only the five PRD-allowed dependencies, with pinned versions\n\n**Context.** NFR-9 limits new dependencies to file-type, react-markdown, remark-gfm, rehype-sanitize and @dnd-kit/sortable. The latest file-type (22.x) declares `engines.node >=22` and 21.x declares `>=20`. Either would break the repo's `>=18.18.0` engine contract. All five packages are ESM-only.\n\n**Decision.** Install these exact pins:\n| Package | Version | Why this version |\n|---|---|---|\n| `file-type` | `20.5.0` | Newest line with `engines.node >=18`. Use `fileTypeFromBuffer` (FR-5, FR-6). |\n| `react-markdown` | `10.1.0` | Peer is `react >=18` (FR-31). |\n| `remark-gfm` | `4.0.1` | Supports tables and strikethrough for FR-25 and FR-31. |\n| `rehype-sanitize` | `6.0.0` | Provides NFR-2 sanitisation. |\n| `@dnd-kit/sortable` | `10.0.0` | Peer is `@dnd-kit/core ^6.3.0`, which matches the installed 6.3.1 (FR-23). |\n\nESM-only packages import cleanly into App Router server and client code under `moduleResolution: bundler`, so `transpilePackages` is not needed. Add it only if `next build` complains.\n\n**Consequences.** No engine bump is needed. Do not upgrade file-type past 20.x without also raising `engines.node`. NFR-11 (single local-disk storage, no S3 SDK) means no cloud dependency is added.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-2: Parse multipart uploads with `busboy` (already installed via Next.js), not `request.formData()`\n\n**Context.** NFR-5 requires uploads to stream to disk, with sha256 computed and the size limit enforced during the stream. In Next 14, `Request.formData()` buffers the whole body in memory, which violates NFR-5 for 25 MB files (FR-4). `busboy@1.6.0` is already in `node_modules` as a direct dependency of `next@14.2.18` (`npm ls busboy`). Adding another parser would break NFR-9.\n\n**Decision.** `POST /api/files` (FR-3) runs on the Node runtime (`export const runtime = 'nodejs'`). It pipes `Readable.fromWeb(req.body)` into `busboy({ headers, limits: { files: 1, fileSize: MAX_UPLOAD_MB*1024*1024 } })`. The file stream goes through a `crypto.createHash('sha256')` tap and into `storage.saveFile()` (FR-2), which writes to a temp path under `UPLOAD_DIR` and then `rename`s it to `<yyyy>/<mm>/<cuid>.<ext>` (FR-1). Busboy's `limit` event aborts the upload, deletes the temp file and returns `VALIDATION_ERROR 'File exceeds 25 MB.'` (FR-4). Type sniffing (ADR-3) runs on the first chunk(s) before the write is committed. The `context` field must come before the file part in the multipart body because the logo rules (FR-40, FR-41) depend on it. FileUpload (FR-13) appends `context` first.\n\n**Consequences.** busboy is a transitive dependency, so `@types/busboy` is not available. Declare a minimal local type or accept `any` at that one call site, with a `ponytail:` comment explaining it. If a Next upgrade drops busboy, add `busboy` explicitly at that point. The intake handoff should note this as the only deviation from the dependency list, and it is a zero-install one. The rate limit check (NFR-3, `rateLimit('upload:'+userId, 60, 3_600_000)` from `src/server/rate-limit.ts`) runs before any bytes are read.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-3: Validate file types with an extension allowlist plus `file-type` sniffing of the head buffer, using an explicit table\n\n**Context.** FR-5 requires checks on both the extension and the magic number. FR-6 notes that txt/csv have no signature and that docx/xlsx/pptx can sniff as `zip`. FR-41 allows SVG only for `logo`, and SVG is text, so file-type cannot detect it reliably. The Vitest environment is `jsdom`, so file-type's `\"node\"` export condition may not resolve there, while `fileTypeFromBuffer` exists in both the node and core entries.\n\n**Decision.** In `src/server/files/validate.ts`, keep one `ALLOWED: Record<ext, { mimes: string[]; sniff: 'required' | 'none' | 'zip-or-ooxml' }>` table:\n- Binary types (pdf, doc, xls, ppt, png, jpg/jpeg, gif, webp, mp3, mp4, zip): the sniffed mime must be in `mimes`. doc/xls/ppt sniff as `application/x-cfb`.\n- docx/xlsx/pptx: the sniffed type is `application/zip` or the matching OOXML mime.\n- txt/csv and svg (logo only): sniffing must return `undefined`. If any signature is detected (for example an ELF, PE or zip disguised as .csv), the file is rejected. SVG also requires the head to contain `<svg`, and is limited to 2 MB under context `logo` (FR-40). The logo context also limits extensions to png, jpg, jpeg, webp and svg.\n- The stored `mimeType` comes from the table, not from the client's Content-Type header.\n- Call `fileTypeFromBuffer(head)` on the first 4100 bytes collected from the stream. `head` is a tee of the first chunks, not a re-read.\n\n**Consequences.** The rules are deterministic and testable as a pure function (FR-48). Integration tests that touch busboy or the filesystem add `// @vitest-environment node`. Errors use the FR-5 message `File type .<ext> is not allowed.`\n\n---\n\n## ADR-4: Store files on local disk outside `public/`, serve them only through a Node route handler, and set headers per response\n\n**Context.** NFR-1 says files are never served from a static path. FR-8 sets nosniff, attachment and inline rules. FR-41 sets a CSP for SVG logos. FR-10 requires `logo` to be readable without a session. `src/middleware.ts` returns 401 for any `/api/` path that is not in `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` (`src/config/public-routes.ts`). `next.config.js` already sends a site-wide `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and a CSP of `default-src 'self'` on `/:path*`.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `src/server/storage.ts` uses `node:fs/promises` and `node:stream/promises` only. `UPLOAD_DIR` is resolved with `path.resolve` and defaults to `./uploads`. Every storage key is checked to stay under `UPLOAD_DIR` after resolution, as a path-traversal guard. This is the only implementation (NFR-11).\n- `GET /api/files/:id` runs on `runtime = 'nodejs'` and returns `new Response(Readable.toWeb(fs.createReadStream(...)))` with `Content-Type` set to the stored mime, `Content-Length` set to `sizeBytes`, `nosniff`, and `Content-Disposition` set to `attachment` (or `inline` only for image/* or pdf with `?inline=1`) plus an RFC 5987 `filename*`. SVG responses also set `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'`. If next.config's global CSP is also sent, browsers enforce the stricter intersection, so no config change is needed for this route.\n- Add `'/api/files/'` to `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` so unauthenticated logo fetches reach the handler. The handler then runs the access registry (FR-9) for every request: `auth()` returns `null` for anonymous users, and only the `logo` resolver accepts a null user. Every other context returns 404 without reading bytes (NFR-8).\n- `.gitignore`: create one at the repo root containing `uploads/`, plus `node_modules`, `.next` and `.env` if missing. `.env.example` documents `UPLOAD_DIR` and `MAX_UPLOAD_MB` (NFR-12).\n\n**Consequences.** Opening the middleware gate moves the auth decision into the handler, so the access registry must deny by default (FR-9: an unknown context is owner/ADMIN only, and an anonymous user is denied). Integration tests must cover anonymous requests for a lesson file (expect 404) and for a logo (expect 200).\n\n---\n\n## ADR-5: Allow the video-embed origins in the site CSP via `frame-src`\n\n**Context.** FR-30 embeds `youtube-nocookie.com/embed/<id>` and `player.vimeo.com/video/<id>` iframes. The current CSP has `default-src 'self'` and no `frame-src`, so the browser would block both embeds.\n\n**Decision.** Add `frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://player.vimeo.com;` to the CSP string in `next.config.js`. The URL parser lives in `src/lib/video.ts` as a pure function shared by the Zod validator (FR-16) and the viewer (FR-32). It only emits those two origins.\n\n**Consequences.** This is a one-line config change. The `X-Frame-Options: DENY` header only stops other sites from framing SchoolHub, so it does not affect these embeds. Playwright should check that the iframe loads (it is not CSP-blocked) on the seeded YouTube lesson (FR-47).\n\n---\n\n## ADR-6: Render Markdown with react-markdown, remark-gfm and rehype-sanitize, map every element to MUI, and restrict images to same origin\n\n**Context.** FR-31 and NFR-2 require sanitised Markdown with `img` allowed from same origin only and MUI element mapping (NFR-7). The current CSP has `img-src 'self' data:`.\n\n**Decision.** `src/components/MarkdownView/MarkdownView.tsx` is a client-safe component (no hooks, so it also renders on the server) with `remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]}` and `rehypePlugins={[[rehypeSanitize, schema]]}`. `schema` extends `defaultSchema` and limits `img` `src` to relative paths: `protocols.src: []` plus a check that the value starts with `/`. The default schema already drops `javascript:` hrefs. A `components` map converts h1–h6, p, a, ul/ol/li, table/thead/tbody/tr/th/td, hr, blockquote, code and pre into MUI Typography, Link, List/ListItem, Table*, Divider and `Box component=\"pre\"`. External `a` elements get `target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`. The editor Preview tab (FR-25) uses the same component.\n\n**Consequences.** One renderer is used everywhere, so a sanitisation regression shows up in one unit test with `<script>`, `javascript:` and off-origin `img` cases (FR-48). No CSP change is needed for images.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-7: Implement reordering with @dnd-kit/sortable, persist the whole order in one transaction, and provide keyboard IconButtons\n\n**Context.** FR-23 covers drag within and across modules, with the full ordering sent to `POST /api/courses/:id/content/reorder`. NFR-6 requires a keyboard alternative. @dnd-kit/core is already installed.\n\n**Decision.** Each module's lesson List is a `SortableContext` with `verticalListSortingStrategy`, all inside one `DndContext`, and cross-container moves are handled in `onDragOver`. Up/down `IconButton`s modify the same client order array. The server validates that every submitted module and lesson id belongs to the course and that the set equals the existing set. It then writes `position`/`moduleId` for all rows inside one `prisma.$transaction`. A course has at most about 200 lessons (NFR-4), so updating every row is acceptable.\n\n**Consequences.** Reorder is a single idempotent call with no partial states. The update count is O(n) per reorder, which is fine at this scale.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-8: Read content with a bounded number of Prisma queries and compute visibility in TypeScript\n\n**Context.** NFR-4 requires the Content tab to render 20×10 lessons in under 600 ms with no N+1 queries. FR-18 evaluates visibility at read time, with no background job.\n\n**Decision.** `GET /api/courses/:id/modules` and the Content tab server component call `content-service.listCourseContent(user, courseId)`. That function makes one `module.findMany` with `include: { lessons: { include: { _count: { select: { attachments: true } } } } }` ordered by position, plus one `lessonProgress.findMany` for the student's lessons in that course when the caller is a STUDENT. That is two queries in total. `isVisible(entity, now)` is a pure function in `src/server/content/visibility.ts` and is unit tested (FR-48). Both module and lesson must be visible. Add an `@@index([courseId, position])` on Module and an `@@index([moduleId, position])` on Lesson.\n\n**Consequences.** Visibility is not pushed into SQL. At 200 rows per course, filtering in memory costs nothing, and having the rule in one place means the viewer (FR-32), the Learning tab (FR-35) and FR-44 all agree.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-9: Use existing stack pieces for scheduling, timezones, autosave and notifications\n\n**Context.** FR-28, NFR-10, FR-29, FR-38/39 and FR-12 all match existing infrastructure.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Schedule picker: use the `DateTimePicker` from `@mui/x-date-pickers` (already wrapped in `src/components/FormField`). Convert school-timezone input to UTC with `date-fns-tz` `fromZonedTime(SchoolSettings.timezone)`. Zod rejects `publishAt <= now` (FR-28, NFR-10).\n- Autosave (FR-29): a `setInterval` of 10 s plus a dirty flag in the editor, a `beforeunload` listener, and `PATCH /api/lessons/:id`. No new library.\n- Notifications: add `content.lesson_published` to `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` and call `notify()` only from the publish-now path (FR-38, FR-39).\n- Orphan purge (FR-12): call `purgeOrphanFiles()` next to `purgeOldNotifications()` inside the existing `lastPurgeAt` daily block in `src/app/api/internal/email/dispatch/route.ts`.\n- Logging (NFR-12): use `logger` from `src/lib/logger.ts`. Rate limit (NFR-3): use the existing `rateLimit()`, which is per-process. That limitation is already marked with a ponytail comment.\n\n**Consequences.** No scheduler, queue or cron is added. Scheduled lessons appear automatically when read after `publishAt`, and no notification is sent for them, as FR-39 requires.\n\n---\n\n## Open risks carried forward\n1. **NFR-7:** `mui-mcp` tools (`useMuiDocs`/`fetchDocs`) were visible in this session's tool list. Dev must still look up every MUI component against 9.5.0 before using it. If the server is unreachable, that is a hard blocker.\n2. **ADR-2:** depending on Next's transitive busboy is a small fragility (see the Consequences of ADR-2).\n3. **FR-40/42 (LOW confidence):** logo URLs stored as `/api/files/<id>` need an `APP_URL` prefix in email templates. The new `/admin/settings/school` page is a minimal page built on the existing `PATCH /api/settings/school`.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":531,"cache_read_input_tokens":46893,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":531},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6161ef1e-09c5-429b-90ef-ea4fc0ebef55","uuid":"f233e2fd-0b35-4316-b34b-36340f6a7a84","timestamp":"2026-11-06T18:49:55.904Z","request_id":"req_011CgnB6VMeG1nJzX2arwBzK","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01C4MM1QDpYnQNZ1tnbtUL5J":{"file_path":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions: PRD-08 File Storage & Course Content (SCH-008)\n\nTicket: SH-8 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-06T06:30:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and the workspace confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict (`moduleResolution: bundler`), Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL 16, Auth.js v5 beta.25, Zod 3.23.8, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons-material, material-nextjs, x-data-grid, x-date-pickers with `AdapterDateFns` already in `src/app/providers.tsx`, x-charts), @dnd-kit/core 6.3.1, date-fns/date-fns-tz, Vitest 2.1.1 (jsdom) and Playwright 1.47.2. `package.json` declares `engines.node >=18.18.0`, and local Node is 24.11.1. Nothing in the requirements calls for a different stack. The ADRs below cover how this feature fits into the existing stack and pin the new dependencies.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack and add only the five PRD-allowed dependencies, with pinned versions\n\n**Context.** NFR-9 limits new dependencies to file-type, react-markdown, remark-gfm, rehype-sanitize and @dnd-kit/sortable. The latest file-type (22.x) declares `engines.node >=22` and 21.x declares `>=20`. Either would break the repo's `>=18.18.0` engine contract. All five packages are ESM-only.\n\n**Decision.** Install these exact pins:\n| Package | Version | Why this version |\n|---|---|---|\n| `file-type` | `20.5.0` | Newest line with `engines.node >=18`. Use `fileTypeFromBuffer` (FR-5, FR-6). |\n| `react-markdown` | `10.1.0` | Peer is `react >=18` (FR-31). |\n| `remark-gfm` | `4.0.1` | Supports tables and strikethrough for FR-25 and FR-31. |\n| `rehype-sanitize` | `6.0.0` | Provides NFR-2 sanitisation. |\n| `@dnd-kit/sortable` | `10.0.0` | Peer is `@dnd-kit/core ^6.3.0`, which matches the installed 6.3.1 (FR-23). |\n\nESM-only packages import cleanly into App Router server and client code under `moduleResolution: bundler`, so `transpilePackages` is not needed. Add it only if `next build` complains.\n\n**Consequences.** No engine bump is needed. Do not upgrade file-type past 20.x without also raising `engines.node`. NFR-11 (single local-disk storage, no S3 SDK) means no cloud dependency is added.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-2: Parse multipart uploads with `busboy` (already installed via Next.js), not `request.formData()`\n\n**Context.** NFR-5 requires uploads to stream to disk, with sha256 computed and the size limit enforced during the stream. In Next 14, `Request.formData()` buffers the whole body in memory, which violates NFR-5 for 25 MB files (FR-4). `busboy@1.6.0` is already in `node_modules` as a direct dependency of `next@14.2.18` (`npm ls busboy`). Adding another parser would break NFR-9.\n\n**Decision.** `POST /api/files` (FR-3) runs on the Node runtime (`export const runtime = 'nodejs'`). It pipes `Readable.fromWeb(req.body)` into `busboy({ headers, limits: { files: 1, fileSize: MAX_UPLOAD_MB*1024*1024 } })`. The file stream goes through a `crypto.createHash('sha256')` tap and into `storage.saveFile()` (FR-2), which writes to a temp path under `UPLOAD_DIR` and then `rename`s it to `<yyyy>/<mm>/<cuid>.<ext>` (FR-1). Busboy's `limit` event aborts the upload, deletes the temp file and returns `VALIDATION_ERROR 'File exceeds 25 MB.'` (FR-4). Type sniffing (ADR-3) runs on the first chunk(s) before the write is committed. The `context` field must come before the file part in the multipart body because the logo rules (FR-40, FR-41) depend on it. FileUpload (FR-13) appends `context` first.\n\n**Consequences.** busboy is a transitive dependency, so `@types/busboy` is not available. Declare a minimal local type or accept `any` at that one call site, with a `ponytail:` comment explaining it. If a Next upgrade drops busboy, add `busboy` explicitly at that point. The intake handoff should note this as the only deviation from the dependency list, and it is a zero-install one. The rate limit check (NFR-3, `rateLimit('upload:'+userId, 60, 3_600_000)` from `src/server/rate-limit.ts`) runs before any bytes are read.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-3: Validate file types with an extension allowlist plus `file-type` sniffing of the head buffer, using an explicit table\n\n**Context.** FR-5 requires checks on both the extension and the magic number. FR-6 notes that txt/csv have no signature and that docx/xlsx/pptx can sniff as `zip`. FR-41 allows SVG only for `logo`, and SVG is text, so file-type cannot detect it reliably. The Vitest environment is `jsdom`, so file-type's `\"node\"` export condition may not resolve there, while `fileTypeFromBuffer` exists in both the node and core entries.\n\n**Decision.** In `src/server/files/validate.ts`, keep one `ALLOWED: Record<ext, { mimes: string[]; sniff: 'required' | 'none' | 'zip-or-ooxml' }>` table:\n- Binary types (pdf, doc, xls, ppt, png, jpg/jpeg, gif, webp, mp3, mp4, zip): the sniffed mime must be in `mimes`. doc/xls/ppt sniff as `application/x-cfb`.\n- docx/xlsx/pptx: the sniffed type is `application/zip` or the matching OOXML mime.\n- txt/csv and svg (logo only): sniffing must return `undefined`. If any signature is detected (for example an ELF, PE or zip disguised as .csv), the file is rejected. SVG also requires the head to contain `<svg`, and is limited to 2 MB under context `logo` (FR-40). The logo context also limits extensions to png, jpg, jpeg, webp and svg.\n- The stored `mimeType` comes from the table, not from the client's Content-Type header.\n- Call `fileTypeFromBuffer(head)` on the first 4100 bytes collected from the stream. `head` is a tee of the first chunks, not a re-read.\n\n**Consequences.** The rules are deterministic and testable as a pure function (FR-48). Integration tests that touch busboy or the filesystem add `// @vitest-environment node`. Errors use the FR-5 message `File type .<ext> is not allowed.`\n\n---\n\n## ADR-4: Store files on local disk outside `public/`, serve them only through a Node route handler, and set headers per response\n\n**Context.** NFR-1 says files are never served from a static path. FR-8 sets nosniff, attachment and inline rules. FR-41 sets a CSP for SVG logos. FR-10 requires `logo` to be readable without a session. `src/middleware.ts` returns 401 for any `/api/` path that is not in `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` (`src/config/public-routes.ts`). `next.config.js` already sends a site-wide `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and a CSP of `default-src 'self'` on `/:path*`.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `src/server/storage.ts` uses `node:fs/promises` and `node:stream/promises` only. `UPLOAD_DIR` is resolved with `path.resolve` and defaults to `./uploads`. Every storage key is checked to stay under `UPLOAD_DIR` after resolution, as a path-traversal guard. This is the only implementation (NFR-11).\n- `GET /api/files/:id` runs on `runtime = 'nodejs'` and returns `new Response(Readable.toWeb(fs.createReadStream(...)))` with `Content-Type` set to the stored mime, `Content-Length` set to `sizeBytes`, `nosniff`, and `Content-Disposition` set to `attachment` (or `inline` only for image/* or pdf with `?inline=1`) plus an RFC 5987 `filename*`. SVG responses also set `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'`. If next.config's global CSP is also sent, browsers enforce the stricter intersection, so no config change is needed for this route.\n- Add `'/api/files/'` to `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` so unauthenticated logo fetches reach the handler. The handler then runs the access registry (FR-9) for every request: `auth()` returns `null` for anonymous users, and only the `logo` resolver accepts a null user. Every other context returns 404 without reading bytes (NFR-8).\n- `.gitignore`: create one at the repo root containing `uploads/`, plus `node_modules`, `.next` and `.env` if missing. `.env.example` documents `UPLOAD_DIR` and `MAX_UPLOAD_MB` (NFR-12).\n\n**Consequences.** Opening the middleware gate moves the auth decision into the handler, so the access registry must deny by default (FR-9: an unknown context is owner/ADMIN only, and an anonymous user is denied). Integration tests must cover anonymous requests for a lesson file (expect 404) and for a logo (expect 200).\n\n---\n\n## ADR-5: Allow the video-embed origins in the site CSP via `frame-src`\n\n**Context.** FR-30 embeds `youtube-nocookie.com/embed/<id>` and `player.vimeo.com/video/<id>` iframes. The current CSP has `default-src 'self'` and no `frame-src`, so the browser would block both embeds.\n\n**Decision.** Add `frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://player.vimeo.com;` to the CSP string in `next.config.js`. The URL parser lives in `src/lib/video.ts` as a pure function shared by the Zod validator (FR-16) and the viewer (FR-32). It only emits those two origins.\n\n**Consequences.** This is a one-line config change. The `X-Frame-Options: DENY` header only stops other sites from framing SchoolHub, so it does not affect these embeds. Playwright should check that the iframe loads (it is not CSP-blocked) on the seeded YouTube lesson (FR-47).\n\n---\n\n## ADR-6: Render Markdown with react-markdown, remark-gfm and rehype-sanitize, map every element to MUI, and restrict images to same origin\n\n**Context.** FR-31 and NFR-2 require sanitised Markdown with `img` allowed from same origin only and MUI element mapping (NFR-7). The current CSP has `img-src 'self' data:`.\n\n**Decision.** `src/components/MarkdownView/MarkdownView.tsx` is a client-safe component (no hooks, so it also renders on the server) with `remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]}` and `rehypePlugins={[[rehypeSanitize, schema]]}`. `schema` extends `defaultSchema` and limits `img` `src` to relative paths: `protocols.src: []` plus a check that the value starts with `/`. The default schema already drops `javascript:` hrefs. A `components` map converts h1–h6, p, a, ul/ol/li, table/thead/tbody/tr/th/td, hr, blockquote, code and pre into MUI Typography, Link, List/ListItem, Table*, Divider and `Box component=\"pre\"`. External `a` elements get `target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`. The editor Preview tab (FR-25) uses the same component.\n\n**Consequences.** One renderer is used everywhere, so a sanitisation regression shows up in one unit test with `<script>`, `javascript:` and off-origin `img` cases (FR-48). No CSP change is needed for images.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-7: Implement reordering with @dnd-kit/sortable, persist the whole order in one transaction, and provide keyboard IconButtons\n\n**Context.** FR-23 covers drag within and across modules, with the full ordering sent to `POST /api/courses/:id/content/reorder`. NFR-6 requires a keyboard alternative. @dnd-kit/core is already installed.\n\n**Decision.** Each module's lesson List is a `SortableContext` with `verticalListSortingStrategy`, all inside one `DndContext`, and cross-container moves are handled in `onDragOver`. Up/down `IconButton`s modify the same client order array. The server validates that every submitted module and lesson id belongs to the course and that the set equals the existing set. It then writes `position`/`moduleId` for all rows inside one `prisma.$transaction`. A course has at most about 200 lessons (NFR-4), so updating every row is acceptable.\n\n**Consequences.** Reorder is a single idempotent call with no partial states. The update count is O(n) per reorder, which is fine at this scale.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-8: Read content with a bounded number of Prisma queries and compute visibility in TypeScript\n\n**Context.** NFR-4 requires the Content tab to render 20×10 lessons in under 600 ms with no N+1 queries. FR-18 evaluates visibility at read time, with no background job.\n\n**Decision.** `GET /api/courses/:id/modules` and the Content tab server component call `content-service.listCourseContent(user, courseId)`. That function makes one `module.findMany` with `include: { lessons: { include: { _count: { select: { attachments: true } } } } }` ordered by position, plus one `lessonProgress.findMany` for the student's lessons in that course when the caller is a STUDENT. That is two queries in total. `isVisible(entity, now)` is a pure function in `src/server/content/visibility.ts` and is unit tested (FR-48). Both module and lesson must be visible. Add an `@@index([courseId, position])` on Module and an `@@index([moduleId, position])` on Lesson.\n\n**Consequences.** Visibility is not pushed into SQL. At 200 rows per course, filtering in memory costs nothing, and having the rule in one place means the viewer (FR-32), the Learning tab (FR-35) and FR-44 all agree.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-9: Use existing stack pieces for scheduling, timezones, autosave and notifications\n\n**Context.** FR-28, NFR-10, FR-29, FR-38/39 and FR-12 all match existing infrastructure.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Schedule picker: use the `DateTimePicker` from `@mui/x-date-pickers` (already wrapped in `src/components/FormField`). Convert school-timezone input to UTC with `date-fns-tz` `fromZonedTime(SchoolSettings.timezone)`. Zod rejects `publishAt <= now` (FR-28, NFR-10).\n- Autosave (FR-29): a `setInterval` of 10 s plus a dirty flag in the editor, a `beforeunload` listener, and `PATCH /api/lessons/:id`. No new library.\n- Notifications: add `content.lesson_published` to `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` and call `notify()` only from the publish-now path (FR-38, FR-39).\n- Orphan purge (FR-12): call `purgeOrphanFiles()` next to `purgeOldNotifications()` inside the existing `lastPurgeAt` daily block in `src/app/api/internal/email/dispatch/route.ts`.\n- Logging (NFR-12): use `logger` from `src/lib/logger.ts`. Rate limit (NFR-3): use the existing `rateLimit()`, which is per-process. That limitation is already marked with a ponytail comment.\n\n**Consequences.** No scheduler, queue or cron is added. Scheduled lessons appear automatically when read after `publishAt`, and no notification is sent for them, as FR-39 requires.\n\n---\n\n## Open risks carried forward\n1. **NFR-7:** `mui-mcp` tools (`useMuiDocs`/`fetchDocs`) were visible in this session's tool list. Dev must still look up every MUI component against 9.5.0 before using it. If the server is unreachable, that is a hard blocker.\n2. **ADR-2:** depending on Next's transitive busboy is a small fragility (see the Consequences of ADR-2).\n3. **FR-40/42 (LOW confidence):** logo URLs stored as `/api/files/<id>` need an `APP_URL` prefix in email templates. The new `/admin/settings/school` page is a minimal page built on the existing `PATCH /api/settings/school`.\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C4MM1QDpYnQNZ1tnbtUL5J","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6161ef1e-09c5-429b-90ef-ea4fc0ebef55","uuid":"ffd8362d-8d0c-483b-898f-b15fc91affe1","timestamp":"2026-11-06T18:49:55.982Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions: PRD-08 File Storage & Course Content (SCH-008)\n\nTicket: SH-8 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-06T06:30:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and the workspace confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict (`moduleResolution: bundler`), Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL 16, Auth.js v5 beta.25, Zod 3.23.8, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons-material, material-nextjs, x-data-grid, x-date-pickers with `AdapterDateFns` already in `src/app/providers.tsx`, x-charts), @dnd-kit/core 6.3.1, date-fns/date-fns-tz, Vitest 2.1.1 (jsdom) and Playwright 1.47.2. `package.json` declares `engines.node >=18.18.0`, and local Node is 24.11.1. Nothing in the requirements calls for a different stack. The ADRs below cover how this feature fits into the existing stack and pin the new dependencies.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack and add only the five PRD-allowed dependencies, with pinned versions\n\n**Context.** NFR-9 limits new dependencies to file-type, react-markdown, remark-gfm, rehype-sanitize and @dnd-kit/sortable. The latest file-type (22.x) declares `engines.node >=22` and 21.x declares `>=20`. Either would break the repo's `>=18.18.0` engine contract. All five packages are ESM-only.\n\n**Decision.** Install these exact pins:\n| Package | Version | Why this version |\n|---|---|---|\n| `file-type` | `20.5.0` | Newest line with `engines.node >=18`. Use `fileTypeFromBuffer` (FR-5, FR-6). |\n| `react-markdown` | `10.1.0` | Peer is `react >=18` (FR-31). |\n| `remark-gfm` | `4.0.1` | Supports tables and strikethrough for FR-25 and FR-31. |\n| `rehype-sanitize` | `6.0.0` | Provides NFR-2 sanitisation. |\n| `@dnd-kit/sortable` | `10.0.0` | Peer is `@dnd-kit/core ^6.3.0`, which matches the installed 6.3.1 (FR-23). |\n\nESM-only packages import cleanly into App Router server and client code under `moduleResolution: bundler`, so `transpilePackages` is not needed. Add it only if `next build` complains.\n\n**Consequences.** No engine bump is needed. Do not upgrade file-type past 20.x without also raising `engines.node`. NFR-11 (single local-disk storage, no S3 SDK) means no cloud dependency is added.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-2: Parse multipart uploads with `busboy` (already installed via Next.js), not `request.formData()`\n\n**Context.** NFR-5 requires uploads to stream to disk, with sha256 computed and the size limit enforced during the stream. In Next 14, `Request.formData()` buffers the whole body in memory, which violates NFR-5 for 25 MB files (FR-4). `busboy@1.6.0` is already in `node_modules` as a direct dependency of `next@14.2.18` (`npm ls busboy`). Adding another parser would break NFR-9.\n\n**Decision.** `POST /api/files` (FR-3) runs on the Node runtime (`export const runtime = 'nodejs'`). It pipes `Readable.fromWeb(req.body)` into `busboy({ headers, limits: { files: 1, fileSize: MAX_UPLOAD_MB*1024*1024 } })`. The file stream goes through a `crypto.createHash('sha256')` tap and into `storage.saveFile()` (FR-2), which writes to a temp path under `UPLOAD_DIR` and then `rename`s it to `<yyyy>/<mm>/<cuid>.<ext>` (FR-1). Busboy's `limit` event aborts the upload, deletes the temp file and returns `VALIDATION_ERROR 'File exceeds 25 MB.'` (FR-4). Type sniffing (ADR-3) runs on the first chunk(s) before the write is committed. The `context` field must come before the file part in the multipart body because the logo rules (FR-40, FR-41) depend on it. FileUpload (FR-13) appends `context` first.\n\n**Consequences.** busboy is a transitive dependency, so `@types/busboy` is not available. Declare a minimal local type or accept `any` at that one call site, with a `ponytail:` comment explaining it. If a Next upgrade drops busboy, add `busboy` explicitly at that point. The intake handoff should note this as the only deviation from the dependency list, and it is a zero-install one. The rate limit check (NFR-3, `rateLimit('upload:'+userId, 60, 3_600_000)` from `src/server/rate-limit.ts`) runs before any bytes are read.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-3: Validate file types with an extension allowlist plus `file-type` sniffing of the head buffer, using an explicit table\n\n**Context.** FR-5 requires checks on both the extension and the magic number. FR-6 notes that txt/csv have no signature and that docx/xlsx/pptx can sniff as `zip`. FR-41 allows SVG only for `logo`, and SVG is text, so file-type cannot detect it reliably. The Vitest environment is `jsdom`, so file-type's `\"node\"` export condition may not resolve there, while `fileTypeFromBuffer` exists in both the node and core entries.\n\n**Decision.** In `src/server/files/validate.ts`, keep one `ALLOWED: Record<ext, { mimes: string[]; sniff: 'required' | 'none' | 'zip-or-ooxml' }>` table:\n- Binary types (pdf, doc, xls, ppt, png, jpg/jpeg, gif, webp, mp3, mp4, zip): the sniffed mime must be in `mimes`. doc/xls/ppt sniff as `application/x-cfb`.\n- docx/xlsx/pptx: the sniffed type is `application/zip` or the matching OOXML mime.\n- txt/csv and svg (logo only): sniffing must return `undefined`. If any signature is detected (for example an ELF, PE or zip disguised as .csv), the file is rejected. SVG also requires the head to contain `<svg`, and is limited to 2 MB under context `logo` (FR-40). The logo context also limits extensions to png, jpg, jpeg, webp and svg.\n- The stored `mimeType` comes from the table, not from the client's Content-Type header.\n- Call `fileTypeFromBuffer(head)` on the first 4100 bytes collected from the stream. `head` is a tee of the first chunks, not a re-read.\n\n**Consequences.** The rules are deterministic and testable as a pure function (FR-48). Integration tests that touch busboy or the filesystem add `// @vitest-environment node`. Errors use the FR-5 message `File type .<ext> is not allowed.`\n\n---\n\n## ADR-4: Store files on local disk outside `public/`, serve them only through a Node route handler, and set headers per response\n\n**Context.** NFR-1 says files are never served from a static path. FR-8 sets nosniff, attachment and inline rules. FR-41 sets a CSP for SVG logos. FR-10 requires `logo` to be readable without a session. `src/middleware.ts` returns 401 for any `/api/` path that is not in `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` (`src/config/public-routes.ts`). `next.config.js` already sends a site-wide `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and a CSP of `default-src 'self'` on `/:path*`.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `src/server/storage.ts` uses `node:fs/promises` and `node:stream/promises` only. `UPLOAD_DIR` is resolved with `path.resolve` and defaults to `./uploads`. Every storage key is checked to stay under `UPLOAD_DIR` after resolution, as a path-traversal guard. This is the only implementation (NFR-11).\n- `GET /api/files/:id` runs on `runtime = 'nodejs'` and returns `new Response(Readable.toWeb(fs.createReadStream(...)))` with `Content-Type` set to the stored mime, `Content-Length` set to `sizeBytes`, `nosniff`, and `Content-Disposition` set to `attachment` (or `inline` only for image/* or pdf with `?inline=1`) plus an RFC 5987 `filename*`. SVG responses also set `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'`. If next.config's global CSP is also sent, browsers enforce the stricter intersection, so no config change is needed for this route.\n- Add `'/api/files/'` to `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` so unauthenticated logo fetches reach the handler. The handler then runs the access registry (FR-9) for every request: `auth()` returns `null` for anonymous users, and only the `logo` resolver accepts a null user. Every other context returns 404 without reading bytes (NFR-8).\n- `.gitignore`: create one at the repo root containing `uploads/`, plus `node_modules`, `.next` and `.env` if missing. `.env.example` documents `UPLOAD_DIR` and `MAX_UPLOAD_MB` (NFR-12).\n\n**Consequences.** Opening the middleware gate moves the auth decision into the handler, so the access registry must deny by default (FR-9: an unknown context is owner/ADMIN only, and an anonymous user is denied). Integration tests must cover anonymous requests for a lesson file (expect 404) and for a logo (expect 200).\n\n---\n\n## ADR-5: Allow the video-embed origins in the site CSP via `frame-src`\n\n**Context.** FR-30 embeds `youtube-nocookie.com/embed/<id>` and `player.vimeo.com/video/<id>` iframes. The current CSP has `default-src 'self'` and no `frame-src`, so the browser would block both embeds.\n\n**Decision.** Add `frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://player.vimeo.com;` to the CSP string in `next.config.js`. The URL parser lives in `src/lib/video.ts` as a pure function shared by the Zod validator (FR-16) and the viewer (FR-32). It only emits those two origins.\n\n**Consequences.** This is a one-line config change. The `X-Frame-Options: DENY` header only stops other sites from framing SchoolHub, so it does not affect these embeds. Playwright should check that the iframe loads (it is not CSP-blocked) on the seeded YouTube lesson (FR-47).\n\n---\n\n## ADR-6: Render Markdown with react-markdown, remark-gfm and rehype-sanitize, map every element to MUI, and restrict images to same origin\n\n**Context.** FR-31 and NFR-2 require sanitised Markdown with `img` allowed from same origin only and MUI element mapping (NFR-7). The current CSP has `img-src 'self' data:`.\n\n**Decision.** `src/components/MarkdownView/MarkdownView.tsx` is a client-safe component (no hooks, so it also renders on the server) with `remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]}` and `rehypePlugins={[[rehypeSanitize, schema]]}`. `schema` extends `defaultSchema` and limits `img` `src` to relative paths: `protocols.src: []` plus a check that the value starts with `/`. The default schema already drops `javascript:` hrefs. A `components` map converts h1–h6, p, a, ul/ol/li, table/thead/tbody/tr/th/td, hr, blockquote, code and pre into MUI Typography, Link, List/ListItem, Table*, Divider and `Box component=\"pre\"`. External `a` elements get `target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`. The editor Preview tab (FR-25) uses the same component.\n\n**Consequences.** One renderer is used everywhere, so a sanitisation regression shows up in one unit test with `<script>`, `javascript:` and off-origin `img` cases (FR-48). No CSP change is needed for images.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-7: Implement reordering with @dnd-kit/sortable, persist the whole order in one transaction, and provide keyboard IconButtons\n\n**Context.** FR-23 covers drag within and across modules, with the full ordering sent to `POST /api/courses/:id/content/reorder`. NFR-6 requires a keyboard alternative. @dnd-kit/core is already installed.\n\n**Decision.** Each module's lesson List is a `SortableContext` with `verticalListSortingStrategy`, all inside one `DndContext`, and cross-container moves are handled in `onDragOver`. Up/down `IconButton`s modify the same client order array. The server validates that every submitted module and lesson id belongs to the course and that the set equals the existing set. It then writes `position`/`moduleId` for all rows inside one `prisma.$transaction`. A course has at most about 200 lessons (NFR-4), so updating every row is acceptable.\n\n**Consequences.** Reorder is a single idempotent call with no partial states. The update count is O(n) per reorder, which is fine at this scale.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-8: Read content with a bounded number of Prisma queries and compute visibility in TypeScript\n\n**Context.** NFR-4 requires the Content tab to render 20×10 lessons in under 600 ms with no N+1 queries. FR-18 evaluates visibility at read time, with no background job.\n\n**Decision.** `GET /api/courses/:id/modules` and the Content tab server component call `content-service.listCourseContent(user, courseId)`. That function makes one `module.findMany` with `include: { lessons: { include: { _count: { select: { attachments: true } } } } }` ordered by position, plus one `lessonProgress.findMany` for the student's lessons in that course when the caller is a STUDENT. That is two queries in total. `isVisible(entity, now)` is a pure function in `src/server/content/visibility.ts` and is unit tested (FR-48). Both module and lesson must be visible. Add an `@@index([courseId, position])` on Module and an `@@index([moduleId, position])` on Lesson.\n\n**Consequences.** Visibility is not pushed into SQL. At 200 rows per course, filtering in memory costs nothing, and having the rule in one place means the viewer (FR-32), the Learning tab (FR-35) and FR-44 all agree.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-9: Use existing stack pieces for scheduling, timezones, autosave and notifications\n\n**Context.** FR-28, NFR-10, FR-29, FR-38/39 and FR-12 all match existing infrastructure.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Schedule picker: use the `DateTimePicker` from `@mui/x-date-pickers` (already wrapped in `src/components/FormField`). Convert school-timezone input to UTC with `date-fns-tz` `fromZonedTime(SchoolSettings.timezone)`. Zod rejects `publishAt <= now` (FR-28, NFR-10).\n- Autosave (FR-29): a `setInterval` of 10 s plus a dirty flag in the editor, a `beforeunload` listener, and `PATCH /api/lessons/:id`. No new library.\n- Notifications: add `content.lesson_published` to `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` and call `notify()` only from the publish-now path (FR-38, FR-39).\n- Orphan purge (FR-12): call `purgeOrphanFiles()` next to `purgeOldNotifications()` inside the existing `lastPurgeAt` daily block in `src/app/api/internal/email/dispatch/route.ts`.\n- Logging (NFR-12): use `logger` from `src/lib/logger.ts`. Rate limit (NFR-3): use the existing `rateLimit()`, which is per-process. That limitation is already marked with a ponytail comment.\n\n**Consequences.** No scheduler, queue or cron is added. Scheduled lessons appear automatically when read after `publishAt`, and no notification is sent for them, as FR-39 requires.\n\n---\n\n## Open risks carried forward\n1. **NFR-7:** `mui-mcp` tools (`useMuiDocs`/`fetchDocs`) were visible in this session's tool list. Dev must still look up every MUI component against 9.5.0 before using it. If the server is unreachable, that is a hard blocker.\n2. **ADR-2:** depending on Next's transitive busboy is a small fragility (see the Consequences of ADR-2).\n3. **FR-40/42 (LOW confidence):** logo URLs stored as `/api/files/<id>` need an `APP_URL` prefix in email templates. The new `/admin/settings/school` page is a minimal page built on the existing `PATCH /api/settings/school`.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":151,"newStart":1,"newLines":125,"lines":["-# Tech Stack Decisions: PRD-07 Attendance Tracking (SCH-007)","+# Tech Stack Decisions: PRD-08 File Storage & Course Content (SCH-008)"," ","-Ticket: SH-7 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-06T06:00:00Z · Status: complete","+Ticket: SH-8 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-06T06:30:00Z · Status: complete"," ","-**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and `package.json` confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL 16, Auth.js (next-auth 5.0.0-beta.25), Zod 3.23.8, and MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons-material, material-nextjs, x-data-grid, x-date-pickers, x-charts). Also installed: date-fns 3.6.0, date-fns-tz 3.2.0, papaparse, Vitest 2.1.1 and Playwright 1.47.2. Nothing in the requirements needs a different stack, so the decisions below cover how this feature uses the stack that is already there.","+**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and the workspace confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict (`moduleResolution: bundler`), Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL 16, Auth.js v5 beta.25, Zod 3.23.8, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons-material, material-nextjs, x-data-grid, x-date-pickers with `AdapterDateFns` already in `src/app/providers.tsx`, x-charts), @dnd-kit/core 6.3.1, date-fns/date-fns-tz, Vitest 2.1.1 (jsdom) and Playwright 1.47.2. `package.json` declares `engines.node >=18.18.0`, and local Node is 24.11.1. Nothing in the requirements calls for a different stack. The ADRs below cover how this feature fits into the existing stack and pin the new dependencies."," ","-This file replaces the stale SH-1 (PRD-01) version that was in this folder.","-"," ---"," ","-## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack and add no new dependencies","+## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack and add only the five PRD-allowed dependencies, with pinned versions"," ","-**Context.** The feature needs a register UI with exclusive toggles (FR-9), a calendar with a custom day slot (FR-26), a scrollable matrix (FR-29), date pickers that block future dates (FR-8), CSV export (FR-32), timezone-aware \"today\" logic (FR-31, NFR-8), and shared validation (FR-36). NFR-6 allows MUI only, and NFR-10 asks for brownfield safety.","+**Context.** NFR-9 limits new dependencies to file-type, react-markdown, remark-gfm, rehype-sanitize and @dnd-kit/sortable. The latest file-type (22.x) declares `engines.node >=22` and 21.x declares `>=20`. Either would break the repo's `>=18.18.0` engine contract. All five packages are ESM-only."," ","-**Decision.** Use only installed packages:","-- UI: `@mui/material` `ToggleButtonGroup`, `Paper` (sticky save bar), `Tooltip` and `Badge`; `@mui/x-date-pickers` `DatePicker` (`disableFuture`, `minDate`) and `DateCalendar` with a custom `day` slot; `@mui/x-data-grid` `DataGrid` for the course matrix and the admin report.","-- Validation: `zod`, in `src/lib/validators/attendance.ts`.","-- Dates: `date-fns` and `date-fns-tz`, through the existing `src/lib/dates.ts`.","-- CSV: the existing `toCsv()`/`escapeCsvCell()` in `src/server/csv.ts`. `papaparse` is not needed for export.","+**Decision.** Install these exact pins:","+| Package | Version | Why this version |","+|---|---|---|","+| `file-type` | `20.5.0` | Newest line with `engines.node >=18`. Use `fileTypeFromBuffer` (FR-5, FR-6). |","+| `react-markdown` | `10.1.0` | Peer is `react >=18` (FR-31). |","+| `remark-gfm` | `4.0.1` | Supports tables and strikethrough for FR-25 and FR-31. |","+| `rehype-sanitize` | `6.0.0` | Provides NFR-2 sanitisation. |","+| `@dnd-kit/sortable` | `10.0.0` | Peer is `@dnd-kit/core ^6.3.0`, which matches the installed 6.3.1 (FR-23). |"," ","-Before writing any component, Dev must look it up through the mui-mcp server (`useMuiDocs`/`fetchDocs` for 9.5.0). The server was reachable in this session.","+ESM-only packages import cleanly into App Router server and client code under `moduleResolution: bundler`, so `transpilePackages` is not needed. Add it only if `next build` complains."," ","-**Consequences.** There are no lockfile changes and no banned imports for QA to catch. The free (MIT) `DataGrid` has no column pinning and caps pages at 100 rows. That is acceptable: the matrix scrolls horizontally (FR-29) and pagination is capped at 100 anyway (NFR-8). Do not use `DataGridPro` features.","+**Consequences.** No engine bump is needed. Do not upgrade file-type past 20.x without also raising `engines.node`. NFR-11 (single local-disk storage, no S3 SDK) means no cloud dependency is added."," ","-Drivers: FR-8, FR-9, FR-26, FR-29, FR-32, FR-36, NFR-6, NFR-8, NFR-10.","-"," ---"," ","-## ADR-2: Use a Prisma schema plus hand-written SQL in one migration for constraints Prisma can't express","+## ADR-2: Parse multipart uploads with `busboy` (already installed via Next.js), not `request.formData()`"," ","-**Context.** FR-1 specifies the `AttendanceStatus` enum and the `AttendanceSession`/`AttendanceRecord` models. FR-2 needs \"exactly one of courseId/sectionId\". FR-3 needs two partial unique indexes. Prisma 5.20 cannot express CHECK constraints or partial (`WHERE`) indexes. FR-4 adds two `SchoolSettings` columns. The latest migration is `20261106000000_user_role_status_index`.","+**Context.** NFR-5 requires uploads to stream to disk, with sha256 computed and the size limit enforced during the stream. In Next 14, `Request.formData()` buffers the whole body in memory, which violates NFR-5 for 25 MB files (FR-4). `busboy@1.6.0` is already in `node_modules` as a direct dependency of `next@14.2.18` (`npm ls busboy`). Adding another parser would break NFR-9."," ","-**Decision.** Create one new migration, `20261107000000_attendance` or any timestamp later than the latest one. Generate it with `prisma migrate dev --create-only`, then append the following raw SQL by hand:","-- `CHECK (((\"courseId\" IS NOT NULL)::int + (\"sectionId\" IS NOT NULL)::int) = 1)`","-- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON \"AttendanceSession\"(\"courseId\",\"date\",\"periodId\") WHERE \"courseId\" IS NOT NULL`","-- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON \"AttendanceSession\"(\"sectionId\",\"date\") WHERE \"sectionId\" IS NOT NULL`","+**Decision.** `POST /api/files` (FR-3) runs on the Node runtime (`export const runtime = 'nodejs'`). It pipes `Readable.fromWeb(req.body)` into `busboy({ headers, limits: { files: 1, fileSize: MAX_UPLOAD_MB*1024*1024 } })`. The file stream goes through a `crypto.createHash('sha256')` tap and into `storage.saveFile()` (FR-2), which writes to a temp path under `UPLOAD_DIR` and then `rename`s it to `<yyyy>/<mm>/<cuid>.<ext>` (FR-1). Busboy's `limit` event aborts the upload, deletes the temp file and returns `VALIDATION_ERROR 'File exceeds 25 MB.'` (FR-4). Type sniffing (ADR-3) runs on the first chunk(s) before the write is committed. The `context` field must come before the file part in the multipart body because the logo rules (FR-40, FR-41) depend on it. FileUpload (FR-13) appends `context` first."," ","-`SchoolSettings` gains an `AttendanceMode` enum column `attendanceMode` (default `PER_PERIOD`) and `attendanceAlertThreshold Int @default(90)`. `date` is stored as `@db.Date`.","+**Consequences.** busboy is a transitive dependency, so `@types/busboy` is not available. Declare a minimal local type or accept `any` at that one call site, with a `ponytail:` comment explaining it. If a Next upgrade drops busboy, add `busboy` explicitly at that point. The intake handoff should note this as the only deviation from the dependency list, and it is a zero-install one. The rate limit check (NFR-3, `rateLimit('upload:'+userId, 60, 3_600_000)` from `src/server/rate-limit.ts`) runs before any bytes are read."," ","-**Consequences.** `prisma db push` and any schema drift check will not see these SQL objects, so tests must run `prisma migrate reset`/`deploy` and must not use `db push`. The service also checks the one-of rule (FR-2), so callers get a clean `VALIDATION_ERROR` before the database rejects the row. Prisma cannot `upsert` on a partial index (see ADR-3).","-","-Drivers: FR-1, FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-40, NFR-8.","-"," ---"," ","-## ADR-3: Save a register in one interactive transaction that only writes changed rows","+## ADR-3: Validate file types with an extension allowlist plus `file-type` sniffing of the head buffer, using an explicit table"," ","-**Context.** FR-14 requires an atomic create-or-update of the session and all its records. NFR-1 requires a 35-student save to finish in under 500 ms. FR-18 requires an audit entry for each changed status, and FR-21/22 need the previous status to detect changes. A partial unique index cannot be used as an upsert target.","+**Context.** FR-5 requires checks on both the extension and the magic number. FR-6 notes that txt/csv have no signature and that docx/xlsx/pptx can sniff as `zip`. FR-41 allows SVG only for `logo`, and SVG is text, so file-type cannot detect it reliably. The Vitest environment is `jsdom`, so file-type's `\"node\"` export condition may not resolve there, while `fileTypeFromBuffer` exists in both the node and core entries."," ","-**Decision.** `saveRegister()` in `src/server/attendance/attendance-service.ts` uses `prisma.$transaction(async tx => …)` and runs these steps:","-1. `findFirst` the session by (courseId, date, periodId) or (sectionId, date). Create it if it does not exist. If the create fails with `P2002`, a concurrent first save won the race: re-read the session and continue.","-2. Load the existing records for the session in a single query to get the previous statuses.","-3. Insert new students with `createMany`. Update only the rows whose status, minutesLate or note actually changed; `@@unique([sessionId, studentId])` makes these targeted updates.","-4. Validate the roster (NFR-3) against ACTIVE enrollments on that date, or section members for a daily register, in one query before writing.","-5. Return the diff `{studentId, prev, next}[]`. Write `audit()` entries for changed records inside the transaction, or right after commit if `audit()` cannot take `tx`. Dev should check `src/server/audit.ts`.","+**Decision.** In `src/server/files/validate.ts`, keep one `ALLOWED: Record<ext, { mimes: string[]; sniff: 'required' | 'none' | 'zip-or-ooxml' }>` table:","+- Binary types (pdf, doc, xls, ppt, png, jpg/jpeg, gif, webp, mp3, mp4, zip): the sniffed mime must be in `mimes`. doc/xls/ppt sniff as `application/x-cfb`.","+- docx/xlsx/pptx: the sniffed type is `application/zip` or the matching OOXML mime.","+- txt/csv and svg (logo only): sniffing must return `undefined`. If any signature is detected (for example an ELF, PE or zip disguised as .csv), the file is rejected. SVG also requires the head to contain `<svg`, and is limited to 2 MB under context `logo` (FR-40). The logo context also limits extensions to png, jpg, jpeg, webp and svg.","+- The stored `mimeType` comes from the table, not from the client's Content-Type header.","+- Call `fileTypeFromBuffer(head)` on the first 4100 bytes collected from the stream. `head` is a tee of the first chunks, not a re-read."," ","-**Consequences.** About 5 to 40 statements run per save, which fits easily in 500 ms. The diff is computed by a pure function (`diffRecords(prev, next)`) that unit tests can cover (FR-39). No raw `INSERT … ON CONFLICT` is needed.","+**Consequences.** The rules are deterministic and testable as a pure function (FR-48). Integration tests that touch busboy or the filesystem add `// @vitest-environment node`. Errors use the FR-5 message `File type .<ext> is not allowed.`"," ","-Drivers: FR-14, FR-18, FR-21, FR-22, FR-39, FR-40, NFR-1, NFR-3.","-"," ---"," ","-## ADR-4: Send notifications synchronously after commit through the existing notify pipeline, with no new job queue","+## ADR-4: Store files on local disk outside `public/`, serve them only through a Node route handler, and set headers per response"," ","-**Context.** FR-20 to FR-23 add four notification types. NFR-5 requires delivery within 5 minutes, with in-app notifications immediate and email through `EmailOutbox`. `notifyStudentAndParents()`/`notify()` already exist and never throw. There is no job runner or cron in `src/server`.","+**Context.** NFR-1 says files are never served from a static path. FR-8 sets nosniff, attachment and inline rules. FR-41 sets a CSP for SVG logos. FR-10 requires `logo` to be readable without a session. `src/middleware.ts` returns 401 for any `/api/` path that is not in `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` (`src/config/public-routes.ts`). `next.config.js` already sends a site-wide `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and a CSP of `default-src 'self'` on `/:path*`."," ","-**Decision.** After the transaction commits, loop over the diff and call `notifyStudentAndParents()` when:","-- a status changes from something other than ABSENT to ABSENT (`attendance.absent`);","-- a status changes from something other than LATE to LATE (`attendance.late`);","-- a status changes from ABSENT to PRESENT (`attendance.corrected`).","+**Decision.**","+- `src/server/storage.ts` uses `node:fs/promises` and `node:stream/promises` only. `UPLOAD_DIR` is resolved with `path.resolve` and defaults to `./uploads`. Every storage key is checked to stay under `UPLOAD_DIR` after resolution, as a path-traversal guard. This is the only implementation (NFR-11).","+- `GET /api/files/:id` runs on `runtime = 'nodejs'` and returns `new Response(Readable.toWeb(fs.createReadStream(...)))` with `Content-Type` set to the stored mime, `Content-Length` set to `sizeBytes`, `nosniff`, and `Content-Disposition` set to `attachment` (or `inline` only for image/* or pdf with `?inline=1`) plus an RFC 5987 `filename*`. SVG responses also set `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'`. If next.config's global CSP is also sent, browsers enforce the stricter intersection, so no config change is needed for this route.","+- Add `'/api/files/'` to `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` so unauthenticated logo fetches reach the handler. The handler then runs the access registry (FR-9) for every request: `auth()` returns `null` for anonymous users, and only the `logo` resolver accepts a null user. Every other context returns 404 without reading bytes (NFR-8).","+- `.gitignore`: create one at the repo root containing `uploads/`, plus `node_modules`, `.next` and `.env` if missing. `.env.example` documents `UPLOAD_DIR` and `MAX_UPLOAD_MB` (NFR-12)."," ","-Re-saving an unchanged register produces no diff and therefore no notifications, which covers the dedupe rule in FR-22. Register all four keys in `NOTIFICATION_TYPES`, using intake's FR-23 defaults for `corrected`/`register_missing`. For daily registers, the body is `<Section> daily register, <date>`, following the clarification default.","+**Consequences.** Opening the middleware gate moves the auth decision into the handler, so the access registry must deny by default (FR-9: an unknown context is owner/ADMIN only, and an anonymous user is denied). Integration tests must cover anonymous requests for a lesson file (expect 404) and for a logo (expect 200)."," ","-**Consequences.** There is no worker or queue infrastructure. Emails go through the existing outbox, so the 5-minute target depends on the existing outbox drain. Save latency grows by about one insert per notified student, which is fine at class size. If the notification loop ever pushes saves past NFR-1, move it to a fire-and-forget call after the response is sent.","-","-Drivers: FR-20, FR-21, FR-22, FR-23, NFR-5.","-"," ---"," ","-## ADR-5: Compute missing registers and \"today\" on demand in the school timezone, with no scheduler","+## ADR-5: Allow the video-embed origins in the site CSP via `frame-src`"," ","-**Context.** FR-31 flags a register as missing if it is still not taken 15 minutes after the period starts, in the school timezone. FR-6, FR-8 and FR-19 depend on \"today\" and on 14-day windows. `Period.startTime` is a string, and `SchoolSettings.timezone` exists. `src/lib/dates.ts` already wraps `formatInTimeZone`.","+**Context.** FR-30 embeds `youtube-nocookie.com/embed/<id>` and `player.vimeo.com/video/<id>` iframes. The current CSP has `default-src 'self'` and no `frame-src`, so the browser would block both embeds."," ","-**Decision.**","-- Compute `schoolToday()` as `formatInTimeZone(now, tz, 'yyyy-MM-dd')`. Every date-only comparison (future-date refusal, 14-day open/edit window, current period) uses this school-local date string compared with `@db.Date`. Never use server-local `new Date()`.","-- `GET /api/admin/attendance/overview` computes the missing list when it is requested. It expands `getScheduleForDate()` for each course meeting today, filters to meetings where `startTime + 15 min < now` in the school timezone, and subtracts the sessions that exist. \"Remind teacher\" is a manual POST.","-- Put the edit-window rule in a pure function, `canEdit(role, registerDate, today)` (FR-39).","+**Decision.** Add `frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://player.vimeo.com;` to the CSP string in `next.config.js`. The URL parser lives in `src/lib/video.ts` as a pure function shared by the Zod validator (FR-16) and the viewer (FR-32). It only emits those two origins."," ","-**Consequences.** No cron and no stored \"missing\" state. The overview always reflects reality. Automatic reminders are out of scope; add a scheduler only if the PRD later asks for automatic sending.","+**Consequences.** This is a one-line config change. The `X-Frame-Options: DENY` header only stops other sites from framing SchoolHub, so it does not affect these embeds. Playwright should check that the iframe loads (it is not CSP-blocked) on the seeded YouTube lesson (FR-47)."," ","-Drivers: FR-6, FR-8, FR-19, FR-30, FR-31, FR-39, NFR-8.","-"," ---"," ","-## ADR-6: Aggregate per student in TypeScript and school-wide with Prisma `groupBy`","+## ADR-6: Render Markdown with react-markdown, remark-gfm and rehype-sanitize, map every element to MUI, and restrict images to same origin"," ","-**Context.** NFR-1 requires the student tab to load in under 400 ms with 1,200 records. FR-25/26/27 need the summary, the calendar and the table from the same data. FR-28 defines the rate formula and the mode-dependent record set. FR-32/33 aggregate across all students for up to 366 days.","+**Context.** FR-31 and NFR-2 require sanitised Markdown with `img` allowed from same origin only and MUI element mapping (NFR-7). The current CSP has `img-src 'self' data:`."," ","-**Decision.**","-- **Student tab**: run one `findMany` over records joined to the session date and period, filtered by date range and by the mode's session kind (`courseId != null` for PER_PERIOD/BOTH, `sectionId != null` for DAILY_HOMEROOM). Pure functions then compute the term and year summaries (`attendanceRate()`), the per-day calendar colours and the table rows. 1,200 narrow rows is a few milliseconds of work.","-- **Report and below-threshold**: use `prisma.attendanceRecord.groupBy({ by: ['studentId','status'], where: { session: { date range, kind } } , _count })` and fold the result into per-student rows in TypeScript. If that turns out slow in practice, fall back to a single `$queryRaw` with `GROUP BY`.","-- Records from DROPPED enrollments are never filtered out (FR-28).","-- `attendanceRate()` lives in one place and is shared by the tab, matrix, report and threshold views (FR-39).","+**Decision.** `src/components/MarkdownView/MarkdownView.tsx` is a client-safe component (no hooks, so it also renders on the server) with `remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]}` and `rehypePlugins={[[rehypeSanitize, schema]]}`. `schema` extends `defaultSchema` and limits `img` `src` to relative paths: `protocols.src: []` plus a check that the value starts with `/`. The default schema already drops `javascript:` hrefs. A `components` map converts h1–h6, p, a, ul/ol/li, table/thead/tbody/tr/th/td, hr, blockquote, code and pre into MUI Typography, Link, List/ListItem, Table*, Divider and `Box component=\"pre\"`. External `a` elements get `target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`. The editor Preview tab (FR-25) uses the same component."," ","-**Consequences.** There are no materialised views or cached counters, and no index beyond FR-1's `[studentId, createdAt]` plus the session indexes. Notes are stripped in the service according to role (FR-27), not in the UI.","+**Consequences.** One renderer is used everywhere, so a sanitisation regression shows up in one unit test with `<script>`, `javascript:` and off-origin `img` cases (FR-48). No CSP change is needed for images."," ","-Drivers: FR-25, FR-26, FR-27, FR-28, FR-29, FR-32, FR-33, FR-39, NFR-1.","-"," ---"," ","-## ADR-7: Keep offline resilience to localStorage drafts, with no service worker or IndexedDB","+## ADR-7: Implement reordering with @dnd-kit/sortable, persist the whole order in one transaction, and provide keyboard IconButtons"," ","-**Context.** NFR-2 requires that unsaved marks survive a network failure and that the next visit offers a restore. The app already uses `window.localStorage` (`src/app/(app)/shell.tsx`).","+**Context.** FR-23 covers drag within and across modules, with the full ordering sent to `POST /api/courses/:id/content/reorder`. NFR-6 requires a keyboard alternative. @dnd-kit/core is already installed."," ","-**Decision.** The register's client component writes its draft to `localStorage` under the key `attendance-draft:<courseId|section:sectionId>:<date>:<periodId|daily>` when a save fails. On mount it shows a \"Restore unsaved attendance\" action if a draft exists, and it clears the draft after a successful save.","+**Decision.** Each module's lesson List is a `SortableContext` with `verticalListSortingStrategy`, all inside one `DndContext`, and cross-container moves are handled in `onDragOver`. Up/down `IconButton`s modify the same client order array. The server validates that every submitted module and lesson id belongs to the course and that the set equals the existing set. It then writes `position`/`moduleId` for all rows inside one `prisma.$transaction`. A course has at most about 200 lessons (NFR-4), so updating every row is acceptable."," ","-**Consequences.** This is not offline-first. The page itself must still load. That is all NFR-2 asks for. Add a service worker only if a full offline mode is requested.","+**Consequences.** Reorder is a single idempotent call with no partial states. The update count is O(n) per reorder, which is fine at this scale."," ","-Drivers: NFR-2, NFR-4.","-"," ---"," ","-## ADR-8: Reuse the existing auth, settings and config extension points","+## ADR-8: Read content with a bounded number of Prisma queries and compute visibility in TypeScript"," ","-**Context.** NFR-7 requires server-side access control everywhere. FR-5 needs an admin editor for settings, but there is no settings page and `src/app/api/settings/school/` is empty. FR-24, FR-29 and FR-34 extend the tab and nav configuration.","+**Context.** NFR-4 requires the Content tab to render 20×10 lessons in under 600 ms with no N+1 queries. FR-18 evaluates visibility at read time, with no background job."," ","-**Decision.**","-- **Access.** Use `requireUser`/`requireRole`, `assertCanManageCourse`/`assertCanViewCourse` and `assertCanViewStudent`/`getAccessibleStudentIds` as they are. The one new check, homeroom access (`section.homeroomTeacherId === user.id || ADMIN`) for FR-15, is a short function in the attendance service. EXCUSED is rejected server-side for anyone who is not an admin (FR-10).","-- **Settings (FR-5).** Use the smallest option. Extend `UpdateSchoolSettingsInput` with the two new fields and add `PATCH /api/settings/school`, which is ADMIN only, Zod-validated (threshold 0–100) and calls `audit()`. The editor is an \"Attendance settings\" card on `/admin/attendance`. A general settings page is out of scope.","-- **Tabs and nav.** Add entries to `student-tabs.ts`, `course-tabs.ts` and `navigation.ts`. Do not move or rename existing entries.","-- **Daily register route (FR-16).** Use `/attendance/take/section/[sectionId]?date=`, rendering the same register client component as the course route.","+**Decision.** `GET /api/courses/:id/modules` and the Content tab server component call `content-service.listCourseContent(user, courseId)`. That function makes one `module.findMany` with `include: { lessons: { include: { _count: { select: { attachments: true } } } } }` ordered by position, plus one `lessonProgress.findMany` for the student's lessons in that course when the caller is a STUDENT. That is two queries in total. `isVisible(entity, now)` is a pure function in `src/server/content/visibility.ts` and is unit tested (FR-48). Both module and lesson must be visible. Add an `@@index([courseId, position])` on Module and an `@@index([moduleId, position])` on Lesson."," ","-**Consequences.** No new auth abstraction is needed. The FR-5 and FR-16 defaults are open for the user to override (see blockers in the intake handoff).","+**Consequences.** Visibility is not pushed into SQL. At 200 rows per course, filtering in memory costs nothing, and having the rule in one place means the viewer (FR-32), the Learning tab (FR-35) and FR-44 all agree."," ","-Drivers: FR-5, FR-10, FR-15, FR-16, FR-24, FR-29, FR-34, FR-35, NFR-7, NFR-10.","-"," ---"," ","-## ADR-9: Test with the existing Vitest and Playwright setups","+## ADR-9: Use existing stack pieces for scheduling, timezones, autosave and notifications"," ","-**Context.** FR-39 to FR-41 define the tests. Vitest 2.1.1 and Playwright 1.47.2 are installed.","+**Context.** FR-28, NFR-10, FR-29, FR-38/39 and FR-12 all match existing infrastructure."," "," **Decision.**","-- **Unit (Vitest).** Cover the pure functions `attendanceRate`, `canEdit` and `diffRecords`/`notificationsFor`.","-- **Integration (Vitest against a migrated test database).** Cover `saveRegister` atomicity, NFR-3 roster rejection, and violations of the CHECK constraint and both partial indexes. The database must be created through migrations, per ADR-2.","-- **End-to-end (Playwright).** Run the take-attendance flow at a 375×667 viewport, and a parent's notification plus the calendar absence.","-- **Seed (FR-38).** Use a small seeded PRNG (mulberry32, a few lines) in the seed script rather than adding a dependency.","+- Schedule picker: use the `DateTimePicker` from `@mui/x-date-pickers` (already wrapped in `src/components/FormField`). Convert school-timezone input to UTC with `date-fns-tz` `fromZonedTime(SchoolSettings.timezone)`. Zod rejects `publishAt <= now` (FR-28, NFR-10).","+- Autosave (FR-29): a `setInterval` of 10 s plus a dirty flag in the editor, a `beforeunload` listener, and `PATCH /api/lessons/:id`. No new library.","+- Notifications: add `content.lesson_published` to `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` and call `notify()` only from the publish-now path (FR-38, FR-39).","+- Orphan purge (FR-12): call `purgeOrphanFiles()` next to `purgeOldNotifications()` inside the existing `lastPurgeAt` daily block in `src/app/api/internal/email/dispatch/route.ts`.","+- Logging (NFR-12): use `logger` from `src/lib/logger.ts`. Rate limit (NFR-3): use the existing `rateLimit()`, which is per-process. That limitation is already marked with a ponytail comment."," ","-**Consequences.** No new test tooling is added.","+**Consequences.** No scheduler, queue or cron is added. Scheduled lessons appear automatically when read after `publishAt`, and no notification is sent for them, as FR-39 requires."," ","-Drivers: FR-38, FR-39, FR-40, FR-41, NFR-10.","+---","+","+## Open risks carried forward","+1. **NFR-7:** `mui-mcp` tools (`useMuiDocs`/`fetchDocs`) were visible in this session's tool list. Dev must still look up every MUI component against 9.5.0 before using it. If the server is unreachable, that is a hard blocker.","+2. **ADR-2:** depending on Next's transitive busboy is a small fragility (see the Consequences of ADR-2).","+3. **FR-40/42 (LOW confidence):** logo URLs stored as `/api/files/<id>` need an `APP_URL` prefix in email templates. The new `/admin/settings/school` page is a minimal page built on the existing `PATCH /api/settings/school`."]}],"originalFile":"# Tech Stack Decisions: PRD-07 Attendance Tracking (SCH-007)\n\nTicket: SH-7 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-06T06:00:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and `package.json` confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL 16, Auth.js (next-auth 5.0.0-beta.25), Zod 3.23.8, and MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons-material, material-nextjs, x-data-grid, x-date-pickers, x-charts). Also installed: date-fns 3.6.0, date-fns-tz 3.2.0, papaparse, Vitest 2.1.1 and Playwright 1.47.2. Nothing in the requirements needs a different stack, so the decisions below cover how this feature uses the stack that is already there.\n\nThis file replaces the stale SH-1 (PRD-01) version that was in this folder.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack and add no new dependencies\n\n**Context.** The feature needs a register UI with exclusive toggles (FR-9), a calendar with a custom day slot (FR-26), a scrollable matrix (FR-29), date pickers that block future dates (FR-8), CSV export (FR-32), timezone-aware \"today\" logic (FR-31, NFR-8), and shared validation (FR-36). NFR-6 allows MUI only, and NFR-10 asks for brownfield safety.\n\n**Decision.** Use only installed packages:\n- UI: `@mui/material` `ToggleButtonGroup`, `Paper` (sticky save bar), `Tooltip` and `Badge`; `@mui/x-date-pickers` `DatePicker` (`disableFuture`, `minDate`) and `DateCalendar` with a custom `day` slot; `@mui/x-data-grid` `DataGrid` for the course matrix and the admin report.\n- Validation: `zod`, in `src/lib/validators/attendance.ts`.\n- Dates: `date-fns` and `date-fns-tz`, through the existing `src/lib/dates.ts`.\n- CSV: the existing `toCsv()`/`escapeCsvCell()` in `src/server/csv.ts`. `papaparse` is not needed for export.\n\nBefore writing any component, Dev must look it up through the mui-mcp server (`useMuiDocs`/`fetchDocs` for 9.5.0). The server was reachable in this session.\n\n**Consequences.** There are no lockfile changes and no banned imports for QA to catch. The free (MIT) `DataGrid` has no column pinning and caps pages at 100 rows. That is acceptable: the matrix scrolls horizontally (FR-29) and pagination is capped at 100 anyway (NFR-8). Do not use `DataGridPro` features.\n\nDrivers: FR-8, FR-9, FR-26, FR-29, FR-32, FR-36, NFR-6, NFR-8, NFR-10.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-2: Use a Prisma schema plus hand-written SQL in one migration for constraints Prisma can't express\n\n**Context.** FR-1 specifies the `AttendanceStatus` enum and the `AttendanceSession`/`AttendanceRecord` models. FR-2 needs \"exactly one of courseId/sectionId\". FR-3 needs two partial unique indexes. Prisma 5.20 cannot express CHECK constraints or partial (`WHERE`) indexes. FR-4 adds two `SchoolSettings` columns. The latest migration is `20261106000000_user_role_status_index`.\n\n**Decision.** Create one new migration, `20261107000000_attendance` or any timestamp later than the latest one. Generate it with `prisma migrate dev --create-only`, then append the following raw SQL by hand:\n- `CHECK (((\"courseId\" IS NOT NULL)::int + (\"sectionId\" IS NOT NULL)::int) = 1)`\n- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON \"AttendanceSession\"(\"courseId\",\"date\",\"periodId\") WHERE \"courseId\" IS NOT NULL`\n- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON \"AttendanceSession\"(\"sectionId\",\"date\") WHERE \"sectionId\" IS NOT NULL`\n\n`SchoolSettings` gains an `AttendanceMode` enum column `attendanceMode` (default `PER_PERIOD`) and `attendanceAlertThreshold Int @default(90)`. `date` is stored as `@db.Date`.\n\n**Consequences.** `prisma db push` and any schema drift check will not see these SQL objects, so tests must run `prisma migrate reset`/`deploy` and must not use `db push`. The service also checks the one-of rule (FR-2), so callers get a clean `VALIDATION_ERROR` before the database rejects the row. Prisma cannot `upsert` on a partial index (see ADR-3).\n\nDrivers: FR-1, FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-40, NFR-8.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-3: Save a register in one interactive transaction that only writes changed rows\n\n**Context.** FR-14 requires an atomic create-or-update of the session and all its records. NFR-1 requires a 35-student save to finish in under 500 ms. FR-18 requires an audit entry for each changed status, and FR-21/22 need the previous status to detect changes. A partial unique index cannot be used as an upsert target.\n\n**Decision.** `saveRegister()` in `src/server/attendance/attendance-service.ts` uses `prisma.$transaction(async tx => …)` and runs these steps:\n1. `findFirst` the session by (courseId, date, periodId) or (sectionId, date). Create it if it does not exist. If the create fails with `P2002`, a concurrent first save won the race: re-read the session and continue.\n2. Load the existing records for the session in a single query to get the previous statuses.\n3. Insert new students with `createMany`. Update only the rows whose status, minutesLate or note actually changed; `@@unique([sessionId, studentId])` makes these targeted updates.\n4. Validate the roster (NFR-3) against ACTIVE enrollments on that date, or section members for a daily register, in one query before writing.\n5. Return the diff `{studentId, prev, next}[]`. Write `audit()` entries for changed records inside the transaction, or right after commit if `audit()` cannot take `tx`. Dev should check `src/server/audit.ts`.\n\n**Consequences.** About 5 to 40 statements run per save, which fits easily in 500 ms. The diff is computed by a pure function (`diffRecords(prev, next)`) that unit tests can cover (FR-39). No raw `INSERT … ON CONFLICT` is needed.\n\nDrivers: FR-14, FR-18, FR-21, FR-22, FR-39, FR-40, NFR-1, NFR-3.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-4: Send notifications synchronously after commit through the existing notify pipeline, with no new job queue\n\n**Context.** FR-20 to FR-23 add four notification types. NFR-5 requires delivery within 5 minutes, with in-app notifications immediate and email through `EmailOutbox`. `notifyStudentAndParents()`/`notify()` already exist and never throw. There is no job runner or cron in `src/server`.\n\n**Decision.** After the transaction commits, loop over the diff and call `notifyStudentAndParents()` when:\n- a status changes from something other than ABSENT to ABSENT (`attendance.absent`);\n- a status changes from something other than LATE to LATE (`attendance.late`);\n- a status changes from ABSENT to PRESENT (`attendance.corrected`).\n\nRe-saving an unchanged register produces no diff and therefore no notifications, which covers the dedupe rule in FR-22. Register all four keys in `NOTIFICATION_TYPES`, using intake's FR-23 defaults for `corrected`/`register_missing`. For daily registers, the body is `<Section> daily register, <date>`, following the clarification default.\n\n**Consequences.** There is no worker or queue infrastructure. Emails go through the existing outbox, so the 5-minute target depends on the existing outbox drain. Save latency grows by about one insert per notified student, which is fine at class size. If the notification loop ever pushes saves past NFR-1, move it to a fire-and-forget call after the response is sent.\n\nDrivers: FR-20, FR-21, FR-22, FR-23, NFR-5.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-5: Compute missing registers and \"today\" on demand in the school timezone, with no scheduler\n\n**Context.** FR-31 flags a register as missing if it is still not taken 15 minutes after the period starts, in the school timezone. FR-6, FR-8 and FR-19 depend on \"today\" and on 14-day windows. `Period.startTime` is a string, and `SchoolSettings.timezone` exists. `src/lib/dates.ts` already wraps `formatInTimeZone`.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Compute `schoolToday()` as `formatInTimeZone(now, tz, 'yyyy-MM-dd')`. Every date-only comparison (future-date refusal, 14-day open/edit window, current period) uses this school-local date string compared with `@db.Date`. Never use server-local `new Date()`.\n- `GET /api/admin/attendance/overview` computes the missing list when it is requested. It expands `getScheduleForDate()` for each course meeting today, filters to meetings where `startTime + 15 min < now` in the school timezone, and subtracts the sessions that exist. \"Remind teacher\" is a manual POST.\n- Put the edit-window rule in a pure function, `canEdit(role, registerDate, today)` (FR-39).\n\n**Consequences.** No cron and no stored \"missing\" state. The overview always reflects reality. Automatic reminders are out of scope; add a scheduler only if the PRD later asks for automatic sending.\n\nDrivers: FR-6, FR-8, FR-19, FR-30, FR-31, FR-39, NFR-8.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-6: Aggregate per student in TypeScript and school-wide with Prisma `groupBy`\n\n**Context.** NFR-1 requires the student tab to load in under 400 ms with 1,200 records. FR-25/26/27 need the summary, the calendar and the table from the same data. FR-28 defines the rate formula and the mode-dependent record set. FR-32/33 aggregate across all students for up to 366 days.\n\n**Decision.**\n- **Student tab**: run one `findMany` over records joined to the session date and period, filtered by date range and by the mode's session kind (`courseId != null` for PER_PERIOD/BOTH, `sectionId != null` for DAILY_HOMEROOM). Pure functions then compute the term and year summaries (`attendanceRate()`), the per-day calendar colours and the table rows. 1,200 narrow rows is a few milliseconds of work.\n- **Report and below-threshold**: use `prisma.attendanceRecord.groupBy({ by: ['studentId','status'], where: { session: { date range, kind } } , _count })` and fold the result into per-student rows in TypeScript. If that turns out slow in practice, fall back to a single `$queryRaw` with `GROUP BY`.\n- Records from DROPPED enrollments are never filtered out (FR-28).\n- `attendanceRate()` lives in one place and is shared by the tab, matrix, report and threshold views (FR-39).\n\n**Consequences.** There are no materialised views or cached counters, and no index beyond FR-1's `[studentId, createdAt]` plus the session indexes. Notes are stripped in the service according to role (FR-27), not in the UI.\n\nDrivers: FR-25, FR-26, FR-27, FR-28, FR-29, FR-32, FR-33, FR-39, NFR-1.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-7: Keep offline resilience to localStorage drafts, with no service worker or IndexedDB\n\n**Context.** NFR-2 requires that unsaved marks survive a network failure and that the next visit offers a restore. The app already uses `window.localStorage` (`src/app/(app)/shell.tsx`).\n\n**Decision.** The register's client component writes its draft to `localStorage` under the key `attendance-draft:<courseId|section:sectionId>:<date>:<periodId|daily>` when a save fails. On mount it shows a \"Restore unsaved attendance\" action if a draft exists, and it clears the draft after a successful save.\n\n**Consequences.** This is not offline-first. The page itself must still load. That is all NFR-2 asks for. Add a service worker only if a full offline mode is requested.\n\nDrivers: NFR-2, NFR-4.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-8: Reuse the existing auth, settings and config extension points\n\n**Context.** NFR-7 requires server-side access control everywhere. FR-5 needs an admin editor for settings, but there is no settings page and `src/app/api/settings/school/` is empty. FR-24, FR-29 and FR-34 extend the tab and nav configuration.\n\n**Decision.**\n- **Access.** Use `requireUser`/`requireRole`, `assertCanManageCourse`/`assertCanViewCourse` and `assertCanViewStudent`/`getAccessibleStudentIds` as they are. The one new check, homeroom access (`section.homeroomTeacherId === user.id || ADMIN`) for FR-15, is a short function in the attendance service. EXCUSED is rejected server-side for anyone who is not an admin (FR-10).\n- **Settings (FR-5).** Use the smallest option. Extend `UpdateSchoolSettingsInput` with the two new fields and add `PATCH /api/settings/school`, which is ADMIN only, Zod-validated (threshold 0–100) and calls `audit()`. The editor is an \"Attendance settings\" card on `/admin/attendance`. A general settings page is out of scope.\n- **Tabs and nav.** Add entries to `student-tabs.ts`, `course-tabs.ts` and `navigation.ts`. Do not move or rename existing entries.\n- **Daily register route (FR-16).** Use `/attendance/take/section/[sectionId]?date=`, rendering the same register client component as the course route.\n\n**Consequences.** No new auth abstraction is needed. The FR-5 and FR-16 defaults are open for the user to override (see blockers in the intake handoff).\n\nDrivers: FR-5, FR-10, FR-15, FR-16, FR-24, FR-29, FR-34, FR-35, NFR-7, NFR-10.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-9: Test with the existing Vitest and Playwright setups\n\n**Context.** FR-39 to FR-41 define the tests. Vitest 2.1.1 and Playwright 1.47.2 are installed.\n\n**Decision.**\n- **Unit (Vitest).** Cover the pure functions `attendanceRate`, `canEdit` and `diffRecords`/`notificationsFor`.\n- **Integration (Vitest against a migrated test database).** Cover `saveRegister` atomicity, NFR-3 roster rejection, and violations of the CHECK constraint and both partial indexes. The database must be created through migrations, per ADR-2.\n- **End-to-end (Playwright).** Run the take-attendance flow at a 375×667 viewport, and a parent's notification plus the calendar absence.\n- **Seed (FR-38).** Use a small seeded PRNG (mulberry32, a few lines) in the seed script rather than adding a dependency.\n\n**Consequences.** No new test tooling is added.\n\nDrivers: FR-38, FR-39, FR-40, FR-41, NFR-10.\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01C4MM1QDpYnQNZ1tnbtUL5J","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
